      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>POULTRY PEN.</head>
        <p>POULTRY PEN.</p>
        <p>HINTS AND INTERESTING FACTS AND CHAT FOR AMATEURS.</p>
        <p>abar DOG BISCUITS 8 POULTQY FOODS</p>
        <p>,ARE THE BEST.</p>
        <p>AGENTS:</p>
        <p>CARTER &amp; SON Ltd.</p>
        <p>BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>TOO MANY EGGS.</p>
        <p>BACKYARDER HIT.</p>
        <p>THE WEEK'S WORK IN BRIEF. Give the young stock a frequent change to new ground. Move the hen coops a little -each day. Do not allow the chicks to run on solid floors; sprinkle the latter with litter. • Little chickens require an ample supply of green.stuff if not. on free range. Do not leave the hen too long with her chicks; get her on the lay again. Well feed the non-sitting breeding stock. Push the earlier hatched chickens along.— "The Poultry Wtorld."</p>
        <p>It was never intended that fowls should lay 300 eggs.ia the course of a. year; but still, lines of breeding-haVe beeri'alployed that have made this result almost possible, says a writer in "The Feathered World." Nature said, how- ever, that two clutches, or about thirty eggs; would be an ample provision to cover all even- tualities, and there is obviously a vast difference between 30 and 300. By proper feeding the abundance of eggs may be provided for effectually, but the question of rendering each egg reproductive is quite another matter. As I have frequently pointed out, each time an egg is laid it is an effort at reproduction, and in that direction it is a drain upon the female as well as the male. The unnatural strain upon the generative organs cannot be provided for by any course of feeding—not to-any material degree—and Dame Nature's provision for thirty eggs being spread over 280 eggs just restilts in constitutionally fragile produce.. To gain these exceptionally high records, and to gain their object as quickly as possible, breeders have resorted to indiscriminate in- breeding, which has brought them to their present difficult position. Pullets have been employed for stock purposes, and used with the object of increasing the rate of fecundity, dur- ing their first season, when they are at, their highest degree' of productiveness, and this is, I think, clearly responsible / for the present universal lack of stamina in the high-fecund strains of White Leghorns and White Wyan- clottes, a conclusion based upon the line of reasoning I have set out, and not on account of any existing inherent disease, as has been supposed.</p>
        <p>there is dirt clinging to the shells it affects the flavour. Generally speaking, infertile eggs 'pre- serve in better condition than do those contain- ing a germ. The most satisfactory method of preserving eggs is either by means of waterglass or P.me, salt and water. The latter method retains the flavour and quality• of the contents in as good condition as does the waterglass, but it possesses the drawbaek of rendering the shells rough and extremely brittle. Whether the waterglass or lime method is used, the treat- ment of the eggs is similar. They are placed in a glazed vessel, and the liquid is poured over them. Waterglass is comparatively inexpen- sive, and can be purchase,d at almost all chemists. Full directions are sent with each sample regarding its strength and the amount of water with which it must be diluted. For the other method, twenty gallons of water, four gallons of lime, and one gallon of salt are mixed together and allowed to settle, when the clear liquid is poured off. The sediment is thrown away, for it is useless.</p>
        <p>FREEDOM IN -SPRING.</p>
        <p>Now that warmer weather and fine'days have arrived, the fowls should be given as much freedom as possible, for such a plan is not only conducive to health, but the economy in feeding is very great indeed. I know that space on some farms is somewhat cramped owing to some of the meadows being shut up for hay, but the fields which are being fed should be heavily stocked with fowls. It is quite a mis- take to suppose that other stock object to the presence of fowls. The advantages of housing on the colony plan are many. The birds thrive better when at liberty than when confined in runs, and the land derives great benefit from the manure, an adult fowl producing about 100 lb. of highly concentrated dung in the course of a twelvemonth; the fowls can be maintaiaed at a considerably smaller expense, and the houses cost less than permanent ones with runs attached. The land is especially rich at the present time, ip animal and vegetable food, and it is possible to keep the stock in good con- dition on two small or one large meal a day.— E. T. Brown, in "Farm and Home."</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <p>This war will mean a lot to all fanciers; but it will hit the little backyarder in more ways than one. Still, what a grand opportunity is being offered to all little fanciers, for now is the time to obtain a, really first-class sitting of eggs at a modest. sum, and not only in one variety but in all varieties. It is to be hoped that backyarders* will take advantage of the offers, and with a good hatch, and the chicks fed orrthe right stuff, they should be well in when the show day comes round. The backyarder can do one brood well and can get the size and colour, and shade from sun, &amp;c. I have seen some grand birds hatched in the backyard, and on the backyard system. The small fancier and workingnian has to be very careful when he speculates, and when these expensive eggs arrive they are generally treated with great care, and the very best is bought to rear the chicks, and the reward is justly earned.—Fred Martin, in "The Feathered World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>HINTS FOR SPRING.</head>
        <p>HINTS FOR SPRING.</p>
        <p>Those poultry-lieepers whose fowls have not been laying well this winter (and there are many) should ask themselves the question if the fault has been their own through wrong feed- ing, bad housing, or the laying qualities of the fowls themselves. As the weather has been une seasonable, the feeding, &amp;c., should have been such as to counteract its effects.</p>
        <p>Sometimes it is wise to feed unnaturally. The fowls (good layers) lay many times their own total weight in twelve months, so that it will be seen the true value of food is not only to keep them alive and healthy, and to replace the energy caused by laying wastage, but to provide the hens or pullets with the necessary foods or ingredients to make or develop those eggs. The greatest aid to egg production is nitro- genous food—or animal food, as it is called— which the fowls aro (if at liberty) able to pro- vide for themselves in the form of insects, slugs, snails and worms. Instinct tells the wild birds the same foods are an aid to them in assisting them to lay their clutch or nest of eggs. _ _</p>
        <p>Watch them in the spring as they search the hedgerows and ditches for this class of food. So, pr,oportionately, fowls require it or a substitute all the time they are expected to lay, and when the season does not admit of its provision the owner should fill the deficiency. 'Meat scraps' are too dear for poultry, but nothing from the table need ,he wasted, as it can be included in the hot mash for the fowls'!.`breakfast. • .</p>
        <p>• As regard-animal food, a very good substitute is boiled lights, which must be chopped up fine, and the water used is serviceable for the mixing of soft food. Of course, fowls kept in small open spaces or covered runs have to have all animal food brought or given them, and Inz. per day of the former, and as much of the latter as they will .eat, providing it is given regularly. They never eat too much of it unless it is given at unequal times and in uneven quantities. The finest egg-forcer or developer is now admitted to be cut green bone—i.e., raw fresh bones with the meat scraps adhering from the butchers. They are exceptionally cheap, about hi. per lb. Some local butchers to meet the demand sell them already crushed. The hobbyist on a large scale could wisely invest in a bone crusher or smasher, the cost would be repaid in one winter by the increased output of eggs. There is no real substitute for green food un- less it is a by-product; Such as bran, which should be well scalded before use. Root crops can be given, such as man,gels or swedes, which should be cut in two and put on pieces of wood or iron and fastened to the ground so that the contents (the middle) can be pecked out and the shells left to be boiled and smashed. No food of any kind should be put on the earth floor except hard food (grain), and this is best buried. Some foods get foul and tainted by being trodden underfoot. It is wise to always remove any food left uneaten.—Xlidlander, in " The Feathered World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>PRESERVING EGGS.</head>
        <p>PRESERVING EGGS.</p>
        <p>Under n'orrnal conditions eggs are so cheap during May that it is a good plan to preserve as many as possible for use the following autumn and winter. This year, things are different, however, and it is quite possible that prices will remain sufficiently high to make it more profitable to sell them as new-laid. Should prices drop, however, as is always possible, pre- serving should be adopted. It is no use attempt- ing to preserve any save quite fresh eggs, while those more than two days old should not be treated. The eggs, too, should be clean, for if</p>
        <p>EGGS for Sitting.—Rhode Island Reds, S.C. White Wyandottes, White Leghorns, Black Minorcas; good laying strains:- 2s. 6d. for 13. Daniel Snape, Slack Farm, Skelmersdale.</p>
        <p>EGGS.—Blaek Minoreas from my prize win- ners; White Wyandottes, Cock Ist and special only time shown; Hens, Barron's strain; 3s. 6d. dozen, clear replaced once, or 14 unreplaced.— Renshaw, Bircle Vicarage, Bury.</p>
        <p>- _ - VNGIiAND'S Premier laying strains, 28,3 .124 White Wyandottes, 282 White Leghorns, Toni Barron's best, mated with view to producing com- petition laying winners, unlimited range; Eggs 45., selected 65., special 7s. 6d. dozen; unfertiles re- placed.—Reverend Hewetson, Beeley Vicarage, Rowsley.</p>
        <p>- LEGHORN'S, White, Brown, Black; finest lay- ing strains in England; 3s. 12, ss. 6d. 24, 10s. 6d. 60, 20s. 100: prompt dispatch.—John Livesey, Blackburn Small Holdings, Blackburn. PADMAN'S 285-egg strain White Leghorns; eggs, .3s. dozen; 2 dozen, ss. 6d.; Chicks, 7s. dozen.-.-Bromfield, Matlock-road Farm, Coventry.</p>
        <p>STRONG CHICKS, cross-bred, for laying and table; day to week old, 6s. dozen. Orders ta.ken for• month old, fis., carriage paid.---Atkins, Windy- gates, Frodsham. ITCCESSFUL—CHICKEN--REARING—diI pends in a great measure upon careful atten- tion to detail, as clean drinking pots and fresh water at least twice daily, clean litter iri coops, a con- stant supply of small grit and shell, and newly- limewashed coops and brooders before and after each hatch. Good sound wholesome food is also of equal importance. ENTWISTLE BROS.' DRY CHICK FOOD has long been recognised as the sweetest, by far the most nutritious, and evenly balanced ration offered to Poultry Keepers, and the one most suitable for young Chickens. It is sold in 311 b. and 71b. bags at Bd. and is. 4d. Ask your Grocer or Corn Dealer for Entwistle's, and see that you get it. Agents:—CARTER &amp; SON, LTD., Bridge-street, Warrington; BARLOW &amp; SONS, Winwick-street, Warrington; and T. GREGORY, Bridge-street. Earlestown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
