      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>ASPIDISTRA FLOWERING.</head>
        <p>ASPIDISTRA FLOWERING.</p>
        <p>We are often asked wiiether the flowering of this has an injurious effet o'n the plant. The flowering will not do any harm, as we have never heard of a case where seeds have been produced. We have at the present time several large plants blooming freely. It is a common thing for old. plants 'which have filled the pots with roots to flower, but the plants look none the worse for it—in fad, we have noticed that those plants that .flower freely always develop plenty of new leaves. The flowers, which rest on the surface of the soil, are very interesting when closely examined.</p>
        <p>D , the place we are now in has not been shelled for mouths by the Huns, but last Mon- day they chitve us a taste and placed about a "dozen 8-inch shells amongst us. One hit the roof of one of _our billets used by 150 of our men. It wrecked the place. Fortunately, the weather was glorious, and most of the men were basking in the sun outside, only a few being inside. We had had warning by one or two sighting shells, and the place had emptied out, but one or two men were hit by fragments. Killed! What Is The Town Doing?</p>
        <p>I hear Kenwright has since died as the-result of the wounds; .a very deoent little chap; un- fortunately married and leaves four or five little kiddies behind. It is to be hoped War- rington is doing its part in looking after our men's families. Our "Terriers have made immense sacrifices, and a little calculation would show what they have thrown up in earnings; comparatively with the figures of the subscrip- tion lists there is no proportion. Handsome as the Warrington figures read, ,would they repre- sent one hour's income or earnings of the War- rington magnates and people? Cheerful as the men are here there is always the thought in the background how those they have left behind are faring—the greatest _pinch seems to be with</p>
        <p>those. who have- worked- for small employers.' The Corporate concerns' have attempted some- thing on a system, though there seems to be the feeling that some of these are not continuing as they started.</p>
        <p>" You should have seen the detritus after the shelling. The men's kits and rifles, coats rent to shreds,. rifles in splinters, and most things unrecognisable. It tickled me a bit to see how philosophically the men treated it all. One man whom I asked why he was short of a _cer- tain article answered, "Oh, I lost that in. the accident."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATURDAY.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>STORIES FROM TliE TRENCHES</p>
        <p>TERRITORIALS SHELLED.</p>
        <p>Avarring ion Officer. And The Recent</p>
        <p>Fighting.</p>
        <p>lIELP FOR DEPENDENTS.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Territorial officer writes : Monday Morning, April 26th, 1915. Possibly you have felt a little anxious not hearing from me for some little time, espe- cially since you may have read about the great activities in this district. For the last week a ceaseless bombardment has continued through the days and nightF. We hear of attack and counter-attack with varying success to both sides. Last night saw the end of another six days': spell in the trenches, and we are back again once more in billets, unfortunately minus an- other officer in Lieutenant Hatton, who is badly wounded in the upper part of the leg. It is said the wound is not of a serious char- acter.</p>
        <p>On. the 17th a brother officer and I rode into the nearest untouched town of any note, a. short distance from our quarters. Since leav- ing had of a peaceful civilisation. You have no idea how nice it was to be in a town which in appearance was quite normal, except for the presence of troops. British and French troops .Were there in plenty, fraternising to- gether; nevertheless the gloom of the war- cloud did not appear to hang over the town as it does here. _ _</p>
        <p>The ride thore 'was interesting (the place is out of bounds and •special permits from the brigade had to be obtained to pass through the lines). All the .way the country swarmed with troops, French and English, represented in all branches of the service; anti-aircraft sections, armoured motor cars manned by Royal 31-Urine Artillery, &amp;c. The Belgian troops practically the whole_way could be seen mending and re-Making the roads which were cut up so badly during the bad weather by the extraordinary traffic passing over them. Our visit to - was short, though plea- sant, as all ranks had to be in billet by seven p.m. Why? We did not know, but when this time arrived we soon realised that big things were afoot. . _</p>
        <p>On the stroke of the hour every gun started to pour forth its hail of shells; the inhabi- tants became very alarmed and thought the Germans were coming through. " L'Attaque, Monsieur" the women frequently •asked in frightened tones, but were quite reassured when told At was an attack on our part. With- in half an hour we heard that trenches had been taken. Ever since this moment the firing has never ceased, and as I write the noise is deafening and everything is on the quiver.</p>
        <p>Hosts of men Wanted.</p>
        <p>Last Monday we commenced our turn in the trenches. The strain this trip beats all our past experiences .as it was quite on the cards that the Germans would take the offensive against our part of the line to distract our attention from the left, whilst they delivered the counter attacks with increasing forces. North of the Ypres the Germans took several trenches from the French, but the Canadians appear to have' re-taken the lost ground and put things right again. We keep reading in the papers that we have got the upper hand. of the German; out here this opinion is qualified. In trench work, mining operations and the use of high explosives from the trenches, they, in a sense, excel ours; of course, this has little bear-- ing on the tactical situation, 'but we realise the Germans are well equipped for war, and until' one. side or the other takes a.complete offensive nothing 6n .be definite, and it-is up to us to .show we can overcome all the skill and pre- paration of the Germans. We feel we can and shall do it, but only by downright hard work and doggedness . will the result be brought about. TO crush the Germans,- hosts of men and only good men will have to 'be found before it can be done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>Trench Repairing.</head>
        <p>Trench Repairing.</p>
        <p>As I said, the time in the trenches had been an anxious one. We worked continuously day and night. You see the sandbag ramparts must be continuously strengthened. They are a necessity to protect us from shell fire, as a shell bursting behind the trench throws 'up all sorts of horrid shaped fragments into a trench where there is no rear protection. -</p>
        <p>I spent the whole period with. , the first three daYs in the fire trenches; second three .in support. We have now all our trenches in com- munication, and it is comparatively easy to move along them now in the daytime. We have been on this work ever since coming- here, and have only just completed. Whilst in the supports we made some palatial shell-proof dug-outs, and I think my own pro- fessional skill served a good purpose. By the way, this is where the Territorial, generally speaking, has a great pull over the regular soldier. Hs, as a rule, has a knowledge of some trade or other which, when applied to the neces- sary work in progress, stands us all in good service.</p>
        <p>The Germans shelled one of our trenches furiously from six to eight on Friday morning, last, and reduced it to a heap of ruins; for- tunately, it was -a trench only occupied at nights. Forty of our men, under Major Cros- field, had to set-to that night—and make good the damage, and they made a good job of it, but next day it was again shelled, but this time little damage was done. Our casualties have not been so heavy this time. I think only one man killeda man of a fatigue party entering• into the trenches. Three machine gunners were,hit, two with hell fragments and one with a bullet. Two are in hospital, the other back to duty, and this last man was particularly lucky. The shell frag- ment tore away his coat collar, and ripped his scarf to bits. He was not scratched; only bruised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>COAT SHOT OFF.</head>
        <p>COAT SHOT OFF.</p>
        <p>Some Very Narrow Escapes.</p>
        <p>Private Chas. Taylor, 1, Kendrick-street, writing home ,'says:— ' "Walker got hit with a piece of shell. On Friday the dirty .Germans shelled lig. Walker and his mate were on the guard with the gun in another trench, when one of the shells burst- ing, a. piece caught Walker on the leg, only slight. His mate must thank God he is not a dead man. A big piece of shell whizzed past him. He had his overcoat on. It took one side of his coat off and tore his scarf in thee places. It never even touched his skin."</p>
        <p>Writing to us from "Somewhere in France," Private A. J. Hoyle, who is serving with the Motor Transport 'Section of the Army Service Corps, and is attached to the No. 4 Field Ambulance, says:— " We do not see much of the newspapers over here, but from-what I have seen of them 1 learn that there have been some strikes in different parts of. the country—strikes which have endangered the supplies .of ammunition, food and other war. supplies. I am no lover of the capitalist. He can take full care of him- self, but I do say. that for men to strike just 110 W is to, deliberately endanger the lives of the. soldiers, for that is what failure to keep up supplies amounts to. I am sure that if only the workmen left in the old country could see the I ruin and destruction that has,, ,been wrought in this country by the Germ- buns the destruction of property and the murdering of helpless women and babes— they would never dream of striking until this horrible business is finished. Rather would every one of them say ti; liiself, 'lt's not much I may be able to do, but What bit I can do 1 will do, and I will do it with a good will, know- ing that thereby I am helping .my chums at the Front.'"</p>
        <p>Prior ,to going on active' service. Private Hoyle acted as one of the secretaries for the' Orford Ward .Committee of the Warrington Liberal Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>SIIELLED TO PIECES.</head>
        <p>SIIELLED TO PIECES.</p>
        <p>Warrinu lon "Terrier "-Shoemaker's</p>
        <p>Lucky Escape.</p>
        <p>Private Frank Shaw, of the Territorials, 10, I Norris-street, shoemaker and assistant postman when at home, writing to his father, Mr. J. Shaw, April 20th, says : I am one of the lucky ones, with other shoe- makers. Our workshop has been shelled to pieces, but we were able to run out just in time, and then the Germans were shelling all over the place. We had a miraculous escape, and I believe it was through God's Providence. Tom was all right in his billet. They all went in the trenches last night for six days, so he asked me to tell you that he won't be able to send any letter till he comes out; but he is doing nicely. "It is surprising how our nerves are equal to standing the awful times we have gone through. I am sorry to hear of ,Ben Homer being wounded, but I only heard of it when I sent my last letter. I am not with the company, so don't hear so much, or I would have written to Mrs. Homer. Kindly give her my sympathy." Private Shaw has been in the trenches, but is now apparently looking to the comfort of his comrades in the matter of footwear. '</p>
        <p>"LAST A LONG TIME YET."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>I,‘ arrington Terrier's " Opinion OE</head>
        <p>I,‘ arrington Terrier's " Opinion OE</p>
        <p>The War's Duration.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. E. Pollitt, 52, Wilkinson-street, Warrington, have received an interesting letter from their son, Private. W. E. Pollitt, now Serving at the Front ,with .the Warrington Terrritorials, and who formerly worked for the Cheshire Lines Committee. "We have been in the trenches for five Tays" he says. "It has been very cold at night-time. We have to go through trendies up to the knees in water to reach the one we are firing from. It is very muddy under foot. They say the Germans can't shoot, but can't they! They hit the top of the sandbags nearly every time they fire. Trench-Mate Shot Through the Head. " Yesterd:ty morning, at about six o'clock, the maul, next to me was shot right through the head. It is the first one I have seen shot down like that. We are at one of the main points of fire now. 'Our tren'ch is about 120 yards from the German lines. The night before last it was a bit misty, and the enemy did not forget to let us know that they were there. They kept on firing for above an hour; they must have been afraid of an attack. There were shells coming over us all the time from- one side or another. They tell me the war will soon be over, but from what I can see, it will last a long time yet."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>NO STRIKES DURING WAR.</head>
        <p>NO STRIKES DURING WAR.</p>
        <p>Industrial Upheavals That Endange</p>
        <p>Life.</p>
        <p>AN ADVENTUROUS NIGHT.</p>
        <p>" Terrier " Describes Trench Work When "Hell Was Let Loose."</p>
        <p>Private Albert Brown,. of the 4th South Lancs., writing to his wife (who lives at 42, Laira-street, Warrington), Under date April 14th, says: "We have just come out of the trenches again after a spell of ninety-six hours. Just before we went into the trenches I saw the most awful sight I ever want to see. We were resting in a barn at the rear of the firing line, and our offfcers were quartered in a farmhouse just across the field. They had just finished dinner and had gone out, leaving their servants to clear up. As the servants were getting on with their. work a shell went crashing through the roof ,and one of the servants was killed, a</p>
        <p>second being badly wounded, whilst two other; were shaken up. After that we went to the trenches. We got in them without losing a single man, so we must regard ,ourselves as rather lucky.</p>
        <p>A " Lively Time " in the Trenches.</p>
        <p>'This time we were in the firing trenel“, forty-six hours, and we had a lively time of it, for the Germans,tried to find our position With their big guns. Again, however, we were lucky, but instead of bursting, right among us their shells mostly .exploded.between.the.firing trench' and the reserve -trenches. Talk about the .holes the shells Made! When the water had drained into the, they were just like cluck ponds. Our forty-six hours in the trenches finished, we retired to :the dugiouts for another forty-six hours' rest, - but we had no sooner settled down than the ;enemy started shelling our rest-place. This time 'their aim was a bit too good for our liking. . •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>" Diving Into Our Holes Like Fererts."</head>
        <p>" Diving Into Our Holes Like Fererts."</p>
        <p>"You ought to have .seen .us diving into our holes; just like ferrets! One of our fellows got hit. but it did him no great harm. Our cor- poral and me were just making some stew— bully beef and flavouring—when a shell burst just over our heads. Happily, the fragments missed us, but it spoilt our dinner. Shortly afterwards relief came and most of our fellows got out for a rest, but a party of ten, including myself, had to stop behind, under the com- mand of Major Crosfield, and set to work drain- ing the trench; a job that took us an hour or so. However, we eventually all got out safely and reached our rest camp. . . . , My mate, George White, has been hit."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>‘,‘ Stormed at With Shot and Shell."</head>
        <p>‘,‘ Stormed at With Shot and Shell."</p>
        <p>Writing another letter to his, wife a day later, Private Brown tells of an exciting time he and his comrades ha 4 whilst engaged in more trench-draining work. "They were, he says, "some of the liveliest Minutes we have ever had or want to have. We were called up in the night and we marched from our camp to the trenches that had to be drained, and on the way one of our men 'was wounded. After a lot of trouble we got into the trench that required draining, and at once fell-to on the job. For about two hours all went well, save for the fact that there were a few bullets whizzing about. Then, all of a sudden, we got a surprise. It was as if hell had been let loose. Shells, mortar shells,- bullets, grenades and star 'shells flew at us and over us, and all around us, and, to,crown it all, the Germans made a charge. At this time we were up to the knees in water. We stood our ground for about ten minutes, wait- ing for orders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Up to the Waist in Water.</head>
        <p>Up to the Waist in Water.</p>
        <p>" A t- last they came; we were to retire along the 'rench as fast as we could. I shall never forget it! . . . When I read in the papers about our chaps having to fight Up to their waists in water I used to think that a lot of it was 'paper talk,' but I shan't think that about it again. To get ont of the trenches we had</p>
        <p>Who'd be a postman? It may be all very well to deliver. letters at home, but when you are in khaki and on the Continent it is a little different (some would say "a little less agree- able," others, "a little ,more spicy.") Rifleman C. Davenport, of the King's Liver- pool Regiment, who is a married man living, in times of peace, at Rockferry, Liverpool,- and whose parents reside at Holly Cottage. Stockton Heath, writes to his sister (Mrs. O'Brien, of London-road,, Stockton Heath), giving interest- ing, not to say hair-raising, details of a soldier- postman's life. Walking towards the trenches with the mails,. Rifleman Davenport saw a church steeple near by struck by a German,: explosive. Throwing himself flat on the ground. the postman escaped injury from the flying fragments, , and calmly proceeded when the im- mediate danger was 'past, to cast around for a suitable souyenir of the occasion. He found a portion of the shell—of a convenient size--and forthwith posted it home. On another oecasion , it was a tree that was the victim of the enemy fire, and again the soldier-postman had a narrow escape. In amither letter, Rifleraan Davenport tells of how the barn in which he and a number of comrades were billeted was destroyed by Shells just after the men had vacated it. Another incident of which he speaks is of a tragic nature. ' The village where he was stationed temporarily was shelled, and two .women were killed and tlventy injured. 'Rifleman Davenport, who was in business in _ . _</p>
        <p>Liverpool when the war broke out, joined a quick-moving corps, and was at the Front seven weeks from the time of enlistment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>MAY 1,19i5.</head>
        <p>MAY 1,19i5.</p>
        <p>to wade in water for about half a mile. Wo could not avoid this watery walk, for any man who showed himself . over the parapet would have gone under with a German bullet in him; so we had to 'stick it.' Anyway, we got out of it at last, but you should have seen the figure we cut—we looked 'just as if we had all fallen into a clay-pit. Once out of the trenches we marched to a 'place about a mile, away, where the roll was called, and it was found that we had had another man wounded. That business finished, • we marched home, after as adven- turous a night as one could very well have."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>TERRITORIAL OFFICERS UNDER SHELL FIRE. dne Servant Killed and Another Wounded.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIAL OFFICERS UNDER SHELL FIRE. dne Servant Killed and Another Wounded.</p>
        <p>Private Elias Thomas, formerly in the War- rington Territorials' Band and now serving at the Front with them as a stretcher-bearer, has written an interesting letter to an old friend, Private Arthur Boyle, the hon. secretary of the Working Men's Mission Junior C.C., who is at present serving in the Army Service Corps, and is temporarily stationed at Birkenhead, after, as he puts_ it, "having been. all over the place since November, attending to sick horses." Private Thomas writes that he has had many startling experiences since he crossed the water to France, and adds: "My latest experience occurred a few days ago, whilst we were having a six days' rest after a spell in the trenches. We were quartered in a small village, about five miles .from the trenches. We stretcher- bearers were billeted in one farm and the officers and their servants, together with the Maxim gun section, were at another, about two hun- dred yards away. Suddenly I heard the first shell of the day come over and saw it take the roof off the farmhouse where the officers, 4:c., were quartered. We stretcher-bearers at once ran to the spot, and ,got there just in time to see some of the men bringing out the dead body of an officers' servant. They also brought out another servant who was wounded. Hit By Pieces of Shell. Both had been hit by pieces of shell. Whilst we were attending to him we heard another, shell come whistling along, so we threw our- selVes flat down on the ground, its also did our Colonel and Major Crosfield, who had hastened up to inspect the effect of the first shell. The second shell burst about fifteen yards away from us, but, fortunately, weiall escaped with nothing worse than a mud thith. Being under shell fire is an awful experience, and when we hear the shells coming through the air, we lose no time in dropping flat on the ground, where we wait for them to explode. We don't half heave a sigh of relief when we- hear the shell go past us. . ; . So far we have had one stretcher-bearer wounded—Pte. L. D: Gough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>MAN WITH THE MAILS.</head>
        <p>THE MAN WITH THE MAILS.</p>
        <p>Narrow Escapes of Soldier-Postmat</p>
        <p>CALEDONIAN ASSOCIATION Roll of Honour Presented At Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>As was briefly reported in our 3ast issue, the annual general meeting of the Warrington Caledonian Association was held at the Lion Hotel on 'Thursday week. The chair was occupied by Mr. J. L. Davenport, in the absence of the President, Mr. J. Fulton-Smith. The reports of the treasurer and secretary were read and adopted, and the officers men- tioned were thanked for the able manner in which they had got together and presented the reports.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>PRESIDENT'S RESIGiiATION.</head>
        <p>PRESIDENT'S RESIGiiATION.</p>
        <p>The Hon. Secretary (Mr. J. Tennant) read a letter from Mr. J. Fulton-Smith, saying another engagement prevented his being present, and resigning the position .as president, an honour- able position he had held for several years. The Association, he said, had a .unique place among the societies of the town. It was the happy medium of bringing together the leading and influential men of the town of all shades of opinion. Members had had the felicity of forming valuable friendships among themselves, while they had also enjoyed in large measure the esteem of ;many friends in Warrington. Hoping for the ~ continued prosperity of the Association, he added his warmest thanks for the many acts of -kindness and courtesy shown to him by the secretary and • the office bearers at 'all times. during the happy period he had been privileged to occupy the president's chair. The Chairman paid a high tribute fo the work of .the retiring President, to whose untiring efforts, he said, the present position of the Association among the social institutions of the town was largely due.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LATE ALDERMAN FORSHAW.</head>
        <p>THE LATE ALDERMAN FORSHAW.</p>
        <p>The 'Chairman alluded to the loss the Associa- tion had sustained since the last general meet- ing, by the death of Alderman Forshaw, who had been a very enthusiastic supporter, and had assisted it in many ways. It was resolved that a record should be made in the minutes, embodying the sense of the loss the Association had sustained by Mr. Forshaw's death.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The-election of officers resulted:—</p>
        <p>President, Mr. J. L. Davenport; vice-presi- dent, Mr. W. Murray; hon. vice-presidents, Messrs. W. Skinner. B: Shearer and J. Fulton- Smith; hon. treasurer, Mr. G. Bowman; hon. secretary, Mr. J. Tennant; hon. assistant secretary, Mr. B. Williamson. Committee, 'Dr J. S. Manson, and Messrs. :T. Bennett, D. R. Cameron, J. Finlayson, J. Hunter, J. McDermid, G. Millar, P. H. Brown, E. Clayton, M. Grant, R. Lockerbie, G. Mar- shall, :W. Mitchell, J. Robertson, W. C. Russell and H. Walsh.</p>
        <p>Charity Committee, Messrs. G. Bowman, J. Tennant, B. Shearer, B. Williamson. Auditors, Messrs. A. Robertson and R. B. B urns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WORKS OF CHARITY.</head>
        <p>WORKS OF CHARITY.</p>
        <p>CouncillOr W. H. J. Jenkins expressed the thanks of the Ladies' Conimittee of the Poor Children's Summer Camp for the handsome donation of £l5, part of the proceeds of the bowling match held in conjunction with the Warrington Works Bowling League on ",White Heather Day." Fifteen pounds was also given to the funds of . the Nursing Association, also from the "White Heather Day" celebration. As the result of a whist drive, held in December, in support of the Belgian -Relief Fund; £lO- Was handed over to that fund.</p>
