      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>HELPING THE HAY.</p>
        <p>This year's hay crop is certain to make good • prices, so it will pay to look after .the meadows and "seeds." It is easy to increase the bulk of the crop by giving a nitrogenous top-dressing now whilst the grasses are in the early stage of growth. Some say that this makes the herbage washy, but this is the cave only where too much nitrogen is given on land deficient in phosphates and potash. In very many cases meadows have been dressed with basic slag or bone meal within the last few years, with or without a potash fertiliser; others have had superphosphate dur- ing the past winter or early spring. On such nitrogenous manures can be applied freely now without any danger of producing bulk at the expense of quality. Nitrogen encourages the deep-rooted, tall-growing grasses which give bulk to the crop, whilst phosphates and potash look after the finer, low-growing nutritious species which give quality to the herbage and hay. In most cases the best dressing to use now' will be licwt. nitrate of soda per acre, or up to 2cwt. (in two doses) on poor land which has re- ceived phosphates. As with wheat, sulphate of ammonia is substituted by some on very light land, and soot is a popular dressing. Liquid manure also is very suitable for this purpose. Temporary leys can be treated in the same way as meadows, but it will not do to apply more than a light nitrogenous dressing where clover enters largely into the mixture. A phosphatic manure, with potash on light land, should gen- erally be applied before sowing grasses and clovers, and then a spring dressing and a nitro- genous fertiliser can'be applied with the fullest benefit.—E. M. Bear, in "Farm and Home."</p>
        <p>CHEESE FOR SOLDIERS. At the annual meeting of the Cheshire Dairy Farmers' Association at Chester, Mr. George Barbour presiding, the council reported that the output of Cheshire cheese during 1914 had been about the average, and up to the outbreak of the war the prices were about on the same level as those of last year, after which the price of cheese went Up very considerably. Owing to the increased cost ofi production, it is question- able whether the prices made have been more profitable than those of normal times. The Chairman, in moving the adoption of the report and financial statement, expressed the hope that there would be nothing to prevent the Association holding the show in October of this year.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lewis Dodd spoke of the purchase of cheese for the Army, and said it would be well</p>
        <p>if farmers would he careful this time of the season, and would make, cheese which would stand the warm weather of the summer. They often got cheese in the early part of May which ran considerably and was of a perishable nature. He advised them to make cheese which would carry, and could be sent successfully to the men in the trenches at the Front. They trusted during this summer the dairy farmers of Cheshire might endeavour to• provide the men at the front with the kind of cheese they liked, which would sustain and keep them better than any other food they could make for them- The Duke of Westminster was re-elected presi- dent of the Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SHOWS IN WAR TIME.</head>
        <p>SHOWS IN WAR TIME.</p>
        <p>One can scarcely open an agricultural or horticultural paper in these days without read- ing that this or that show has been suspended owing to the war. Flower, vegetable, poultrY• cattle, and mixed shows in all parts of the country have been struck off the fixture lists. And the question arises: What is the reason for this wholesale suppression of what is to 130 many remote rural districts the one great event of the year? We are told it is done "on account of the war." While it must be granted that many pecuniary supporters of agriculturl!l shows are out Of the country, and while it )5 possible that these events may suffer a little _through a deficiency in gate-money, it seems to me that there must be something radically wrong if rural shows of moderate size and good reputation cannot still be held without risk of disaster.—" Agricultural Economist and Horti- cultural Review."' ' •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>TUB GARDENING.</p>
        <p>Even in these days of increased opportunitie for obtaining allotments and small holdings, there are many who lack the plot of garden they would fain possess. For such these fey; lines are penned. True, no phase of gardeninii can quite take the place'of tending a patch ol real Mother Earth, but one may have a fair measure of compensation by indulging in what may be termed succedaneous gardening—in other words, by using tubs, boxes, earthenware vases, and, indeed, ams,v receptacle that will hold soil, and in which a hole or two can be made to provide proper drainage and aeration.-- " Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>PARSLEY.</head>
        <p>PARSLEY.</p>
        <p>This is a good time to sow a breadth of parsle:r to afford a supply during the summer and earlf autumn. When sowing we have found it a good plan to sow several small plots which can be covered with lights in the autumn. When too thick the seedlings may be removed and trans' planted for lifting and placing in frames for winter use. For early spring use seed maybe sown in July, covering the young plants during the winter. Such plants start into fresli growth in February, aml a fine lot of foliage as a rule, the result. The ground for parsley should be carefully prepared early .in the year, and if wireworm or grubs are plentiful, gas lime should be freely mixed with the soil. bear- ing in mind that cropping must not be at' tempted where tbe lime is used for at least three months. When quite a lad I can remember 00' father pointing with pride to a fine lot of pars" Icy in the onion bed. A friend who was present asked the reason why parsley should always (I°,,' well in the onion bed, and the reply was, "Trey,' the ground as well for parsley as you do for onions, and you will never regret it."</p>
        <p>TWO SWEET-SCENTED ANNUALS. Some people may say that the mignonette and the night-scented stock are common, but they cannot be left out of a garden where sweet-smelling flowers are wanted. The night' scented stock is not much to look at in the daytime, but in the evening when its flowes6, open one forgets it has any drawbacks. Iv should be sown thinly, and when the seedling appear requires to be severely thinned. In the case of mignonette sturdy growth is necessall from the first, and this ,can only be had bit severe thinning, allowing not less than one foe front plant to plant in the row, with the row 6 at least 12in. apart I have allowed Thin. hei tween the lines, and in good soil this was n° too much.—" Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
