      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>HIPPODROME MANAGER.</head>
        <p>HIPPODROME MANAGER.</p>
        <p>ieming Warrington To-day For New Appointment.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. W. Turpin—the well-known, popular a 111,41 valued manager of the Hippodrome—to-day relinquishes his Warrington duties, and will on 'innday take over the management of another l °use on the Maenaghten Vaudeville Circuit— the Pier Pavilion,Southport.</p>
        <p>Varied tamer. e Turpin is a man of wide and varied tlPorience. Born in Bombay, he came to this irltrY in infancy, and commenced his business e as office boy to a London accountant. Later he became a stockbroker's clerk. The theatrical Profession, however, strongly attracted him; and Presftntly he secured "a somewhat humble POSitioD at the Shakespeare Theatre, Clap- -171.z Junction. His ability in a short time being recognised by the proprietors, he was aPPeinted manager's secretary. From that time onwards success awaited him. He became act- lug manager of the Theatre Royal, Sunderland, (Ind other similar engagements followed in Lea- ?n, Croydon, Crystal Palace, Birmingham and fletneld. It was whilst at the Grand Theatre, 'beffield, that he first met Mr. Macnaghten, With whom he has remained ever since. With Terked success Mr. Turpin. managed the Hippo- r°ree at Halifax, then coming to Warrington, where he quickly established himself a favour- It_ is interesting to note that Mr. Turpin I"as the last theatrical manager to receive the ate General Booth, and that the Hippodrome, Warrington, was the last theatre in which the Cieneral ever spoke. Services to Warrington. Good-natured, courteous and obliging—as well 4R efficient and enterprising—Mr. Turpin had gained a large circle of local friends not only in vaudeville, but also in other spheres. To many branches of the town's work he has rendered v. aluabie services; his willingness to co-operate leecharitable or other deserving efforts having workOjileereof his distinguishing features of his enterta* special * The promoters of concerts, and tions and w 'DT:Rents in aid of local institu- ar fonds have always found him willing—with en,ytt_to Mr. lelacnagliten's hearty endorsein handsome assist even by placing / the special Occ.,ll.llVedrome at their disposal on Mr osions snecee* dws J,..le:kson, of the Palace, Bradford, Tiirpin at Wa • rrington. He may rest assured of a cordial welcome.</p>
        <p>Leather Trade Good. Ilusinks in the leather trade in Warrington Isast week was very good," writes the local Corre- pondent of " The Leather Trades Review,'' .a.a.nd a much brisker note was apparent in most virections. After the hesitation of the past ek or two buyers appear to have magie up tpeir minds that further delays would be t‘l.allgerons, for inquiries and orders came along er3r strongly, and much in excess of available t110P137. To muck of the inquiry a deaf ear had 0 be turned, and such a situation was as Un- to tanner as it was to the consumer, .4)r however full the order-book may be, there is dsoinething very distasteful 'in having to turn Own good business. The new spurt, apparently, ..8 itB genkis in the new contracts being placed, Ivilich extend to the end of September, the first 'Nits of which is a demand for heavy bends Roreater than any so far experienced. The einion must again be expressed that the big :Oland for Army-substance bends cannot be and that a proportion somewhat lighter in ,struftance will have to be accepted. The ordin- qj trade is improving, and in view of the small antitY of light to medium bends being pro- the demand seems to be quite brisk. abriders are selling very well at full prices, tathere are signs of a change in the position iibellies. After a dull interval interest in this .is beginning to revive, and a renewed in rough dried bellieg may probably Out a sharp re-action in the rolled ailk.`7,`Y,- The raw goods market still provides 10ed for anxiety."</p>
        <p>Acute Kidney Disease letely Cured at 82 by Dr. Cassell's Tablets, the All-British Remedy. Irk Cjift-o*cle Grey, an, old gentleman (82), ken, --terr ' ace Queen-street, S -rkly tear. -In, says A few ave.' 110, .1 began to /ay ba• Isflawing pain across Pain aek. At all times this with me, but it thr,s sharp as 'knife When •I had to or Ittitolßhten up from a sitting .tald ing position. I was hat Tras kidney trouble, f geoeuting I tried did any he Then my bladder . ca;'-'s affected, and then mr. e the keen agony of 418Steries. Operation was advised, but as a_ 1,. resort I got Dr. Cassell's Tablets. awl orizalost frotn the first they relieved me. Now I ir:red and in splendid health." 1.410 re Cassell's n Tablets. ree, s-assell's Tablets are a genuine and tested tus' for all forms of nerve or bodily wealviesssiin. Rrecti: 3')llni.". hey are composed. of harmless the ..s_rits waich have an invigorating effect htit'verve centres. and are the surest remedyior n all ritRAWSIBREAKDOWN KIDNEY DISEASE SINNA. PARALYSIS INDIGESTION INPAGA PARALYSIS STOMACH DISORDER Ntun"'ILEPARALYSIS MAL-NUT.RITION___ Iititc,,ASTHENIA • W DEBILITY PALPITATION ASTING DiSEASES ____ kiIePLESSNESS VITAL EXHAUSTION Arid , OLD-AGE WEAKNESS and iTir° esneciallv valuable for Nursing Mothers. star,. Critical Periods of Life. All chemists and !t Tale, in all 10 parts of the _v_mrld sell Dr. Cassell's ,k_ 24. 9eie A t Id., le. lid., and 2s. 9d.. Triaf-"Fze being the most economical. A Ilire 1253 i Add %)ad Ply will be sent to you on receipt of na ress and two penny stamps for postage and Add Penny Dr. Cassell's Co., Ltd. unaster-road, Manchester.</p>
        <p>of rook, Bir- - &lt; . • &lt; • , •ey, Birinieujitana</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>CHILDREA MURDERED.</head>
        <p>CHILDREA MURDERED.</p>
        <p>Medical Missionary Tells of the Opium Curse in Persia.</p>
        <p>In Kerman, Persia, children of five and six years of age work from sunrise to sunset in unhealthy factories, making Persian carpets and thus earning money so that their parents may the more completely indulge the deadly opium habit. The consequences are revolt- ing to contemplate.</p>
        <p>This crying evil, and others, must be abol- ished, says the Church Missionary Society, under whose auspices Miss W. Westlake (mis- sionary from Kerman) delivered an extremely interesting address on Thursday evening in the Patten Ball.</p>
        <p>LOVE IN ACTION. The Chairman (the Rev. E. Chamberlain) said that he wanted to take his hearers back, to the year 1865, when two very special Mis- sions were started—the Church Missionar: Society and hisown,matrimonial-mission. Both these missions had for. their 'motto !"Love in. action." The C.M.S. now had fifty years' suc- cessful work behind it, and he personally, if the personal element might be permitted.. looked back on fifty-years of equally successful married life. The object of medimi missions was one of .the finest possible ,motives--it con- sisted in giving practical expression to love 'of Christ; and they all wanted to "do their little bit," as the soldiers said, .for that great work. The society now treated 1.2,000 in- patients and 126,000 out-patients: The Society for-the Propagation of the Gospel, which as founded out of the C.M.S. funds, was now in a flourishing and quite independent condition— as regarded the central funds. That was one of the things the. C.M.S. had done, but the chief work, of course, had been the direct work in the foreign field, where the doctor had gradually been supplanting the wizard, and all Pchts of barbarous customs had been abolished.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Plea for United Mission.</head>
        <p>Plea for United Mission.</p>
        <p>Having deplored ,the apathetic attitude of a number of ministers in the matter of mission- ary work and remarked that Christianity was not a life-giving tree .unless it. was full of miesionary sap, the Chairman,, made a plea for a united foreign mission, pointing out the confusion that was bound to arise—and did arise—out of the number of different denomina- tions of .Christianity ,which had. sent missions out to the various heathen lands. Miss Westlake opened with an allusion to the corrupt 'Government 'of Persia. The Moslem, Government had no idea of honesty Or truth in any form—that, at any rate, was the con- clusion she had come to after ten years at Kerman. The system was one of 'might. is right." Illustrating her point, the speaker stated that the method employed by the auth- orities in the event of a rebellion against, say, undue taxation, or some other injustice, was to bring out the soldiers, who would chase the rebels out into the desert, catch a few and make examples of them by walling them up in plaster-of-Paris and leaving them to die.</p>
        <p>Healing the Brigands. The story of an interesting journey from Kerman to the coast, where she was going to meet a colleague who was about to take her place at Kerman, was told by Miss Westlake. She was in a hurry to reach the coast, as the vessel . bringing, the relieving:.mii,sionary. was due to arrive within a very short space of time. In consequence she asked the permission of the authorities to be allowed to travel via a road that had been, closed to the public owing to its being infested by brigands, but which was a very much shorter route than the safer one. She was warned of the risks, but was allowed to go by the short cut. On. the journey she was met by some oft the brigands and taken to the chief brigand's castle, where she was asked to exercise her powers as a doctor and utilise her medicines in healing a sickness that had broken out amongst the men. She consented; and, was allowed to camp out near the castle. She was excellently and hospitably treated and spent twelve most entertaining hours there, healing the :men, women and: children of the band. A guide was provided by the chief brigand, to safely escort her to the regular track.</p>
        <p>The army in Persia, said Miss Westlake, con-, sister- of a mob of untrained ne'er-do-weels: drawn from the village loafer class. Touching, on the question of diseases, the speaker said that smallpox and kindred infections com- plaints were still rife, but not to anything like the extent they used to be before the introduc- tion, in those parts, of vaccination, to which the natives submitted with an excellent grace: It had proved very effective.</p>
        <p>The Curse of Rich and Poor. Opium smoking and eating had been an in- creasing curse in Kerman for the last thirty years. Rich and poor alike indulged in that terrible habit. The poor people, in order the more completely to indulge themselves in the vice, sent their children to work in stuffy, ill- ventilated carpet manufactories, where they stayed from sunrise to sunset, without getting any recreation and often scarcely anything to eat. This sort of thing was done in the case of children as young as five and six years of age. They frequently developed rickets and paralysis, and many died as the result of the cruel conditions of their lives. The drug was unfortunately so commonly obtainable and comparatively cheap. A large number of women tried to commit suicide by means of Upturn. The missionaries had many kinds of things to fight against. There was not a lot of deliberate opposition, but a troublesome factor was the apathy that prevailed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Effect of the War.</head>
        <p>The Effect of the War.</p>
        <p>The war jii.ta -ii"s"-a,---ve-1;-- serious effect on conditions out there. As soon as the Reuter telegram came through, announcing that war had been declared, the heads of the carpet firms gave orders to stop all looms, and in consequence much privation and suffering had been experienced. Concluding with an appeal for more helpers,. Miss Westlake stated that in March, April and, May of each Year the staff they had to deal with the cases arising in Kerman might he trebled and then would not be adequate to the demand. The Rev. W. R. Cole remarked- that the heathen amongst whom the- medical mission- aries ministered regarded the cases of healing almost as miracles. • The message of the medical mission was a three-fold one: firstly. there was the message of healing; secondly, the message of sympathy; and, thirdly, the message of the lA)Ve of God. On the proposition of the Rev. B. Percival, a vote of thank it was .accorded Miss Westlake for her interesting address. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>CONVALESCENT WARRIORS</head>
        <p>CONVALESCENT WARRIORS</p>
        <p>Star liinema Helps New Auxiliary Home Hospital.</p>
        <p>At tlje Star Kinema House, Church-street, on Wednesday afternoon, a special matinee was .given in aid of the newly-opened Thelwall Auxiliary Home hospital (Thelwall Heyes). The attractive programme which had been arranged opened with "Pathes' Animated Gazette," depicting various scenes from the seat of war. Following this came a cleverly- played violin solo by Miss Myra Payne, whose sister—Miss Bertha Payne—later on gave the patriotic song entitled "Our Land." The four- part, picture "Mrs. Black is Back," enacted by the famous Players Co., and featuring "May Irwin," proved to be a fine comedy, and it thoroughly delighted the large audience present. Another of the talented Payne family, Miss Phyllis, rendered the song "The Daughter -of Cadiz," whilst later on all three sisters joined in the trio "A Summer Night." The pro- gramme closed with the picture "The Monkey Cabaret." During ,the afternoon the Mayor (Maerman Peter Peacock), in the course of a short speech, thanked the proprietors of the Kinema for so kindly placing the building at the disposal of the Hospital Committee. He also thanked the artistes for their services, and also Mrs. Liglitburn and Mrs. Frost, and her lady helPers, for giving their services at the check boxes and in the body of the hall, Bze. PUBLIC INSPECTION OF THE NEW HOSPITAL. • The-new Home Hospital was thrown open for public inspection on Thursday, 'when many attended and admired the prAparations which had been made for the comfort of the pi•os- pective patients. The committee, of which Mr. F. Aylmer Frost is the secretary, will be glad to receive presents of money, groceries, station- ery,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAYS MAY 1, 1915. TRADE AND LABOUR. !TRIED TO SAVE HIS MATE IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAYS MAY 1, 1915. TRADE AND LABOUR. !TRIED TO SAVE HIS MATE IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>WANTED TUE BALL BACK.</p>
        <p>011119 Defendants' Request To Magistrates.</p>
        <p>Three lads were summoned at Thursday's Police Court for playing football in a public thoroughfare, when it was stated that boys all over the town caused a great deal of annoyance by playing this and similar games in the streets besides occasionally doing damage. In fining the trio of defendants 3s. 6d. each, the Chairman (Mr. J. Rymer Young—whose colleagues on the Bench were Dr. Naden and Messrs. W. T. Williams and A. J. Jolley), said "Football is a good game if played in a proper place, but the street is not a proper place." After hearing the magistrates' decision, the three lads 'stood whispering together, which caused the Chairman to ask "Well, what is it?" "Please sir," stammered, one of the blushing youngsters, "may we have the ball back? It doesn't belong to us!" The Chief Constable: Then they are not entitled to it.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I take it the Chief Constable will return it to the _boy from whom you borrowed it.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: lie must come and parley with us for it. It is probable that we shall, then want to summon him for aiding and abetting these toys in the offence. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>" KIDDIED " TO HIS COST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Interesting Speeches At Dinner and Social. SECRETARY COMPLIMENTED.</head>
        <p>Interesting Speeches At Dinner and Social. SECRETARY COMPLIMENTED.</p>
        <p>To celebrate the occasion of Mr. Chas. Dukes' appointment to the secretaryship of the Lanca- shire and Cheshire Federation of Trades and Labour Councils, a well-attended and enjoyable social followed a dinner held at Atkinson's Cafe? Bridge-street, on Tuesday evening. After the excellent repast had been done full justice to the toasts called forth many eloquent speeches—which were happily interspersed with capital humorous items from Mr. W. W. Whittle; Mr. A. E. Prow presided efficiently at the piano. HOPEFUL HAPPENINGS.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Berry occupied the chair, and voiced his gratification at the pleasure and privilege of doing so. When, he declared, they thought of the number of crafts whose members had come tagether that night to .enjoy SOcial inter- course, and of the many unions they repre- sented, they might well hope it would prove a great impetus to the strengthening of their movement in .Warrington. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>They were met together to do honour to their secretary. Whilst some of them differed from him on many peintS, they were bound to give him credit for having done fine service for many branches of the movement—not only in Warrington, but in many other towns. He hoped the time was not far distant when they would all, like the employers, be united; and when they would see the emancipation of their class. (Hear, hear, and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S IMPORTANCE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S IMPORTANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Haley, in proposing the toast of "The Guests," wished Mr. Dukes every success in the new office he was undertaking. Warring- ton was often said to be an important town, but from the labour point of view it was a very important town—one which Contains a multi- plicity and variety of trades, which makes it quite different in a sense from other towns. Where there was such a multiplicity and variety of trades there was bound to be a corre- sponding number of unions, and they had needed a head and-hand that could bring them into cohesion. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Without touching upon the terrible conflict at present going on, concluded the speaker, they would need a strong hand and a clear head in charge of their Affairs, for all the tendencies of the hour: pointed 't6,,the fact that tkey would have a momentous struggle as soon as the great military struggle was over. "(Applause.) Mr. James Hamblett (in. the absence of Messrs. E. Plin.ston and J. (3rearley) responded, and added that it was for him a real delight to be associated with Comrade Dukes. He did not agree with him in everything—he did not think Comrade Dukes would like him to; but he did respect him for his deep desire to lift up his fellow-men. They looked forward to the time when out of tribulation they would, by stand- ing firm,. come forth stronger than ever. They could not have a more worthy secretary than they had. They all joined in wishing him health and wealth—not so much financially, but wealth in everything that was good. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>AT FACTORY-GATES.</head>
        <p>AT FACTORY-GATES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Adam Bennett also testified to the work of the secretary. Many a time on the strike committee, he stated, they had been: very thank- ful that they had Comrade Dukes amongst</p>
        <p>them; he seemed to give them an impetus to go orf when things were looking black. The thing which had really made Trade Unionism "go" in Warrington was the stand made at the factory-gates, where Comrade Dukes spoke to the men. That was a new departure in the town,; it marked a step forward. Ile wished Mr. Dukes every success and prosperity. "He is," said the speaker, "always 'wishful for in- tellectual development, and I think- we shall all benefit from the new work he has . taken up." (Applause.). The toast was then drunk, and "He's a jolly good fellow" heartily sung.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SOLDIER TRADE UNIONISTS.</head>
        <p>SOLDIER TRADE UNIONISTS.</p>
        <p>The health of "The Trades and Labour Coun- cil" was proposed by The Chairnian; who said that in doing so One Could not help thinking about the national crisis 'and of their Trade Unionists i who were fighting their battles out in France in a struggle which would doubtless be a prolonged one. "It makes our hearts bleed," be continued, "to think of the terrible agonies which many of our members are going through, and our activities. have .been crippled by the absence of those members in the regrettable conflict. -Put those of us who have not yet felt the nation's call shall at least stand by the principles to which we have adhered in the past—that our members who have gone away shall not come back to find lost the principles for which they fought so hard." (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>TRADE UNION TERMS.</head>
        <p>TRADE UNION TERMS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chas. Dukes, on rising to reply, was well received, lie said he scarcely knew how to return thanks for their kind 'words; the one thing was that he had only been the outstand- ing figure in a group of men who had perhaps been too shy themselves to shine in the lime- light, but who had in a hundred and one ways seen that he had the opportunity of carrying out the work which probably more by natural temperament than inclination he had apparently been fitted to perform. He was proud to have been a Trade Unionist ever since he commenced work. His parents had also in their day been somewhat lively in the Labour world, but certain it was that he knew little or nothing until his introduction to the I.L.P. —at that time a pioneer Labour movement' in this country. Perhaps that qualification was necessary, because he himself had left it. (Laughter.) _ _</p>
        <p>But there seemed to be a general reticence in accepting progressive thought; it was one thing to call oneself a Trade Unionist, and quite asather thing really to understand in a modern sense \what was meant by Trade Unionism. Some accented it in the eraft interpretation,' or in the industrial interpretation; others aught• go so far as' to say • that it never could benefit that class which they desired to emancipate. The very mention of Trade :Union term' frightened people simply because they refused to understand them and got an erroneous im- pression. What the movement was thinking to-day had all been thought twenty years ago. But • class emancipation! they could not have talked about that twenty years ago; they would have been put, down as the most rabid of the red rags! Yet they went further than; that to- day; they went about instituting what in their opinion was a new way of bringing these things about. They .could not separate, the Trade UNtion movement from the Labour movement, or the Labour movement from the Socialist move- ment—simply bechuse at the head of affairs those who were giving the real guiding prin- ciple of their movement were not merely men who desired to play for popularity, they were men who desired to give expression to the popu- lar will, and to mould it and give it an objec- tive.</p>
        <p>What was the cl)jeet of. .the Trade Union movement? . They took their banners— "Defence, not "Defiance," and a hundred</p>
        <p>other things. Well, reference had been made to the great advance in Warrington in a few years they raised the wages from 17s. 10d. to 255. Yes, but the employers had not lost any time in knocking them down again during the national crisis. After all, the money they had spent in strikes and in defending them- selves in lock-outs, they were worse off collect- ively% than ten years before. In Ae progress of time forces were at work • negating all their achievements. They must have less leadership and more delegation—more consciousness amongst the mass. (Applause.)•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>EXPIRED PASS</head>
        <p>THE EXPIRED PASS</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Mannion was charged at Monday's Police Court with having been absent since Friday from the 6th South Lancashires. stationed in Ireland.—P.C. lloare said that when, at his request, the Private produced his pass, he found it to ,be expired.—The defaulter was handed to, an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>TWO MORE DEFAULTERS.</head>
        <p>TWO MORE DEFAULTERS.</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Grady, of the 6th Loyal North Lancashires, at present in Ireland, wa-; at Monday's Police Court ordered to be handed to an escort.—Acting-Sergeant Whitby told how he founa the prisoner to be an absentee.—The same decision applied to the case of Private McCleary, who was shown by Inspector Hesp to be an absentee from the A Company, 10th Highland Light Infantry, now in Surrey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Thirteen-Year-Old Boy's Great Gallantry. STRUGGLE IN A RESERVOIR</head>
        <p>Thirteen-Year-Old Boy's Great Gallantry. STRUGGLE IN A RESERVOIR</p>
        <p>The story of a thirteen-year-old boy's gallant attempt to save a drowning workmate was re- lated at an inquest held by Mr. Deputy-Coroner F. A. Jones, at the Warrington Coroner's Court on Thursday morning, on the body of John James Robinson, aged 15, whose parents li at 57, Longford-street.—Mr. H. Brothers, Factory Act Inspector, watched the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>FATHER'S SAD DISCOVERY ON THE RESERVOIR BANK.</head>
        <p>FATHER'S SAD DISCOVERY ON THE RESERVOIR BANK.</p>
        <p>The first witness was the deceased lad's father —John Robinson, a general labourer, who -is employed by the Cheshire Lines Committee. The witness said that his son, who was fifteen last birthday, was employed at the Longford Wire Works.. Witness last saw him alive at six o'clock' on Tuesday morning, when he left to go to his work. About seven o'clock the same night witness heard that he was in a reservoir at the Wire Works, and hurrying there found his son lying on the bank-. He was examined by Dr. McKee in witness's presence, and the doctor pronounced life extinct. Wit- ness had cautioned deceased several times about bathing in the reservoir; indeed, he spoke to him on the matter as recently as last Saturday. The boy could swim.</p>
        <p>The Deputy-Coroner: 'lf lie could swim, why didn't you want him to swim?—Witness: I objected to the place where he went to bathe, and I had chastised him for bathing there.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>HOW HE TRIED TO SAVE HIS CHUM.</head>
        <p>HOW HE TRIED TO SAVE HIS CHUM.</p>
        <p>Arthur. James Slater, aged 13, the son: of Charlotte. Slater, of--I,."Dudley-street, said he worked with the &amp;Ceased as a wire twister. At six o'clock on Tuesday evening, after finishing work, deceased came up to him and asked him to go for a swim in the reservoir, as it was warm. Witness consented to do so. After he had been in the water about five minutes de- ceased got in difficulties in the middle of the reservoir, and called out for help. At the moment witness was-dressing. himself. He im- mediately started to undress again, and as he was doing so noticed deceased go down twice. When he was stripped witness plunged in and swam to the deceased's assistance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Would-be Rescuer Loses Consciousness.</head>
        <p>Would-be Rescuer Loses Consciousness.</p>
        <p>As he drew near the deceased caught him by the. legs. Witness cried: out for help and an- other boy who had been bathing with them entered the water. After that witness lost con- sciousness, and the next thing he remembered was coming to on the bank of the reservoir. Altogether witness had bathed in the reservoir three times. To get to the reservoir a wall had to be climbed. There was no wire netting on the wall at the place where they got over. Detective-Sergeant Summers said there had been wire netting on the wall, but it had. been completely torn down. The firm were con- stantly replacing the wire, but the boys kept pulling it down again. Slater denied that he had helped to tear any of the wire down.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>ANOTHER PLUCKY LAD'S UNAVAILING</head>
        <p>ANOTHER PLUCKY LAD'S UNAVAILING</p>
        <p>EFFORT.</p>
        <p>Albert L. Froggart, another thirteen-year-old boy employed at the works, whose home is at 8, Sharp-street, Warrington, and who was bath- ing with the deceased and the previous witness, said he saw Robinson and Slater struggling in the water and dived in and went to their assistance. Witness got up to them and started to pull them to the side, but he could not hold them- by himself, 'so- shouted for more •help. Some of the workmen Came up and pulled the decease&amp; Slater and witness out. of the water. Witness, then went_ for, Dr. McKee.- Witness could'swim:and did not know, that the boys had- no right to bathe in the reservoir;. in fact, he "thought. it was for the working lads to swim in.,,</p>
        <p>The Deputy-Coroner: And that's the 'reason why the firm took all the trouble to put a wall round it, is it?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WORKMAN'S PROMPT ACTION SAVES \ ONE LIFE.</head>
        <p>WORKMAN'S PROMPT ACTION SAVES \ ONE LIFE.</p>
        <p>William Holloway, a general labourer, of 115, Longford-street, deposed that at 6.30 on Tuesday night he was at work, when he was told that there were two boys drowning in the reservoir. He ran to the bank and saw the deceased and Slater in the middle of the water, evidently in a state of complete exhaustion. He threw a piece of tintber out to them, and then followed in after it. By the time witness reached the spot where they were struggling, the deceased had disappeared, and Slater was just sinking. Witness caught hold of Slater, and then also grabbed the other lad and kot them to the bank and hauled them out of the water. He then started to apply artificial respiration, and Slater soon recovered consciousness, but _Dr. McKee, who arrived on the scene in a very -Aloft time, pronounced the other lad to be beyond aid.</p>
        <p>The Deputy-Coroner: Is the reservoir used regularly as d bathing place. Wituess : I have only known it to be used these last three weeks, but then I have only been at work there that length of time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RESERVOIR NOT A BATHING PLACE.</head>
        <p>THE RESERVOIR NOT A BATHING PLACE.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Hannah, assistant manager at the Longford Wire Works, said the reservoir was not provided as a bathing place, as one of the witnesses had appeared to think. It was sur- rounded by a wall four .feet high, and on top of the wall there was a barbed' wire fence, which had been repeatedly broken down and replaced. lii order to keep boys from bathing in the reser- voir the services of the police had been requisi- tioned at week-ends. The Deputy-Coroner: If a boy can swim it is only very natural that he shouldgwant, to take to the water whenever the opportunity is afforded' him. One cannot but 'admire a boy's liking,to 'be clean. Is there anywhere near- the works where these boys could have bathed? Witness': There is no place on the firm's premises. . Detective-Sergeant Summers: There are no swimming baths within a mile. The Deputy-Coroner: That's not far. I used frequently to travel eight miles for a swim. In answer to Mr. Brothers, the witness said that he thought that when the reservoir was first built the idea was that it should be used for bathing, but latterly the firm had tried all they could to prevent it being so used. Notices prohibiting bathing in the reservoir had been put up, but they had been pulled down. The Deputy-Coroner: The boys or Warring- ton are like the boys everywhere else.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>VERDICT AND A SUGGESTION.</head>
        <p>THE VERDICT AND A SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of "Accidentally drowned," expressed their sympathy with the relatives, and suggested that fresh notices should be put up warning the boys and others not to bathe in the reservoir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>DEPUTY-CORONER COMPLIMENTS A PLUCKY YOUTH.</head>
        <p>DEPUTY-CORONER COMPLIMENTS A PLUCKY YOUTH.</p>
        <p>Calling the lad Slater up to him, the Deputy- Coroner said: ." You did a very creditable and very plucky thing in'going in to try and save your workmate. You ran a great risk; indeed, you had a very narrow shave of being drowned yourself. I admire anybody who swims; indeed, if I had my own way, I should make swimming part of the curriculum of every school. Swim- ming is very healthy, and it is also a very necessary accom.plishment. Don't give up swim- ming because of this sad accident, hut don't go swimming in places—such as reservoirs—which are likely to prove dangerous, nor in other places where notices are put up forbidding bathing- You did your best to save this lod, and, as I said before, you are entitled to great ..ro(lit for what you did.</p>
        <p>An Old Question. I'll leave it to sou," declared Eve. " What's-the question?" inquired Adam. "Shall we clean house or dig a new cave?"</p>
        <p>Hard on Jim. Mother: "Well, Master Jim hasn't gone to the Front after all!" Cook: "Oh, poor Master Jim! And• 'e's so fond of a day's shootin'."—"Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>"NOT A LEG TO STAND ON."</head>
        <p>"NOT A LEG TO STAND ON."</p>
        <p>Man Who Over-Celebrated.</p>
        <p>"lie hadn't a leg to stand on," was 'a con- stable's descriptive way of informing the War- rington Bench, on Wednesday morning, of the plight in which he found Bernard Dowd in Bold-street.</p>
        <p>Bernard, it appeared, had over-celebrated hiS departure for his first military depot. He enlisted on Monday, and should have been at his depot in Dublin on Tuesday evening. The police officer found him leaning against a friendly wall, trying to remember the way to walk straight, and took him in charge, as he was using unseemly language. The Bench gave the prisoner twenty-four hours in which to. join his regiment, and the Chief- Constable said he would see that he was pat on the irish mall train.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>SEVEN DAYS FOR BEGGING.</head>
        <p>SEVEN DAYS FOR BEGGING.</p>
        <p>Manchester Man and Sonic Marmalade</p>
        <p>A man from Manchester who preferred the hospitality of kindness to the hospitality of the Poor Law, is now being provided for by the Government—in prison.</p>
        <p>John Marshall was charged before Mr. J. W. Hayward (chairman), Alderman Smethurst, Mr. C. Jackson Holmes and Mr. W. T. Williams, at the 'Warrington Police Court on Wednesday, with begging in Roome-street, on Tuesday. Evidence was given by a police constable. who -stated .that he saw prisoner receive bread and marmalade- and tea- from a resident, in Rome- street. Witness arrested prisoner. The man from Manchester pleaded guilty, and said that he had walked from that city on the day in question, and left in need of sustenance. The Chairman pointed, out to prisoner that he could have obtained what he wanted with- out going to private houses, and sentenced him to seven days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>NEGLECTFUL DRIVERS.</head>
        <p>NEGLECTFUL DRIVERS.</p>
        <p>Chief Constable On "A Common And</p>
        <p>Dangerous Practice."</p>
        <p>The'Chief Constable had something to sa3, about the carelessness of lurry drivers as a class at the Borough Police Court on Thursday morn- ing, when Walter Clare, of 42, Rose and Crown- street, was summoned for being such a distance away from his horse and cart in Wilson Patten- street, as not to have control over the same. A polite officer spoke' to seeing a horse and lurry proceeding up Wilson Patten-street with- out a driver in charge. A few seconds later defendant -came running up to it. When asked why he had allowed the horse to proceed un- attended, defendant bad no excuse to offer, the fact being that he had been gossiping with another driver lower down the road. The Chief Constable: And I suppose these pen will be striking one of these, days for an increase of pay. As a'class they persist in leaving their horses and carts unattended, and it is not always a case of going into public-houses and getting drink, but sometimes, as in the 'present case, the men loiter behind chatting, whilst in other they go slouching along the pathway. It is a practice that ought to be stopped, and it is becoming a common and dangerous one; dangerous not only to the driver' masters' horse and property, but also ,to motorists, cyclists and pedestrians. ' The Bench iinpoSed a fine of ss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>HAT THE DRINK DID.</head>
        <p>HAT THE DRINK DID.</p>
        <p>Responsible for Conduct of Man Alleged to Have Stolen Watch.</p>
        <p>Rowland. George Partridge, electrician, of 5. St. Mary,street, was 'charged at the Borough Police • Court on Monday with, having stolen a lady's watch (value. 65.. 6d.)—the property of Benedict Tyrer, 100, Buttermarket-street.— Detective-Sergeant Summers and Detective Skelton had effected the arrest.</p>
        <p>• It was. stated by the Chief Constable, who asked for a remand, that' at half-past nine on Saturday night Partridge entered Mr. Tyrer's shop, and asked Mr. Cooke (the assistant) to show him .some boy's metal watches. A num- ber of these—each about 3s. in value—were Placed before him, and after some bargaining he asked to see some more expensive ones; where. upon the assistant produced, several lady's silver watches. Subsequently he bought a 3s. watch; but when the assistant's back was turned took a lady's 6s. 6d.1 watch, and left. Immediately he had gone the assistant found that the watch was missing, and rtin out into the street. There, was, however, no trace of Part- ridge. The police were informed, and they found prisoner at his lodgings in St. Mary-street. s So far as the police knew; added Mr. Nicholls, prisoner's antecedents were respectable. He had apparently been employed by a corporation in Yorkshire. If the . magistrates would order a remand the police would make inquiries. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Prisoner's Plea.</head>
        <p>Prisoner's Plea.</p>
        <p>. Prisoner: I have been discharged from the service, sir, with an exemplary charac- ter. I didn't do it with the intention of doing any wrmig: L wasn't drunk, but I had had a good deal of drink. The case was remanded for seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>AA OMEN IT \"R.</head>
        <p>AA OMEN IT \"R.</p>
        <p>Trouble In Dial-street.</p>
        <p>A story of women's warfare was unfolded at the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday morning, when Anne Jane Fleming, a married woman, of 14, Dial-street, Warrington, was charged with being drunk and disorderly, and with assaulting Catherine Millington and Selina Urey, of 1, Dial-yard and 6, Melville-street, respectively, on April 27th. P.C. Dixon said that at 6.55 on the evening of the clay in question, he was on duty in Buttermarket-street, when he was called to Dial-street. He saw the prisoner come out of</p>
        <p>her house and rush at -Mrs. Millington and Mrs. Urey, striking them in various parts of the body and on the head, and using an enamel bowl and a poker as weapons. She was drunk, and her language was disgraceful. She had been "carrying on" in Dial-street like that for the past fortnight. She had a three-weeks-old baby. _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Poker and Bowl as Weapons.</head>
        <p>Poker and Bowl as Weapons.</p>
        <p>Catherine Millington, who stated that she was the wife of Samuel Millington, gave evidence to the effect that prisoner threw a stone at her ,(witness's) back door. Witness came out, and prisoner rushed at her and struck her with .her fist. Prisoner then got a poker and bowl, and with these struck.: Mrs. Urey. Prisoner's language was such that witness could not bring herself to repeat it. The,Chairman (Mr. J. W. Hayward) said it was not necessary.</p>
        <p>Selina Urey averred that prisoner threw a bucket at her door and, when she came out to see what the noise was about, rushed at her with a poker and a boW-1, and struck her several blows, using very bad language.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Said She Did It In Self-defence.</head>
        <p>Said She Did It In Self-defence.</p>
        <p>Prisoner's defence was that she was attatked by the two women who had given evidence, and by other members of their families all at once. She had to defend herself.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Fleming proceeded to get excited, and made allegations against one of the women who had given evidence against her. but she as pulled up sharply by the Chief Constable, who reminded her that she was not in Dial-court, but in the Police Court, and had better addr•-!ss the Bench in a proper way. The Chief Constable proved ' previous con- victions for the neglect of children and com- mon assault, and gave prisoner the character of a lady of warlike propensities. He thought she ought to be made an example of. Her hus- band was employed at the Whitecross Works. The Chairman, in telling the prisoner that she would be fined 108., remarked that she was being let off lightly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Trip From Frodsham Costs Ticketless</head>
        <p>Trip From Frodsham Costs Ticketless</p>
        <p>Traveller £l.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, be- fore Councillor H. Roberts and other magis- trates, John Mannion, puddler, 109, Bewsey- road, was summoned for travelling on the L. and N.-W. Railway from Frodsham to War- rington without paying his fare, and with in- tent to avoid payment.—He did not appear. Mr. Arthur Browne appeared for the Railway Company, and said that on Easter Monday Mannion LIM several friends went to Frodsham, taking single- tickets. They returned by train at 9.6 p.m. When Ticket-collector Blinston asked defendant for his ticket he replied that he had not come by train, but had only been on the platform. He had not, however, got a platform ticket, so was invited to pay his fare from Chester. He then refused, again declar- ing that he had not come by train; be would not give his name or address. When, however, 'Detective Wisbey (of the Railway Co.) was called, defendant admitted that he had come from Frodsham. The officer asked him why he said he had only been on the platform, and Mannion answered: "I was only 'kidding' him; I did not mean anything. I will pay the fare from Frodsham." He again refused to give his name and address, and the ease -was reported. Detective Wisbey and Detective-Sergeant Hewitt corroborated. The former said he had to ask for Mannion's name four times before getting it. The fine totalled 20s. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>"TOUCHED UP" HORSE TILL IT BLED. –</head>
        <p>"TOUCHED UP" HORSE TILL IT BLED. -</p>
        <p>carter, Once kind To His Animal,</p>
        <p>Becomes Cruel.</p>
        <p>To Tuesday's Police Court was summoned Thomas Spendor, carter, of Soapery-row, Man- chester-road, Paddington--for having in Win- wick-street, ill-treated a horse at 12.20 on the afternoon of Saturdayrthe 17th ult. Ex-Sergeant Evans said he noticed Spendor in charge of a lurry laden with coal. The man struck the' horse very savagely ten or a dozen times. He remonstrated with Spender, and -pointed out that if the animal was not strong enough to draw the load that he should take it out of the shafts. Defendant did so, and wit- ness was about to go away when he saw Spender again savagely strike the animal five or six times. He remonstrated once more with the man, and threatened to have him locked up.</p>
        <p>"Therefore You Flog It." Inspector Dee, of the R.S.P.C.A., said that on .the •horse were many cuts. and freshly-drawn block!. When he charged defendant with ill- treating the horse, the reply was: "I only just touched it up." When asked how he accounted for the bloodstains on the animal his reply was "I must have done that when I just touched it up a bit." Defendant: If you leave the animal alone it will fall asleep on the floor. The Chairman (Councillor Roberts): And therefore you flog it! Defendant: I told the foreman she was not fit to he used; she is slow and not equal to the work.</p>
        <p>Inspector Dee: A few minutes before Mr. Evans came to me I had seen Spendor with his horse, and it appeared to be going along fairly well then. And an hour or two before it was also going along all right.</p>
        <p>What He Deserved.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said defendant had twice previously been before the Court—for stealing a horse-cloth and a horse-rug. "I don't know," he added, "whether the Bench think it would he desirable to administer the whip on the defendant as he used it on the horse."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Rymer Young (who-N--k---as--On'ilie Bench): The best thing!</p>
        <p>A letter was read to the effect that the defend- ant had been previously in the employment of Messrs. R. A. Naylor, who gave him a good characfer, and said he had been Very attentive to his horse. A fine of 20s. was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>"THE GREEN-EYED MONSTER."</head>
        <p>"THE GREEN-EYED MONSTER."</p>
        <p>JealouSy At Root Of Troublesome Case</p>
        <p>Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair) and Messrs. Jas. Hepherd, C. Jackson Holmes, T. Locker and J. Rymer Young, at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, Alice Vernon, or M.urrow, who has been living with her 'parents at 47, Algernon-street, was charged with having neglected her two children.—Mr. Arthur Browne appeared on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., and said prisoner was before the Court in October. The presiding magistrate then told prisoner "You will either have to consent to go to a home, or we shall have to send you to prison. If you leave the home before six months you will come up for sentence."—Mr. Laws (the Court missionary) obtained admis- sion .for the woman to the Convent of the Good Shepherd, Liverpool. She remained there two months, and then insisted upon leaving— despite the efforts of the sisters to prevail upon her to stay. Arriving home, she behaved well for a time, but subsequently Detective Turner was called to the mother's house three times tint} found that ,the woman had smashed ,crockery and broken the windows. Prisoner's parents were respectable, but could do no good with her. One child was.' being kept by ' Mrs. Ball, of Grafton-street, and the • other by prisoner's mother. They would remember that it was prisoner's mother who last time had pleaded for her daughter to have one more chance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Sane, But Uncontrollable.</head>
        <p>Sane, But Uncontrollable.</p>
        <p>The police report stated that, although the woman appeared perfectly sane, she was irresponsible and uncontrollable, and the most excitable and hopeless dipso-maniac, and that in the drunken fury in which she would always be, she might do some serious injury to herself, her children or other persons. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Laws said the Dian she lived with was all right, lie was a Quartermaster-Sergeant, and every week sent her five shillings for each C-hild and ten- shillings for herself. Last time she went over to see him at the training centre she upset him also, and got him turned out of his billet. Prisoner: No, sir; it's a lie!</p>
        <p>Prisoner said she had not tasted drink for three weeks, and had only had some three times</p>
        <p>: AFTE R • MEALS TAKE ;71 -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NOT RUB THAT</head>
        <p>NOT RUB THAT</p>
        <p>, , 3 I t : _ _ • - , • :._..... ..,.. ~. • V' ' . ' , 1,....t.„,, i ' • I • . .7- Ile . . 1 I ~- t_-, • V ~ i '.• .: .. _ _-.." - •- ' i , 7 ' ' • ' 1 , • ,sii. , , .. ,•.. /. ' • /\'''' ' J.,' j i j..., - ----- (f,:4__-,,,a, . , .. , W‘dii ...-</p>
        <p>''ktp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>ITCHY SPOT.</head>
        <p>ITCHY SPOT.</p>
        <p>WHEN your skin burns and tingles, and a small itching pimple or rash appears on the face, chest or elbows, the one</p>
        <p>necessary precaution is to apply to the affected parts some of that soothing anti- septic balm, Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>Irritation is then stopped, and the temptation to scratch and rub the itching places immedi- ately disappears. WARD OFF DISEASE,</p>
        <p>As the rich Zam-Buk sinks into the pores, the action of the latter is so stimulated that the impurities imprisoned in the skin find,a natural outlet instead of remaining to ferment in the tissues, and break out in Spots, Pimples, and Rashes, which are the beginnings of Eczema, Ulcers, and Blood-poison.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk should be kept handy in every home. It has eclipsed ordinary ointments and is un- equalled for cuts. bruises, sprains, pike. and skin sores. The 219 box contains nearly four Limes the 111 size. Of all Chemists. ZAM-BUK MEDICINAL SOAP is nodally recommended to all whose skint are sensitive. No7d i 3 c?lies., or 2/9 per box of: Wee enkcs.</p>
        <p>since she left the home. She could not sleep at the home, as she worried about her children. She did want to have the children.</p>
        <p>Inspector Wainwright (R.S.P.C.C.): She kept going to Mrs. Ball's for her child, using my name without authority.</p>
        <p>Mr. Laws (Police -Court missionary) said jealousy was the rocit of the whole matter. Prisoner: No, sir! The woman was put on probation for six months, and the question of the custody of the children was deferred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>QUICK WAY HOME.</head>
        <p>A QUICK WAY HOME.</p>
        <p>But Also A Dangerous One.</p>
        <p>Before the Borough magistrates on Tuesday. George Allen, a yOuth, of 12, Dutton-street, was summoned for exposing himself to danger, or risk of danger, bn the L. and N.-W. Railway near Wilderspool Crossing, on April 10th.—He pleaded guilty. Mr. Arthur Browne prosecuted on behalf of the company. On the Saturday of the cup-tie on the football ground, he said, defendant— along with a number of other boys—climbed over the fence after the match, and walked along the railway to Wilderspool-causeway. The other boys gave false names and addresses. The youths were caught by Detective Wisbey, to whom defendant made the excuse that that was a quick way to get home. Allen said there were a great many spectators on the field, and he thought he would never get out by the ordinary way. The fine was 20s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>-Before Mr. S. Wright (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton, James, Evans and Smethurst, and Messrs. W. Furness, T. Locker and W. Roberts. Four " Drunk and Disorderlies."—Edward Burns, labourer, 42, Foundry-street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly on Saturday in the Bay Horse yard. P.C.'s' Ford and Harrison proved the case, and Burns \ —who said he was an Irishman—was fined ss. or seven days.—Richard Dagnall, forge-labourer, was fined 12s. or 14 days for a similar offence in Church-street, on Saturday. P.C. Cameron was responsible for the arrest.—James Rushforth, basketmaker, of 145, Knutsford-road, also` appeared for being drunk on the same day, in Knutsford-road. Detective-Sergeant Redding- ton and Detective Turner figured in the ease, and the defaulter had to pay 6s. or serve seven days.—For drunken and disorderly conduct (proved by P.C.'s Gibbs and Hoare) in Bridge- street, on Saturday, Thomas Keaveny, of 14, Alice-street, Sutton, St. Helens, was fined 6s. or seven days. Drunken Soldier.—Private William Mannion, of the sth Battalion King's Liverpool Regiment, was shown by P.C. E. Taylor to have been drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street on Satur- day.—Prisoner was remanded for 24 hours.</p>
        <p>1•'!</p>
        <p>t f I. • "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>RURAL BLAZE.</head>
        <p>A RURAL BLAZE.</p>
        <p>I lime Cottages Destroyed By Fire.</p>
        <p>MOTHER</p>
        <p>AND 7.</p>
        <p>BANM-''ov STOMI-Lt TROUBLESI</p>
        <p>Shortly after four o'cloek on Wednesday afternoon, news was received at the Warring- ton,Fire Station that a fire had broken out at Dutton Cottages, about seven miles from War- rington.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Turner at once dispatched the fire engine Major, in charge of Sergeant Cook and a full complement of men to the scene of the outbreak, and on arrival they found that three thatched cottages, occupied respectively by Messrs. Parker, Taylor and Lightfoot, were on fire. The thatches were well ablaze, and despite the combined efforts of the Warrington brigade and a number of willing workers with fire appliances from a neighbouring school, the cottages were totally destroyed. The oeCupants safely removed all their furni- ture, while Mr. Lightburn, who had been an invalid for years, was also removed to safety. The amount of damage is not known. Colonel Talbot, of Aston, is the owner of the cottages.</p>
        <p>1 ;;;*; 4 ' .F_ _</p>
      </div>
