      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Deaths.</head>
        <p>Deaths.</p>
        <p>CAMPRELL.—ApriI 27th, at 23, Grove-road. West Kirby, in- his 64th year, John Foulkes Campbell, formerly of 67, Marsh House-lane, Warrington. TALBOT.—On April 26th, at. 79, Hood-lan.e. Sankey. Jane, the beloved wife of William Talbot, aged 60. Deeply regretted. SHAW.—April 23, Ellen Shaw, 2, Marbury-street.</p>
        <p>aged 64 iears. BIT/MON.—April 24. Alice Burton. 27, Golborne- •street, aged 68 rears.</p>
        <p>Acknowledgment. MIJ. BARNES and MISS •BARNE§ 136, .:Church-street, Warrington, desire to THANK the many kind friends for their kind expressions of sympathy, also for floral tributes, in their recent sad; bereavement. and MRS. CLARE and MISS EDITH JUL CLARE beg to THANK their many friends who have forwarded to them expressions of sym- pathy and floral tributes during their recent sad bereavement. 23, Forster-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>FIINERALS by I. MOUNFIELD- &amp; SONS. The Best in all Grades. Office: 15, Church street. Tel. 99.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, MAY 1, 1915.</p>
        <p>LOCAL TORIES' GOSPEL OF HATE.</p>
        <p>It has been our misfortune several times since the war began to have to protest against the manner in which a section of the local Tory party attempt to violate the truce which was set up w between the political parties. Before the war we had grown accus- tomed to hear the gospel of hate preached in high quarters locally in the opposite camp; 'we profoundly regret that even the present storm and stress are unequal to-stemiting the 'flow of venom which exists and presumably must, whatever, the result, be kept in use. A fortnight ago we, published, and we de- lighted to 'have the opportiinity. of. doing so, bi intervieW Which Mrs. CROSFIELD, at the suggestion and invitation of a. local county magistrate, had with the captured German officers at Harlech. There was much interesting information in that interview, and many newspapers would have paid handsomely could they have had the privilege of placing it before their readers as " The,Examiner " did. No doubt our readers duly appreciated the, service Mrs. CROSFIELD had done them. The Warrington "Guardian" did not have the interview, and, maybe, envy had a hand in providing the sequel. • That Sequel. appeared in the " Guardian " last Saturday in a scandalously dishonest travesty of the interview, which took the form of a letter signed "Englishwoman." The letter was written, for a certain class, and to that class we do nott address ourselves, as their case is hopeless. Decent-minded Con- servatives and the few Liberals who may have read the letter we would confidently ask to compare it and the interview closely, and then say how much the writer oe the letter deserves the name of English—, be she man or woman; and we suggest that 'point is in doubt. The writer professes to have had her or his) blood upset—" boiled " is the word used—by the frivolous conversations at this interview, and by the fact of these German officers giving confident " winks " to their "charming, visitors "—that is Mrs. PROSFIELD and her sister. Again we have the gospel of hate introduced, for we are re- minded of what our prisoners are enduring in 'Germany, and we have the hint made that we ought to imitate the Germans by giving our hatred free play, instead of playing the English game and showing them the better way. Fortunately, we are not all made of the stuff .that goes to make up the baser kind .of Warrington Tory. We would sweat that the next time 'German prisoners are to be seen in this neighbourhood the proprietors of the " Guardian " should commission "An Englishwoman" to go to see them, and that instead of • speaking to them in German— which, of course, she (or he) would be unable to do—she (or he) should emulate the . Ger- mans and spit upon them, which would pe quite within her (or his) powers. The En 4.- lish Way, of course, is to, show the world how much we Can rise above the in7style, and this suggestion is only intended for the guidance of this particular and jaundiced- visioned "Englishwoman."</p>
        <p>The enlightened rigmarole finishes up with a snggestion that it would have been in better taste "had the ladies reserved their undoubted linguistics accomplishments and charm for, say, interviewing the many thousands of homeless Belgians in our Midst or for putting the case for the Allies before their Greek compatriots." The ignorance or the malice, or both, contained in this sentence is appalling—quite a Tariff Reform, trip-to- Ulster kind of ignorance, in fact. Mrs. Cnos- FIELD has interested herself in behalf of the .Belgians in quite a lavish way, and Mr. CROS- rrELD, acting in conjunction with his wife, has made the case of Greece peculiarly his own. But we suppose "Englishwoman" never reads anything but the "Guardian"—and thereby</p>
        <p>hangs a tale. It perhaps explains why she (or he) gets leaded type for her (or his) letter which answers an interview in a rival news- paper, which is not quoted properly, but mal- treated dishonestly in ,order to make a petty, ungenerous, un-English point against a lady who stands as far above her traducers as they stand from honour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Welsh Chapel Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Welsh Chapel Anniversary.</p>
        <p>Anniversary services will be held in the Welsh Chapel, Crosfield-street, to-day and to- morrow. The special preacher for to-day is the Rev. T. J. Rowlands, of Birkenhead, whilst to- morrow Rev. W. Wynn Davies, of Rhos, will preach. All the services save that on Sunday afternoon will be in Welsh.</p>
        <p>Salvation Army "Field Days." Saturday and Sunday will be "field .days" in connection with the local Salvation Army Corps, Colonel Sowton, late Chief Secretary for India, having promised to pay their head- quarters in Academy-street a visit. He will give an address on Saturday evening, will preach twice on Sunday, and will lecture on Sunday afternoon on "India; its people and customs and Salvation Army work there," Shorthand Successes.</p>
        <p>The following students of Wood's College, Warrington, have been successful in passing the National Shorthand Association examination:— Theory: Winnifred Bennett, May Heath, Marion Howard, Winnitred Rylands, Gertrude Brad- ley and Cissie Charnock. Advanced theory: Marion Twiss, Elizabeth Billson and Ethel Wallington. Sixty words per minute: Cissie Penketh, Ethel Wallington, Reginald Gallop</p>
        <p>Fanny Martin, Elizabeth Billson, Florence Mal. kin, Helen Wallington, Marion Twiss (with dis- tinction), and William Roughley. Seventy words per minute: Fred Shelley, Lilian Quarrie and Marjorie Mather. Eighty words per min- ute: Marjorie Smith, Gilbert Cook, Clarice Dixon, Francis Davenport, May Mather (with distinction), and Jean Bunten (with distinc- tion). Ninety words per miniite : G. Cook, Annie Haslerii, Mabel Isaac and Annie Darling- ton. One hundred words per minute: D. Howard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Funeral of the Late Mrs. Nladeley.</head>
        <p>Funeral of the Late Mrs. Nladeley.</p>
        <p>Many relp.tives and friends, attended the funeral of the late Mrs. Madeley (wife of Mr. Charles Madeley, the respected director and librarian of the Museum), which took place at the Cemetery on Saturday, the service being conducted by the Revs. J. Ashby (of Southport) and Irvine Lister (of Cairo-street Unitarian Church, Warrington). The ~hief moirrners were: The bereaved husband, Private Benton Madeley,'l9th King's Liverpool Regiment (son); Mr. Robert Pemberton (son-in-law), Mr. I. S. Hill and Mr. James Ward. Others present in- cluded Mr. F. W. Monks, Mr. L. Greening, Alderman Bennett, Mr. G. A. Dunlop, Mr. 3. McAdam and Mr. H. S. Page. Floral tributes were sent by the following: Mr. Madeley, Mr. B. Madeley, Mr. and Mrs. R. Pemberton, Mrs. Holme (Upton Grey), Mr. and Mrs. A. S. Wain- wright (01ton); " Derek" Wainwright, Mr. and Mrs. Roland Stone (Hampstead), Major and Mrs. Geoghegan (Basingstoke), Miss Stone (Streetly), Mrs. Bill and Mrs. Milner, Mrs. and Miss Pemberton, Miss Gillaird (Altrincha,th), Mr. and Mrs. Hill and family, Mr. and Mrs. H. Bond and family, Mr. and Mrs. Monks, Mr. and Mrs. Greening, Mrs. and Miss Kirby, Miss Houghton, Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Woodcock, Mr. and Mrs. H. S. Page, Mr. and Mrs. J. F. Dutton, Mr. and Miss Perris. Mr. and Mrs. Noble, Mr. and Mrs. J. Ward (Leigh), Gertrude Ratcliffe, the Ladies' Sewing Society, Cairo-street Chapel; the Mothers' Meeting, Cairo-street Chapel; the Staff of Warrington Museum, and the Warring- ton and District Local Government Officers' Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>WALKING DAY.</head>
        <p>WALKING DAY.</p>
        <p>Should It Be Abandoned This</p>
        <p>Year?</p>
        <p>MMENI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>SUGGESTED COMPROMISE.</head>
        <p>SUGGESTED COMPROMISE.</p>
        <p>Town Hall, Warrington, 28th April, 1915. I beg to inform you that represen- tations have been made to me by some of the large Works in the town as to the advisability of postponing "Walking Day" for this year on • the ground of the loss of time and the interference with Works, a large number of which are busily engaged on Government Contracts. I presume that owing to all railway facilities and cheap trains being withheld by Railway Companies, no "Workmen's trips will take place 4?. • and consequently - there will no necessity for a two days' holiday as in • previous years. If the workmen would keep to their work on "Walking Day," I see no reason why the children should not have their usual holiday; but it is doubtful whether the men could be expected to work under the circum- stances.</p>
        <p>It has occurred to me that it might be possible to have " Walking Day" fixed for August Bank Holiday. Perhaps you will let me have your views on the whole matter, after which I propose to call a meeting at an early date. Yours faithfully, PETER PEACOCK, Mayor.</p>
        <p>The above letter has been addressed to the people concerned by his Worship the Mayor. In ordinary times, the suggestion to interfere with our annual holiday in any way would have been resented. Even the comings and goings of kings have not been allowed to modify our arrangements for the children's fete, which has been held locally so long that it has conic to be looked upon as one of the unalterable fixtures.</p>
        <p>But, while agreeing that Walking Day is a thing apart, both in tradition and object, there are special reasons why this year very serious thought should be given to the suggestions of those for whom the Mayor has been made the ' spokesman as civic head of the borough. There is the initial objection, which has been urged on previous occasions, that Walking DO is not the concern either of the town or the ~ works. It is a Sunday school question almost. - entirely—the works' holiday and the town ' supervision being, of course, necessary accom- paniments to its smooth and successful work- ing. We may • take it that these contributing parties to the success of the festival will, when called together by the Mayor, discuss the ques- tion solely in the light of present conditions. We may take it that the suggestion of &amp;ban' doning Walking Day altogether is out of the question. It would be a disappointment to tho children and a loss to the tradesmen. Tile treat in one form or another must take place, and it may be assumed that, as a last resort. each school will have its treat upon individual or denominational lines. It seems likely, fact, that this is the utmost that could be done 1 in any case, because bands will not be avail- -1 able for the larger combined demonstration t° which we are accustomed. Tradesmen who 1005. forward to one of their few harvests at the, time and the children who can count, at to' events, upon this one occasion for the acquisi- I tion of a new set of clothes, will welcome OW • kind Of a compromise. There are, we understand, some firms Ini,t engaged on war work who think their peoPl should have this holiday—in fact, it is arrange' for in the statutory holidays for the year. Ile" sides, many of those who take part in 01° procession are adults at work, and Walking, Day without them would not be Walking Da' ' This argument would appear to be fatal 0 the Mayor's suggestion that the childre4 should have the holiday and the works Ghoul go on as us.ual. There is an equally stroll objection to the holding of Walking Day August Bank Holiday. The day school sullici- mer holidays will then be in full swing many teachers and scholars will be away fo' home. From these few notes it will be seen complicated is the question that has to settled at the meeting to be called by jj Mayor. Doubtless the school authorities iclij meet, and the works' managements will consider the problem in advance. We unlie stand that clergy and superintendents of Church of England will meet to consider 1-1" matter in Patten Hall on Tuesday evening'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>G and elothinq Mall The Boys' Outfitters.</head>
        <p>G and elothinq Mall The Boys' Outfitters.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CAMP OF PEACE.</p>
        <p>We are informed that the poor school children of Warrington will be found en- camped at Prestatyn as usual during the summer holidays—unless conditions materi- ally alter or unforeseen difficulties arise. Con- ditions, so far as the war is concerned, will not, we hope, materially alter for the worse, and we may take it for granted that no diffi- culties as regards finances will intervene. Travelling will cost More and the price of pro- visions will have ascended in sympathy, as compared with last year, but already the com- mittee have been generously met by some of the subscribers Who have doubled their sub- scriptions: These thoughtful people have earned the deep gratitude of the committee, who hope their example will be widely copied, and that new subscribers may also come tc help foEward a deserving object.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>GERMANY'S INDELIBLE STAIN.</head>
        <p>GERMANY'S INDELIBLE STAIN.</p>
        <p>When LORD DEIt 1: vi,ited • Warrington to help forward the scheme for raising a Lanca- shire Artillery Division he said that when the -people of this Country knew openly what he knew privately of the barbarous treatment that had been meted out to British prisoners in Germany there would ,not be a man amongst us who would not ardently desire to kill a German before he died. It was a strong and out-of-the-way method of expressing one's feelings; but LORD DERBY weighs his words and would not have. 80 spoken without the best of reasons. This' week we have had the question discussed in Parliament. LORD KrreuENER, who, like the rest of us, ,had hoped that exaggeration and irresponsible rumour would account for much of the hor- rible story that has been in circulation, has. proof now that inhumanity has been prac: tised on a large scale towards our men who have been unlucky enough to get into the clutches of these professors• and disciples of the, thing called Kultur. It is to be feared that anything we might do by way of pro- . , . test would only be So Much waste 'energy Upon. our unworthy opponents; but we are delighted with the •way LORD KITCHE.,NER has put' the offenders; in the pillory for all the world, and, what is more, for future generations, to gaze upon. To have earned the ill-will of present- day civilisation and a black place in the , blackest page of history for all time will have proved the present war to have been a re- markably unprofitable undertaking for a nation that was thought to be in the van of progress. The words in which LORD KITCHENER condemned the modern Atilla and his Runs were few,, but they were of burning fire. They are here reproduced in their brief simplicity:— Germany has for many years posed before the civilised .world as a great military nation. She has abundantly proved her military • skill and courage, but, surely, it was also for her to set up a standard of military honour and conduct which would gain the respect, if not the friendship, of nations. Instead she has stooped to acts which will surely stain indelibly her mili- tary history, and which would vie with the barbarous savagery of the Dervishes of the Soudan. Ido not think there Can be a soldier of any nationality, even amongst the Germans themselves, who is not heartily ashamed of the slur which has been thus brought upon the profession of arms. The profession of arms, as a whole, will now feel the, slur that has thus been cast upon it; but upon the unspeakable German,' who has taken the unspeakable Turk into partnership and robbed him of his special attributes, the stain twill permanently remain. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>POISONERS.</head>
        <p>THE POISONERS.</p>
        <p>It is clear we have no right to expect the Germans to act in any way different from that in which they are acting. Men who will throw innocent children upon the flames will not hesitate to torture men who have been actively opposed to them, and perhaps given them a little more trouble than they expected. Last week-end they provided us with more evidence as to the lengths to which they are prepared to go. LORD Kncarszn having dis- posed of the prison brutalities continued The usages of war have not only been out- raged by the infliction of cruelties on Brit- ish prisoners, but by a contrivance which must have arrested your lordships' atten- tion. The Germans have, in the last week, introduced a method of placing their oppo- nents hors de combat by the use of asphyxiating and deleterious gases, and they employ these poisonous methods to prevail when their attack, according to the rules of war, might have otherwise failed. On this subject I would remind your lordships that Germany was a signatory to the following article in The Hague Convention The contracting Powers agree to abstain from the use of projectiles the object of which is the diffusion of asphyxiating or deleterious</p>
        <p>gases."</p>
        <p>Of course, the Hague Convention was merely a "scrap of paper." But LORD KITCHENER'S biting suggestion that the Germans resorted to poison because they were failing to win by the regular civilised methods is calculated to penetrate even the ,thick hides of the blonde beasts of Europe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>REPARATION.</head>
        <p>REPARATION.</p>
        <p>• There is a great and ever-growing list of German 'outrages against humanity and inter- national law that calls for punishment, now and hereafter- In the review by Mr. PARKER of a book on the wrongs of Belgium, which we give in to-day's "Examiner," theie is a pitiful story of the way in which the Gerns maltreated the civil population of a country that had done them no harm, but against which they themselves were grievously sin- ning. The author of the book, "A Surgeon in Belgium," Dr. &amp;UTTAR, describing what</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>TH-Fi EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</head>
        <p>TH-Fi EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</p>
        <p>he saw on the road from Antwerp by the aid of his motor lights, says:— As darkness came down we lit our lamps, and there along the roadside sat rows of fugitives, resting before conimeneing their • , long journey through the night. There .was one row of little children which will live for ever in my memory, tiny mites sitting together on a bank by the roadside. We only saw them for an instant as our lights fell on them, and they disappeared in the darkness. Germany will have to pay for Louvain and Termonde. It is not with man that she will have to settle for that row of little children.</p>
        <p>But there are some things for which .Ger- many will have to settle with man. It is said there is no authority to enforce the require- ments of international law, but it will not be altogether impossible for the nations to set up Courts to carry out their ideas of right. There is reason ,to believe that some such course is in contemplation, for, replying to Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, who urged that when the conditions of peace are settled, "satisfaction should be given to outraged humanity for the cruelties perpetrated on defenceless men," the PRIME Ml:cis-nit gave an emphatic and</p>
        <p>deliberate pledge, which was strictly in accord with the unanimous sentiment of the House. Mr. AsQurrit said:</p>
        <p>When we come to an end of this war, as please God we may, we shall not forget, and we ought not to forget, this horrible record of calculated cruelty and crime, ,and we shall hold it to be our duty to exact such reparation against those who are proved to be guilty agents and actors as it may be possible for us to do. Those who are responsible for the atrocities that have disgraced this war are known with a tolerable degree of certainty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>INTERVIEW 1%1111 GERMAN</head>
        <p>THE INTERVIEW 1%1111 GERMAN</p>
        <p>PRISONERS.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "Th. Ex.aminet."</p>
        <p>- - Sir,—lt has been somewhere remarked that in former times some false alarms usually preceded or accompanied a new war. If it were possible to know the origin of the letter signed " Englishwoman " (which ap- peared in your contemporary last Saturday) one would doubtless find that the writer had not so much in mind "to protest very strongly against the visiting of German pris- oners by English ladies (or by ladies of a foreign and only partially friendly country)," as a very keen desire—in a most unladylike way—to hurt the feelings of, and to en- deavour to cast a slur upon the fair name of a lady who has endeared herself to very many people in Warrington and who happens to be —and here's the rub—the wife of the prospec- tive Parliamentary candidate at the !next election. I venture to suggest that the lady contributor's blood would not have "boiled" save for that fact. Of all mean subterfuges, there surely can be nothing to equal such un-English methods of party political warfare. • The Germans have proved themselves to be pastmasters in the art of doing dirty work, and are despised accordingly. There is no reason—for party political purposes even- why their own idea of " kultur should be emulated by an "Englishwoman."—Yours truly, • ,• EMMA C. OSBORNE. 22; Bold-street, -Warrington, 30th April, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WARRINGTON PROSPEROUS. As Proved By Rate Collector's</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PROSPEROUS. As Proved By Rate Collector's</p>
        <p>Report.</p>
        <p>The Borough Collector submitted the follow- ing half-yearly statement showing the amount of the respective rates unrecoverable, to a recent meeting of the Finance and Estate Committee The abnormal cpnditions existing during the period covered by the half-year, October Ist to March 31st, might have suggested the possi- bility of the collection of the rates being any- thing but satisfactory, but I am glad to report that the results have not proved this view to be justified. It will be more convenient if I deal with the collection as a whole, and for this purpose I shall include the poor rate in the figures.</p>
        <p>s. d. Total amount collected Composition allowed Allowances for empty property 843 14 1 Allowances for assessments reduced 295 2 0 Excusal of poor rate, on account of poverty, by the magistrates and overseers Excusal of improvement rate. sewers rate and water rate, by the Finance Committee Allowances for demolished pro-</p>
        <p>18 310</p>
        <p>10 14 3</p>
        <p>perty, &amp;c. Loss in collection</p>
        <p>8913 2659</p>
        <p>--- Recoverable arrears It is interesting to note that only 10 sum- monses were issued as against an average of 21 during the last three years. These results testify to the continued pros- perity of the town, and the fairly even distribu- tion of business among all sections of the com- munity. It was resolved that the Collector be author- ised to discharge as irrecoverable the sums of £lO 14s. 3d. excu.sals, and £26 ss. 9d. "less in collection, referred to in the above report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WAR ITEMS.</head>
        <p>WAR ITEMS.</p>
        <p>FOR SEA SERVICE. Mr. James Monk has resigned his position as a temporary clerk in the Warrington Education Offices and has joined the Royal Navy.</p>
        <p>" TERRI ERS' " THANKS.</p>
        <p>Colonel Fairclough, writing from the Front, thanks the Town Clerk for having sent his men 60,000 cigarettes from the local tobacco fund, which were very welcome. The Colonel says the "trenches are now considerably stronger and safer than they were."</p>
        <p>WOUNDED "TERRIER'S" CONNECTION</p>
        <p>WITH WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Private Henry Cadman, of Lytam—f all par- tielars of whose wounding whilst serving at the Front with the sth Cheshires,, we reported last week—is a brother of Mrs. James Gilbert, 51, Pierpoint-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>LORD DERBY'S APPEAL FOR TERRITORIALS. •</head>
        <p>LORD DERBY'S APPEAL FOR TERRITORIALS. •</p>
        <p>The splendid work done by our Territorials at the Front may be fittingly called to mind at the present moment, when the Earl of Derby is issuing an appeal for additional Territorials for West Lancashire Regiments. This division comprises twelve battalions, of which nine already are doing their duty in Flanders. In order that they may be maintained at full strength an efficient supply of reserves is essential, and it is to this end that Lord Derby's appeal is directed. Lancashire men's participation in the fighting has redounded to the credit of the county, and it is the feeling that her young men will uphold that credit that gives Lord Derby confidence that his present appeal will not be in vain. Particulars as to the recruiting stations are given in our adver- tising columns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>PROBLEMS OF THE POOR.</head>
        <p>PROBLEMS OF THE POOR.</p>
        <p>Advance in Food Prices And Increasing Rents. SOME THOUGHTS ON PRESENT-DAY CONDITIONS</p>
        <p>It may be stated as a fact that the British public was never more charitably disposed than it is at present. Rich man, poor man, and the man who is neither rich nor poor, all seem itchingly anxious to give of their time and money to some deserving cause. _ Even the individual who greedily grabs his _ .</p>
        <p>huge profits because of the inflation:of prices smiles beneficently as he writes a Cheque for £5O towards the relief fund, and dainty MisS Delicacy, on reading of some millionaire's donation of £2O, cries out in an ecstasy, "How awfully good r? At every increase in price, whether it be in food, taxes, or rates, -ardent .leader writers complacently remark, "But the British .public will cheerfully bear the extra cost " ; and the man with the private income adds the valuable thought, "Work's plentiful." And so it is that with our minds centred</p>
        <p>upon the war-, with our energies concentrated upon patriotic activities, arid with our reason partially clouded by platitudes, we fail to</p>
        <p>recognise the terrible laet-thut in every case of strain there is a . breaking point, a last straw that will cause a collapse. '</p>
        <p>Poverty in Warrington. An interesting article in " The Examiner" a few weeks ago revealed a state of affairs which should make Warringtonians think somewhat. In certain wards in ,the -town poverty is found in the proportion of one family in six. This was in peace time. How do these people *exist? Their wages are insufficient to support a family even on bare necessaries. Moreover, drink is not the cause of poverty in very many cases. Twenty- eight shillings spread out over seven days for father, mother and five youngsters does not run to luxuries. Any comfortably circum- stanced housewife can prove this by experi- ment. And twenty-eight shillings is "good money," mark you. The poor soul whose duty it is to make a • sovereign spin out for, rent, food, fuel and other necessities is a greater financier than any Chancellor of the ETPhequer that Eng- land ever produced, if she succeeds in doing it.</p>
        <p>Moreover the food of the poor is purchased in small quantities, and hence the price is up from .20 per cent. to 50 per cent. Of cot, rse, such people have to find other means of. raising money. The mother must go out to work. The children must earn their bit as nurse girls, errand runners, "dinner takers," newsboys, old iron gatherers and parcel carriers.</p>
        <p>A Chance for Councillors. In times of crisis recourse is often made to money-lenders. What this means only the poor housewife knows, who plods wearily each week to pay her interest without dimin- ishing the principal by a farthing. Why some great public organisation does not expose the vampires who prey upon ignorant, innocent housewives, has puzzled a multitude. The large-hearted town councilors who pose as the friends of working men have a wide field here.</p>
        <p>Life for the poor in peace time is a struggle. What about life . to-day? If before August, 1914, the sovereign was worth sixteen shillings, it is not worth fourteen to-day. In fact, if one states that the cost of living has risen 20 per cent., the estimate is not far wrong. And note that the increase is in necessities.. Bread cannot' be termed a luxury. . Tea, -sugar,- meat, coal—aye, and rent, are -every .one necessities. Even gas, which is doled out to the poor man at extor- tionate -prices just because he pays ready money, has risen. Of course, rents must 'rise. No one would deny the landlord of his profits. Such.charit- able institutions as Property Owners' Associa- tions must be supported "as usual." tiouses are scarce. The people cannot pos- sibly "flit." The conclusion is therefore clear. Put the rent Up. Loyal men! What then must the poor working class folk do?</p>
        <p>Overcrowded and Underfed. A sad story came before the writer's notice only this week. Two brave Englishmen, both too old for military service, lived with their families in the same street. Rents go up, food goes up, coal goes up, everything goes up except, of course, wages. Life before the war was an existence. Now it is a nightmare. Empty bellies must be fed. ,Half-clad nippers must be clothed for school. How to do it is another question. What is the solution? • These two men go into partnership. To put it plainly, they joined together. Now five adults, six children (and another baby soon) all " pig " together in three bedrooms. Work out how you would arrange two hus- bands, two wives, a son age nineteen, and boys and girls whose ages range from eleven years to eleven months, in three rooms. Remember, too, that on poor wages, in a small house in a back street, you must main- tain decency of mind, body and soul, and rear a healthy stock to replace our fallen heroes. Stop your ears and say" I don't believe it." It will ease your conscience, possibly—but it's true.</p>
        <p>_ Pick up your paper and read the account -of the annual report of Messrs. Spiller and Baker, Ltd., millers and flour, merchants. "The profit for the year ended February was £367,865, against £89,351 in the previous year." Then think of those lines of Tom Hood in his "Song of the Shirt " : 0 God, that bread should be so dear, And flesh and blood so cheap!</p>
        <p>THE CALL TO ARMS.</p>
        <p>Amazed was -Peaceful England at the call to</p>
        <p>battle-roar, When—at the Kaiser's mad command—towards</p>
        <p>our silent shore The Teutons men and armaments in millions did forge, Till gallant little Belgium .checked up their</p>
        <p>savage hordes. The "cultured" barbarous monarch, set out on ruthless gain, Expected us to leave him to realise that aim: What did HE care for honour or pledges to his</p>
        <p>name? He wished to vanquish England, and sink her head in shame.</p>
        <p>Now rouse ye,, Sons of Britain! Gird on your armour new; ' Stand, worthy of your title, against this brutal The spirit of .you fathers have ,slept within ,your breast ;- Arise and strike for God and Right in this tremendous test! Keep bright the glistening 'scutcheon of Victory's noble deed. Protecting those now weakened by Wilhelm's curse of greed. Remember, lads, your parents, wives, sisters, o'er the foam Show all those Prussian nobles the Cult of English home. And when at length the fight is won by comrades , tried and true In martial pride march side by side to those who wait for you; ' Our banner fluttering in the breeze, and clashing arms laid by, With grateful memories of the dead dry every tearful eye. ELEANOR A. BRIDGWATER. Sylfaen, Stockton Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>New Sehoolke,eper.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hugh Hughes has been appointed school- keeper of the Silver-street School in place of Mr. A. E. Dean, resigned.</p>
        <p>Women Liberals Draw.</p>
        <p>The draw for the Women Liberals' Sewing Fund realised £lO 4s. 7d. The doll given by Mrs. Bunch was won by Mrs. Corcoran, 14, Ellesmere-street, Warrington (No. 2,874).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>NEWS FROM AND TO</head>
        <p>NEWS FROM AND TO</p>
        <p>THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>Now that so many Warrington men are at the Front, the closest possible ties connect the Town with the Con- tinent; there is a mutual interest, on a scale never before experienced, between those who have gone away and these left behind. The Town longs for news from the Trenches; the Trenches pine for tid- ings from home. We ask our readers to help us to meet this craving for news on both sides. Those who have friends at the Front would be doing a great public service if they would communicate to "The Examiner" the parts of the letters they receive from them, or concerning them, which may be made public, including ,early intimations. of casualties, whether killed, wounded, or missing. It is of first importance that the Town should have accurate and early information.</p>
        <p>As to the men in the Trenches, we suggest that our readers should, when they have finished with their "Ex- aminer," post it to a friend or relative on active service.</p>
        <p>Or we shall be glad to receive orders to forward "The Examiner" by first post after publication to men at the Front for any period at the rate of 2d. a copy, including postage.</p>
        <p>This notice applies with equal force to men who are serving their country in other parts of the world on land or sea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Nearing the 76,000.</head>
        <p>Nearing the 76,000.</p>
        <p>In his report for the week ending April 24th the Medical Officer of Health (Dr. (I. W. N. Joseph) estimates the population of he town at 75,532.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Fewer Babies.</head>
        <p>Fewer Babies.</p>
        <p>During the week ended April 24th 36 births and 26 deaths!Were registered in Warrington,, as against 54) births and 28 deaths in the cor- responding period of last year. Of the babies born during the period under review 17 were males and 18 females.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Dangerous Ages.</head>
        <p>The Dangerous Ages.</p>
        <p>During the week ended April 2,ith eight War- ringtonians between the ages of 25 and 65 died, whilst other deaths registered included four of babies under the ages of one year and an- other four children between the ages of one and five. Six of the deaths recorded were those of persons over the age of 65.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Scarlet Fever Epidemic Dying Out.</head>
        <p>Scarlet Fever Epidemic Dying Out.</p>
        <p>From the fact that last week no fresh cases of scarlet fever were reported, added to the fact that the week before the number of new patients was very small, it may be deduced that the scarlet fever epidemic, which has been with the town since the back-end of last summer, has spent its force and is gradually dying out. On Saturday last week there were 76 scarlet fever patients in the Borough hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Barnardo's Homes : Young Helpers' League.</head>
        <p>Barnardo's Homes : Young Helpers' League.</p>
        <p>The annual box opening in connection with the above has resulted in £2O ss. 9d. being raised for the Hordes in . the following amounts :—Collected in the boxes, £l2 3s. 9d.; 'subscriptions, 178.; annual donation of the Warrington Co-operative Society, £5 ss. Silver badges have been awarded to Kathleen Davies and Andrew Ker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Promotion for Bank Clerk.</head>
        <p>Promotion for Bank Clerk.</p>
        <p>The news that Mr. James Gauld -1,1;ho, for fifteen years, has been chief clerk at the Lanca- shire and Yorkshire Bank, Warrington, has been promoted to the management of, the Darwen Branch of the same Bank; will be received locally with mingled feelings. Mr. Gauld was one of the pleasantest and most capable of bank clerks, and his many friends while wishing him every success at Darwen, will regret the passing Of an agreeable person- ality that had become quite a part of their business—and, in some cases, private—lives. Mr. J. S. Richards, from Manchester, has been appointed chief clerk at Warrington. For some years he has been in the inspector's department at the head office, and has there acquired a very intimate knowledge of banking matters, His knowledge and urbanity make him a fit successor to Mr. Gauld.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Weather..</head>
        <p>The Weather..</p>
        <p>The weather this week has picked up splen- didly, spring bursting upon us with all her charm on Monday and causing something like a rush on the shops where the more sum- mery wear is sold, though we have, up to the time of writing, looked in vain for the first masculine straw hat. But its advent in the streets cannot be much longer delayed, for already they repose in the shop windows, most alluring displayed. Last week, says the Medi- cal Officer of Health's report, the mean tem- perature was 46, or one. degree higher than in the preceding week, but the glass will have a. much bigger jump to record this week. The warmest day during the week ending April 24th was the 18th inst., when the glass touched 58 degrees, whilst the coldest day during the same period was the 20th, when only 33 degrees were recorded. The rainfall last week was again very slight, only .14 of an inch being recorded. The general direction of the wind was W.N.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Parr's Bank Changes.</head>
        <p>Parr's Bank Changes.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the directors of Parr's Bank, Limited, on Thursday, Mr. R. W. Whalley (director and general manager) was elected 'deputy Chairman, in succession ta Mr. A. J. Fraser who, owing to advancing years, retires fFom that position. Mr. Fraser will retain his seat on the Board, and the Bank will thus continue tO have the benefit of his long and mature experience in the city. Mr. Whalley is, to the very great regret of the Board, retiring from the position of general manager, but will continue to .take an active interest in the conduct of the business of the Bank. Mr. John Stewart, deputy general manager, and Mr. John Rae, assistant general manager, who have been for many years closely associated with Mr. Whalley, have been appointed joint general managers from the Ist May.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. W. Ingall, London manager, has been appointed assistant general manager.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Young Abstainers' Union.</head>
        <p>Young Abstainers' Union.</p>
        <p>The members of the youngest Warrington Temperance Society—the local branch :of the •Y.A.U.—had a, very enjoyable picnic. at Lymm on Wednesday. The Clerk of the Weather was in' his very best humour. and those members who decided to make the journey on their cycles had the advantage over those who went by train. On arriving at Lymm the members inspected the beautiful old church and enjoyed the magnificent view across the Dam. After— wards the party visited the lovely 'building. sites laid out by Mr. W. U. Lever. They were greatly pleased with the boulevards and the stately bridge across the Danl. On return- ing from their ramble the party were enter- tained to tea by the Hon. Secretary of the union (Mrs. S. Griffiths), who had been most fortunate in securing the assistance of Mrs. Edwards, this lady ha--ing very kindly under- taken the work of preparing the refreshments. After tea games were the order of the day, and all too soon the time of departure arrived. The young people returned to their homes safely, but will long remember their visit to one of Cheshire's most picturesque villages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Arpley-street School.</head>
        <p>The Arpley-street School.</p>
        <p>The managers of the Arpley-street School have written to the Education Committee intimating that the committee's suggestion that an infants' department might be established at the school cannot be entertained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Licence Transferred.</head>
        <p>A Licence Transferred.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Thursday, morning, Jno. Henry Joyner was granted tem- porary authority to sell beer, (ice., at the off- licensed premises in Aiken-street, formerly kept by Sarah Skelton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>For the Sick and Poor.</head>
        <p>For the Sick and Poor.</p>
        <p>A rummage sale was held in ,the St. George's Mission on Saturday, in aid of the Sick and Poor Fund. As a result, the sum of about £7 was raised for the fund, which is at present badly in need of support.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Sunday School. Anniversary services in connection with .the Latchford Baptist Sunday School will be held to-morrow, when the preacher morning and evening will be Mr. A. E. Hoyle. In the after- noon the Sunday school choir will perform a sacred cantata, "The Choicest Gift."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Will of Late Mr. Wynne-Jones.</head>
        <p>Will of Late Mr. Wynne-Jones.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Jorwerth Wynne-Jones, of The Grove, Moore, Cheshire, brewery director—third son of the late Archdeacon Wynne-Jones, of Treiorwerth, Anglesey, and a brother of the Rev. J. W. Wynne-Jones, vicar of Carnarvon; commandant of the special constables, and associated with many local public bodies—left net personalty nil and £1,046 gross.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Shorthand Successes.</head>
        <p>Shorthand Successes.</p>
        <p>The following pupils of Mr. J. Marsh, A.B.T.' of 42, Chester New-road, Warrington, have been :awarded the National. Shorthand Association Certificate, after ex- amination:--Eighty words per minute: Miss G. Allanson, Mr. L. G. Lythgoe (with distinction). Theory: Mr. J. E. Thomasson. Elementary: Miss P. Parsonage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Boys' Brigade Enrolment Service.</head>
        <p>Boys' Brigade Enrolment Service.</p>
        <p>• An enrolment service in connection with the Ist Warrington Company of the Boys' Brigade will be held in the Wycbile Congregational Church, Bewsey-street, on Siinday morning, Eft 10.30. In the evening a special service of inter- cession in time of war will be held, when the Rev. T. T. James will preach on " God and the Nation."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Run Over By A Motor Car.</head>
        <p>Run Over By A Motor Car.</p>
        <p>While crossing Knutsford-road, Grappenhail, on Wednesday, a boy named Albert Plant was run over by a motor car, and had his right leg broken. Dr. Robinson was called, .and ordered the lad's removal to the Warrington Infirmary. The scene of the accident is a very dangerous one, and about two years ago a gentleman was killed there whilst crossing the road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Wheat Prices !Wain Advance.</head>
        <p>Wheat Prices !Wain Advance.</p>
        <p>Wheat again showed a.slight advance at War- rington market on Wednesday. Supplies were scarce, and there was a difference of Is. per sack on the week, with the result that the all- important grain is now as dear as it was at the commencement of the war. Oats were firmer, fetching from 4s. 7d. to 4s. Bd. per bushel of 151 b. Straw was better, and sold at 355. to 375. 6d. per ton. XX Flour fetched 495. 6d. per 2801 b., B bran Is. Sid. per 201 b., and eggs 10 for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Death of 'Airs. Shaw.</head>
        <p>Death of 'Airs. Shaw.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death of Mrs. Harriet Shaw, the wife of Gouncillor James Shaw, who passed away at her residence, Lythgoe's-lane, yesterday. The cause of death was pneumonia. Mrs. Shaw was in her 65th year and her loss will be keenly felt by a wide circle of friends. The funeral takes place on Monday neat. There will be a service at the Brick-street Mission Church at 2.30, and the interment will take plate at the Cemetery at three o'clock.</p>
        <p>Teaching the Young Idea to—Swim.</p>
        <p>Miss Gertrude J. Beresford and Miss Ethel M. Beresford have been appointed to carry out jointly the duties of ,swimming instructress to the girls from the public elementary schools in the borough, at a total inclusive fee of £lB 18s. for the session. On account of the extension of the session, the salary of Mr. Joseph Peers, swimming instructor to the boys, is, so the Education Committee decided on Monday night, to be increased from £3O to £35 for the session.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>In Memory of the Fallen.</head>
        <p>In Memory of the Fallen.</p>
        <p>At St. John's Presbyterian Church of Eng- land on Sunday evening, a public service will be held commemorative of the men who, in the great European conflict, have laid down their lives for their native land. To this service a hearty welcome is extended to the public gener- ally and to any soldiers who are in the neigh- bourhood. His Worship the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) will be in attendance, and will take part in the. service. A special offertory will be taken on 'behalf of the local war relief fund.</p>
        <p>Medical Inspection of School Children.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee, held at the Town Hall on Monday evening, the Medical Inspection Sub-committee reported that a grant amount- ing to £363 Is. 9d. had been received from the Board of Education in respect of the medical inspection and treatment of school children in the borough.—ln moving the adoption of the sub-committee's report, Mr. C. E. Parker said that the grant amounted to half the expendi- ture on medical inspection work and was, he understood. the highest grant that could be obtained.—Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes seconded, and the report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Buckley-street Free Gospel Church.</head>
        <p>Buckley-street Free Gospel Church.</p>
        <p>The Sunday school anniversary wag cele- brated at the Buckley-street Free Gospel Church on Sunday, when special sermons were preached in the morning and evening by Mr. J. W. Cannon, of Leeds. Mr. Cannon also gave a brief address at a special service in the after- noon, when the children, assisted by the choir, sang a number of suitable hymns, and Miss May Hockenhull gave a finished rendition of the well-known sacred solo "The Holy City." The services throughout the day were well attended, and the collections totalled CT 12s. 6d. On Monday night Mr. Cannon gave an interesting lecture entitled." In Everybodies Mouth; or the Doings of the Dreadful Red Dwarf."</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Sunday Schoo'</p>
        <p>The Sunday school anniversary in connection with the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church will be held to-morrow, when the special preacher in the morning and evening will be the Rev. J. Grange Bennett. In the afternoon a musical service will take place, when Mr. John Taylor will the the chair, and the speaker will be Mr. John Upson. The school superin- tendents make a special appeal this year to the members of the congregation and old Sunday school scholars to provide them with the sum of £5O. They have •over 500 scholars and teachers on the books, 40 of whom are now serving their ' country. "Help us" says the superintendents in their printed appeal "to keep the flag flying until they return. They will appreciate your practical sympathy and help in their 'absence."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>TERRITORIAL ROLL OF HONOUR.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIAL ROLL OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>Officer Wounded;</p>
        <p>Three Men Killed and Eight Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>GROWING LIST.</head>
        <p>A GROWING LIST.</p>
        <p>This week's casualties have . added twelve nikanles to the Roll of Honour of the local Territorials—Killed, 3; Wounded, 9. Th ey are :</p>
        <p>KILLED. Private J. G. Kenwright Private J. Dagnall. Private E. Williamson.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED. Second-Lieutenant A. H. Hatton. Corporal L. Atherton. Private J. H. McAvoy.</p>
        <p>Private A. Jackson. Private S. Miller. • Private H. Kinsey. Private J. Kinsey (name does not agree with official roll). Private W. Jenks. Drummer J. Halford. These figures bring the totals to date to:— Killed • Wounded 70 Sick (about) 117</p>
        <p>PRIVATE E. WILLIAMSON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>KILLED,</head>
        <p>KILLED,</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. G. KENWRIGHT</p>
        <p>ENpeeled Home AVountleti, But</p>
        <p>• A. pathetic little figure is Mrs. J. G. Ken- of 15, Forster-street, Warrington, whose husband has joined the immortal ranks of the 'hero&amp; 'slain in the Allies' great cause. , Singularly sad are the circumstances under. Which Mrs. Kenwright, who has four children 'ranging from eight year 'to eighteen unths, learned of the death of her gallant hus- 'nand. Last Friday she had news that he had .heen shot in the stomach and taken to, hospital., iMrs- Kenwright during the .week-end made .Preparations for her husband's return home, 'the Wish being father to the thought.- She has. 410 relatives in Warrington, but neighbours' had vised her not to be too optimistic. Neverthe- !ese, she continued to entertain the idea that her -nusband would be home within a few days, and -When a letter arrived from the Front on Mon- morning she exclaimed to a friend who hap- Pened to be present: "Here's his - letter, he's I;nt dead." Then, realising that the handwrit- ..itig un the envelope was strange. she opened it More subdued spirits and read:— Dear Madam,-1 am sorry to have to tell you your husband, Private J. G. Kenwright, of the 4th Lancashire Regiment, died in hos-</p>
        <p>pital on April 24th. He was shot through the abdomen and there was practically no hope for him from the first.--Yours sin- cerely, M. G. Medforth, sister-in-charge.</p>
        <p>• A COMRADE'S TESTIMONY. On Tuesd - •eeei 11) morning the grief-stricken widow hved the following letter: "Dear Mrs. Ken- est SympaYou allow me to tender my deep- t• to you in this, your hour of great. t 24aL; Your husband meant more to me than _0 anyone in A Company. From the time when he joined the company at Tunbridge- Wells to the day when he met his death, storeman he as company was invaluable. No, matter what difficulties were In the way, his cheery spirit and good ,humour never left him. - His patience Was never exhausted, and his heart was as tender as a child's. I was with him when he was hit, but as they lifted him on to the stretcher I thought it would be only a few weeks before he returned. But it was not to be. You ,rEloy console yourself with the knowledge that Ile died as he lived—bravely. May God bless 'and help you and your children. I shall look forward to the time when I shall be able to tell fIU What in a letter I cannot say.—Yours very sincerely, David Ridyard, C.-Q.-M.-S.. A Co., 4th S. Lanes. Regt."</p>
        <p>"COMING HOME IN THE 13IQ SHIP." Three days prior to receiviing the first news of hig being hit, Mrs. Kenwright had a letter from Iter husband, in which he stated that he was lust going into the trenches, and spoke cheer- , 111 Y of the war being over in August. and of • all coming home in the big ship. 7 Private John Gilbert Kenwright was 27 years .(1 age, and an affectionate father and a devoted • husband. He joined the Territorials about four nars ago, and was just "out of. his time" when ‘he war broke out. Without hesitation, how- he decided for. King and country, and gned for foreign service. his, first entry into "le tienchea• was made on his birthday. • 14%4:fixated at St. Ann's School. Private Ken- ght served his time with the Pearson and 4vnoveles Coal and Iron Company. by whom he 748 employed up to the time of his enlisting •Or the war. He- was qualifying for the post of citllgine-driver, and in this connection it is of interest to note that his father was killed ::• 11ile working in the capacity to which the' • ceased soldier aspired. He was connected all life with St. Ann's Church, and belonged to „."e All Saints' Bible Class, for whom he played Anotball. (f‘ 'E OF PRIX-ATE KENWRIGHT'S</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN DAGNALL.</p>
        <p>" In The Final And Sure To Win !"</p>
        <p>News was received on Sunday morning,by Mr. :old Mrs. W. Dagnall, of 53, Winwick-road, Warrington, that their son, Private John Dag- nall, of the 4th Lancashire Regiment, had been killed in action. The message, which was dated April 26th, read as follows:— Dear Mr. and Mrs. Dagnall,—lt is with very deep regret that I have to inform you that your son, John Dagnall, of the 4th Lancashire Regiment, was killed in action yesterday. and was buried this morning in the soldiers' cemetery. It is very hard indeed for these young fellows to be cut off in toeir prime, but no words of mine will heal the wound that I know this short note will cause. You will be gratified to know that he died doing his duty for King and country, and that his name will be honoured among those of others of his regiment who have gone before. Please accept my deepest sympathy in your loss, and I feel sure that his company commander will write you as soon as he returns from duty. I am sending on his personal effects to-day.— Yours faithfully, W. H. Ingram (Q.M.) P.S.—The letter enclosed was written by</p>
        <p>Pm V MI 3. DAGNALL.</p>
        <p>pital, at 10, Canton House Gardens, where the latest reports show he is progressing favour- ably, although he is likely to be disabled for a long time</p>
