      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LAST LETTERS.</head>
        <p>LAST LETTERS.</p>
        <p>ilfqxxl He Would Be Back For Bird</p>
        <p>Races.</p>
        <p>ifill•rivate Kenwright, who was interested in WR,(4:011 flying, wrote the following letter, to a libour, Mr. J. Milsom, 19, Forster-street, in h • leh • be• a:. bear Joe,—Just a line hoping you and Alice 4e all right and little Freddie, as. it leaves .me &amp;it Present. We are having glorious weather t 71.111 here, .and we have just returned from the liLnehes after six hard days, when ,we were re- by the 2nd south Lancs. • it is a pleasure. ,„ De With your own town's lads. Well, Joe, . I 'lat. tell you that we had a hot night, as the 7 started to bombard our trenches. but -our , artillery knocked hell out of them when Started, and the time is near to hand when tui Will go back, as the line is getting stronger an th" the weather' better, and then we will show eau.</p>
        <p>fir f exPect you have read about T. Jarvis. ed, poor chap, he died a hero for a good ruse. and if I live to get back to dear old Eng- I Will tell .you where he • is buried and t at sortof a grave,we have made him. It is a I only wish we could have it photo- -05 it stands alone. „....‘.°llow I had better change the subject, or you VlRht be getting creepy. I would like to know :!_crw You are going on with the pigeons? ,Are Lao trajoblg them up the line, as we intend to e'llr_ske our names when we start again? Now. ziTeOaber me to Jim Higham, and if Dick has Ci,°it Roue back tell him from me that he is lucky. N'e raY t best respects to all the members and ell them I am sorry I cannot be amongst them, La_ a ,living tellt I in hopes of being home.for the "Troencement of the young bird races. I : it h we shall have got rid of .the enemy by e;lix Whenwe start we play hell with them.a is all this time! Bye-bye.—l. re- -112 la' 310ar Old Pal, GILDERTP</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <p>him before he went out on duty, and was handed to his C.Q.M.S. It has only just come into my hand. Where To Get a Medal.</p>
        <p>The letter alluded to is one found in the dead soldier's • pocket. It contained the following sentences: " If a. man has not the nerve to join of his own, accord, he is no use to us, as we would go to the last one, and think nothing about it. You can tell --- (one of the War- rington Football team) that as they have lost ' the cup the only chance of getting a medal is to come Out here, as we are in tip final and are sure to win." _ Two more letters were received On Tuesday morning, one from Private T. Pemberton, of Warburton-street, and the other from Corporal R. F. Anderton. Dreamed He Was Shot. Writing to the brother with whom deceased lived—Mr. William Dagnall, jun., of 42, John- street—Private Pemberton says:— " I am sorry to inform you that your brother Jack was killed to-night about 11 o'clock, while he was carrying rations to the other part of the battalion who are in the trenches. We were about 70 yards from the trenches, when poor Jack was hit in the neck. It was only on Monday that he was telling me and another, mate named A. Dean—we were all :threo-to-. gether when poor Jack fell—that he had a dream that a bullet had passed right through him. Myself and all his company send our deepest ' sympathy to you and all at home. You have lost a good brother and we have lost a good I comrade, for he was well liked and respected by us all."</p>
        <p>One of Our Best Men. In a letter to Mr. and Mrs. Dagnall, under date April 22nd, Corporal Anderton writes:— " It is with very great regret that I have to inform you that your dear son Jack was fatally shot last night about 9.45. At the time he was one of our ration party on the way to the trenches, when he was hit through the neck by a bullet. I was near him at the time and, may say, he suffered practically no pain whatever, as death was instantaneous. It is very hard indeed for you to lose such a good son, but he was a true Englishman, and gave his life fight- ing for Ms King and country, and you have every reason to be justly proud of him. All his comrades in No. 12 platoon will greatly miss him, as he was of a good-natured and kindly disposition. and many a time have I personally seen him giving a helping hand to a fellow who was not so well blessed in strength as himself. As our officer remarked, he was without doubt one of our best men, never failing in his duty. His one ideal was work, which he always did with every thoroughness. Scott once wrote: One crowded hour of glorious life Is worth an age without a name. " During the last two months your son has lived many such hours, and it will be a little comfort for you to know that God has given him his reward--everlasting peace. "On behalf of the N.C.O.'s and men of his -platoon I offer you our deepest 'sympathy, and trust that God will give you strength to help You over your trouble. I would just like to say our friend is laid to rest by the side of some more Warrington heroes who have fallen in this great fight for England's honour, and a nice cross has been Placed over the spot, bearing his name." Popular With .Worknlates. PriYate -Dagnall was 26 years of age. . The reputation he earned amongst his comrades at the Front is indicative of the popularity which he enjoyed at the Dallam Forge, where he was employed up to the time of his enlistment for foreign service, on the outbreak of war. He had already served the regulation four years in the Territorials when the call came, and he re-joined for a more serious service the regi- ment with which he ,had been associated for purposes of training. Keenly interested in most forms of Manly sport, the dead soldier on several occasions played Rugby football for the Dallam Forge team. Private Wm. Rhodes, who was wounded in the retreat from Mons, and is now in England, is a step-brother of the deceased soldier, two of whose cousins are also with the Colours. He was educated at the Silver-street and St. Anne's Schools:</p>
        <p>Elmwood-Avenue Congrega ion al Recreation Club. In rather unfavourable weather the tennis courts of the above club were opened on Satur- day afternoon by Mr. J. W. Travis. A .fair number a members were present, and after a short speech by Mr. Travis and a few words from Rev. C. P. Pitt, tea was partaken in the schoolroom, a very enjoyable evening after- wards being spent on the courts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>"I Had Just Time To Commend His Soul," Writes The Chaplain.</head>
        <p>"I Had Just Time To Commend His Soul," Writes The Chaplain.</p>
        <p>Yesterday the news was broken to Mr. and Mrs. Williamson, of 53. Priestley-street, War- rington, that their son, Private Ernest William- son, .of the 4th (Territorial) South Lancashire Regiment, had been killed. The following letter, dated April 27th, was received by the Rev. E. Downham (curate-in- charge of St. Paul's Church), who was entrusted. with the painful task of conveying the sad news:—</p>
        <p>My Dear Sir,—Would you very kindly break the news of the death of Private E. William- son, 4th S. Lancs., to his mother, Mrs. Williamson, 53, Priestley-street, Warrington? He was brought into the hospital to-night and died within a minute. I had just time to com- mend his soul. He was quite unconscious. He was killed by shrapnel shell. Amongst his papers was a card of yours telling of your nightly prayer at 8 p.m. in St. Paul's Church. All his papers, &amp;c., will be sent to his Colonel, and so eventually reach his mother. I shall bury him to-morrow in our soldiers' little cemetery. A wooden cross will be erected, and the soldiers will keep the ground well with turf and flowers. The exact position is noted at headquarters, and no doubt will be communicated in due course.— Yours sincerely, H. Marshall, chaplain. Joined As A Bugler.</p>
        <p>Private Williamson was 20 -years of age. He joined the Territorials as a bugler at the age of fourteen. When the call came for men, he enlisted as a stretcher-bearer. Additional pathos is lent to the case by reason of the fact that Private Williamson was engaged to be married. He was educated at the Bluecoat School, and, on leaving there, was engaged by Mesgrs. J. Crosfield and Sons at their soap works, where he held, at the time of his enlist- ment, the office of time-keeper.</p>
        <p>An All-Round Athlete.</p>
        <p>The deceased soldier was exceedingly keen 'on physical fitness, and it has been said that he made a hobby of keeping himself in perfect condition. He could truthfully be termed an all-round athlete—football, swimming and walking entering into his private curriculum of physical culture. Perhaps it was due to the excellent health. he enjoyed as the result of the way he looked after himself that he was one of the brightest and best tempered youths one could meet, and was very popular amongst those with whom he worked, and, indeed, with al/ who knew him. He held a medal won as a member of a victorious team of Association football players representing the Works. Private Williamson was also a member of the Warrington Swimming Club and of the "Health and Strength League." Another institution with which he was connected was the Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>His Last Letter.</head>
        <p>His Last Letter.</p>
        <p>In the last letter he sent home to his par- ents, Private Williamson wrote: "I have slept in carts for the last two nights, but we're hop- ing to have another place before long Every place we move to is in an awful state— farms and houses smashed to atoms." Two step-brothers of Private Williamson are with the Colours. They are Private Geo. Edwar,des (in Prance) and Private John Ed- wardes (at Tunbridge Wells). The late Private Williamson's father is em- ployed at the St. Helens Cable Company's Works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>SECOND-I.IEUTEN I' II 11 TOX.</p>
        <p>Second-Lieutenant Alan Herbert Hatton, 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment (War- rington Territorials), was shot through the thigh while in the firing trenches on Thursday afternoon last week. Captain Reginald Smith, who was in an adjoining trench, made his way to his wounded fellow-officer, dressed his in- juries temporarily and made him as comfortable as possible to await the darkness, when the stretcher-bearers came round and conveyed him to the dressing station. Having been conveyed to the base hospital, Lieutenant Hatton was subsequently sent to London, and is now an inmate .of. Vicountess Ridley's Military. Hos-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Good and Kind Officer.</head>
        <p>A Good and Kind Officer.</p>
        <p>"Lieutenant Hatton has been wounded in the thigh," says a letter from a Private. "He got hit in the sable place as Sissmith. He was a very good and kind officer."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Private's Gallant Conduct.</head>
        <p>Private's Gallant Conduct.</p>
        <p>Later information shows that Lient. Hatton, being the officer commanding the renches, was taking stock of the spades and other imple- ments and ammunition at the end of the day's work, •before handing over the treno'iles to the officer commanding the relieving 'party. He left the trench and was shot by a sniper operat- ing from the place at which the sniper shot who killed Lieut. Seymour-Jones. Two of his men jumped out of the trench, and though they were exposed to the dangerous fire of the Ger- mans they succeeded in lifting him back into the trench. With regard tto Lieut. Hatton's condition, it is feared that it may be some months before he recovers the use of the limb. as the sciatic nerve is injured; but at present it is not pos- sible to gauge the extent of the injury. Lieutenant Hatton, Fho is a on of Mr. Herbert Hatton, a: membei. Of the firm. of Messrs. Robert Davies and Co:,' solieitors.; and lived with his parents at .Thelwall, is twenty years of age, the youngest officer in the battalion. He is serving his articles tis a solicitor with his father. Educated at Charter- house Sehool, Lieutenant Hatton served to the officers' training corps connected with that institution, and joined the Territorials im- mediately after war was declared.</p>
        <p>Mr. Kenneth Wray, articled with the same firm, is also an officer in the Territorials. His father is Vicar of Timperley, and his grand- father was for many years Vicar of St. Paul's, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>PRIVATE W. .lENtif--.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE W. .lENtif--.</p>
        <p>A Stretcher-Bearer Sends the Inform-</p>
        <p>lion.</p>
        <p>One of this week's wounded Territorials is Private W. Jenks, of the Albion Hotel, Battersby-lane, Warrington, intimation of whose injury was received on Wednesday morn- ing by his sister, Miss Jenks, of Green Man /nn, Green-street, Warrington. The message was not official, but came from a stretcher-bearer named Mullins. The information contained in the letter is extremely meagre. No mention is made of how the wounded man came by his injury, or under what circumstances the mis- hap occurred. It was titded OA Private has</p>
        <p>DON'T LooK OLD.—Keep the ravages of time at bay by darkening the grey streaks with ..,..ELYER'S SULPHUR HAIR RUSTORIN, which deepens to a natural colour in a few days. Lockyer's maintains a youthful appearance. Large bot's. everywhere, VOst tree lie Bodtore Laboratory, Loudon, BX.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <p>CORPORAL L. ATHERTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <p>is staying. No mention was made of the nature of the wound, or how it was received.</p>
        <p>Says Germans Do Not Attack in _Droves.</p>
        <p>Just previouS to receiving his wound, Corporal Atherton wrote two letters 4o his mother, in one Of which he sold: " Just a line or two to let you know lam 'in the pink.' .. . I hope it won't be long before I am at home, and then we will have a fine do.' It is all right for people to say the .Germans come up in droves They do no such thing—they are afraid to show themselves. But the Russians are playing the game, ,and the war . does not look like lasting long now." In the second letter the gallant Corporal says: "I have got a bit of a strain in, my side, but save for that, I feel the pink.'" •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Terrier' and Footballer, Too !"</head>
        <p>Terrier' and Footballer, Too !"</p>
        <p>Corporal Atherton, who is 22 years of age, has been in the Territorials for eight years.. .He was formerly employed as a moulder at Messrs. Richmond's, at which place he had been since leaving St. Mary's School, Latchford, at the age of thirteen. He was very keen on football, and played in the Medal Competition for Orford St. John's, being amongst the medal winners the year his club topped the table. He wor- shipped at St. Mary's Church, Latchford. Corporal Atherton has three relatives serving at the Front, two cousins in the Territorials and a brother-in-law in the King's Royal Rifles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>PRIVATE S. MILLER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE S. MILLER.</p>
        <p>Shot Whilst Asleep In His Billet.</p>
        <p>Private Samuel Miller, one of the War- rington Territorials, was enoying Well-earned repose in his billet; indeed, he was asleep after the cares and anxieties of a spell in the trenches, when the German guns commenced long-distance operations and one of their shells found the billet, and, .bursting, brought down the roof on .the sleeping soldier's head, inflicting nasty and painful injuries to the head, but, happily, not of so serious a nature as. to necessitate his removal to hospital. This was on April 19th, and since it happened a letter has been received from the injured man's brother-in-law, Private Hamlet Savage,' stating that the hurts received by Private Miller are comparatively slight. "The shell burst just over the place where he was sleeping." says the letter,- ',and brought down part of it, and some Of the fragni.et.its:got- him in the head." '</p>
        <p>- The writer of this later, besides. being the injured man's brother-in-law, is also a close friend, and his wife is at the present time living in• the same house as Private Miller's mother, viz., 361, Winwiek-road. . •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Four Years a Drummer. in the "Terriers."</head>
        <p>Four Years a Drummer. in the "Terriers."</p>
        <p>Private Samuel Miller is 23 years of age, and he signed on in the Territorials for four years. His time was on the point of expiring when the war. broke .it, but he was too enthusiastic a citizen-soldier to allow that face to stand in the way of his volunteering for active service, being among the first batch to send in their names. He was a drummer in the " Terriers " in The piping times of peace, and it was as a drummer that he went to the Front. Private Miller was educated at St. Ann's School, and after leaving went to work at the Orford Tanyard, where he was employed up to the time of volunteering for the Front. The first part of his war training was undergone at Dunfermline, and the latter part at Tunbridge Wells. He was an enthusiastic follower of foot- ball, and at one time played it as a member of a local junior club. He was ever since he joined, sä his mother, Mrs. Annie Miller, assured our representative, a keen "Terrier"; "in fact," she said. "he thought of nothing else, and he was bent on going out as soon as he heard that the war was declared."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <p>PRIVATE W. JENKS</p>
        <p>( , 41,4 ..'''.',.'',','A'''l.•;.;‘: , ~,-.* -4.1.:t45. ' , -„,,,- .' , 't, • , .: &lt;l,le. %!.,:ei:Ysty .4.; .. ' •' : "' . . ter..p5,,,,,er5, • ~,, ' ' ' ''.4....1:e/,';:, .:•, ' ' , -,,, ", : ' ','', Vi;t`f.i . • - ' ' •). '' . '''', .',` .sl,"'!-- '3' s:'.,,' ~' '',' . '' - ' ' - ': ,i• ~,,,:.v*,:r.•,,i'',,r,i'ktl.'" . - ', ' ' '' . ''''' ' ‘ 4,1`1.1if-;f:rif:!-''''• '• ' - •41 : ''',7*;P;-.:..,:' ' - • , :....P, . ~,',. ' ' '4 . , ' - •,` ;.:,,J,lt' ,-1.:•., . . .‘ . • ,:'4 g , , , • • . . • ,' • ..,:`,-. I' . - 0 1 fb ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>PRIVATE R. WOODS.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE R. WOODS.</p>
        <p>wife as follows:—" Just to let you know I got wounded in the right thigh on a recent Monday night, but you must not be upset. I will soon be all right again. I am in hospital in Boulogne. I hope you and baby are going on all right. Our officer said he would write, so I suppose you will know by the time you get this. . . . I had finished my share in the trenches, but I had to take something to eat for those who were in, and I got hit while going in. I hope they let me come home again, be- cause it seems ages since I saw you, and I am bursting to have another look at you and baby. I feel a bit weak at present, but I think I shall soon be all right." In Hospital At Liverpool.</p>
        <p>An official intimation was subsequently re- ceived by Mrs. Woods, and alsa a postcard from her husband at the Ist Western General Hos- pital, Fazakerley, Liverpool:— _ _</p>
        <p>_ Writing to his mother, Mrs. Elizabeth "Woods, of 4, Hardy-street, Private Woods says he passed through Warrington in the train, on his way to the hospital at Liverpool, He explained that the bullet that shot him went right through his leg, but broke no bones. Private Woods has been visited in hospital by his wife. ' Private Woods, who first joined the Terri- torials at the outbreak of war, is twenty-two years of age. He is a Catholic, and attended St. Alban's Church. Educated at St. Bene- dict's School, he was subsequently employed at the Orford Tannery, where he worked till he responded to the call to arms. He is a keen boxer. Private Woods has an uncle and a cousin in the Territorials, and other relatives in different branches of the Forces.</p>
        <p>• " ;:y4101•;14,P,.:N s k 54.," : , , . . . • • ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>PRIVATE J. MeAVOY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. MeAVOY.</p>
        <p>Hit in The Cheek By Glancing Bullet.</p>
        <p>Mrs. McAvoy, of 18, Ernest-street, Warring- ton, whose husband, Private H. 'MeAvoy, belongs to the Warrington Territorials, has re- ceived the following letter:—</p>
        <p>Dear Mrs. McAvoy,-1 am sorry to have to inform you that your husband, Private</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <p>McAvoy was wounded shortly after noon to-day. A bullet struck the gun and glanced off and gave him a rather severe wound in the right cheek. However, I do not think his life is in danger, though he is suffering a good deal of pain. Your husband is an efficient soldier, and his officers could always rely on him to do his duty well and cheerfully. . . . I am sending him back to the dressing station as soon as it gets sufficiently eark.—Yours faithfully, Clar- ance F. Cary, 0:c A Company.</p>
        <p>'44 - k . ' -..111/*•: ". . • 1,? • `, , _ 4e,</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. H. MeAVOY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Broken Jaw.</head>
        <p>A Broken Jaw.</p>
        <p>"I was close to Mac at this time" says a Private writing home. "The bullet caught the sight on the gun and then hit Mac flat on the face, breaking his jaw. I was with him all day in the trench. He could not speak at all. I had to supply him with pencil and writing paper."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>IN HOSPITAL AT LEICESTER.</head>
        <p>IN HOSPITAL AT LEICESTER.</p>
        <p>Later, Mrs. McAvoy had a postcard from her husband, written from the sth Northern General Hospital, Leicester, saying that he had arrived at that institution.</p>
        <p>Private McAvoy, who is, thirty-one years of age, and has one child, came to Warrington about ten years ago from Fleetwood. He was working up to the time of the outbreak of war at Messrs. Crosfield's Soap Factory, ,and was one of the first of the South Lancashires to go to the Front. has been in the Territorials for nine years. The particular department in which he serves his country is the machine gun section.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>PRIVATE H. KINSEY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE H. KINSEY.</p>
        <p>Picked Up With His Hat "Full Of</p>
        <p>Blood."</p>
        <p>Private H. Kinsey, of 59, Fox-street, is another Warrington Territorial who has had the misfortune to be wounded. The official list, received at the Drill Hall on Friday afternoon, contained his name, and his parents also re- ceived a letter from him, in which he said: "I was wounded in the head by a bullet, and was picked up with my hat full of blood: It is, however, nothing serious." Private Kinsey. who is a single man, and is nineteen years of age, resides with his parents, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Kinsey, at 59, Fox street. He joined' the "Terriers" since the outbreak of war. He received his education at rreathsido School. A brother of his, Jack, who is 21 years of age, is serving in Kitchener's Army, and is undergoing training at Win- chester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>DRUMMER JOHN lIALFORD.</head>
        <p>DRUMMER JOHN lIALFORD.</p>
        <p>Drummer John Raiford, whose name appeared in yesterday's official list of casualties, was wounded in the back by shrapnel whilst on sentry duty, on Wednesday of last week. The news first reached his parents at 9, Walker- street on Saturday and Sunday—when Major Ingram and Sergeant Bottomley wrote res- pectively .to say that he had been hit. Doubt- less the special reason for their doing so is that the Drummer had also belonged to the St. Paul's Church Lads' Brigade under their officership. In his letter Sergeant Bottomley said he did not think 'the wound was dangerous, as the la 4 was conscious during the dressing. He jokingly added that Drummer ITalford had "got his blighter that would bring him tO England !" The wounded Drummer himself has not yet written home.</p>
        <p>" The. Examiner" only a fortnight ago pub- lished several interesting extracts from "Drum- mer Jack's" letters home to Warrington—one of them anent an obliging hen which kindly laid an egg for the " Terriers " nearly, every day.</p>
        <p>Where He Had Worked.</p>
        <p>Born twenty-one years ago at 15, Garibaldi- street, Bank Quay, Drummer " Jack " Halford is the son of Mx. and Mrs. R. H. Halford, of 9, Walker-street. His father is an engineer for the Warrington Wire Rope Works, Bewsey. Educated at the Wycliffe School, under Mr. A. Corney's headmastership, Drummer Raiford there gained the distinction of inclusion in the school photograph of the record attendance group of his period-7-which photograph now occupies a place of honour in the school. At the age of fourteen he went to work at Messrs. Rylands's warehouse, but six months later be- came apprenticed as an iron-turner to Messrs. Forshaw. and Son.</p>
        <p>Here he remained until September, when he decided to enlist in the 4th South Lancashires. After three or four months' training at Black- pool he proceeded to the Front via Tunbridge Wells. With an inclination for bugling, the Drummer has been making good use both of drum and bugle whilst in the war-zone. Always interested in Army work, Drummer Halford formerly served five -yefirs in the Chnz*h Lads' Brigade, arid is Thus entitled to their long service medal. He also belongs to St. Paul's Church, and has passed through its Sunday School—in which he is a member of the Brigade Bible Class. Upon leaving school, Raiford attended for a session the Commercial Institute, Arpley-street, under the headmastership of Mr. S. W. Jeffery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>PRIVATE ALFRED JACKSON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ALFRED JACKSON.</p>
        <p>Shot In The Back Whilst Having "Another Packet" At The Germans.</p>
        <p>Private Alfred Jackson, who, prior to going to the Front, made his home with his parents at 68, Orford-street, and who was among the first of the Warrington Territorials to volunteer for active service, is in the list of those who have been wounded. He has been in the Territorials four years, and at present is a member of "A" Company. He is 25 years of age, and was formerly employed at Messrs. Crosfield's. He has a brother-in-law; Private Peter Clarke, serving with Kitchener's Army in the 3rd South Lancashire Regiment, who is at present under- going training at Crosby.</p>
        <p>Whilst engaged with the enemy on April 21st, Private Jackson received a bullet wound in the back.</p>
        <p>" Tommy " Keen on Cakes.</p>
        <p>In a recent letter home he wrote: "We have been in the trenches, and have had another packet at the Germans. We are having pretty good weather at present, and taking things all round, we are having a fairly good time. I would like you to send me a parcel with a few cakes in it, for they would be very welcome here."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>TERRITORIAL CASUALTIES</head>
        <p>TERRITORIAL CASUALTIES</p>
        <p>Another List faxes One Killed, One Died of Wounds, and Eight Wounded.</p>
        <p>The following completes the list of casualties sustained by the 4th (Territorial) Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment up to April 18th:—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Second-Lieutenant Allen H. Hatton, A Com- pany; bullet wound in the right thigh; received April 22nd.</p>
        <p>OTHER RANKS. KILLED IN ACTION.</p>
        <p>2321, Private J. Dagnall, C Company; bullet wound in back; received April 21st. DIED OF WOUNDS. '</p>
        <p>1963, Private J. Kenwright, A Company; shrapnel wound in buttock; received April 19th; died April 21st. WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>1286, Private S. Miller, A Company; head wound, caused by falling debris; received April 19th. Not bad enough to necessitate admission to hospital. [Tome address, 361, Winwick-road. 546, Private J. H. McAvoy, A Company; bullet wound in cheek; received April 20th. Home address, 18, Ernest-street.</p>
        <p>•1355. Private A. Jackson, A Company; bullet wound in back; received April 21st. Home address, 68, Orford-street.</p>
        <p>2653, Drummer J. Ha!ford, C Company; shrapnel wound in back 'whilst on sentry duty; received April 21st. Home address, 9, Walker- street. _</p>
        <p>2295, Private W. Jenks, B Company; shrapnel wound in the upper right leg; received April 24th. Home address, Albion Hotel, 'Battersby- lane.</p>
        <p>2413. Private H. Kinsey, B Company; bullet wound in head; received April 24th. Home address, fortnerly of 39, Leicester-street, and now of Fox-street.</p>
        <p>1420. Private J. Kinsey, C Company; shrapnel wound in the lower right leg; received April 21th. The number and name given do not agree with the official roll.</p>
        <p>- In the previous list it was.. reported that Second-Lieutenant L. Holden had been wounded on April 7th. The correct date was April Bth. The foregoing list was received by Lieut. Williamson at the Drill Hall yesterday after- noon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>DRINK PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>THE DRINK PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Government Proposals Submitted 11</p>
        <p>The Chancellor.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd George told the House of Commons on Thursday night how the Government Ivo- poset to control the drink traffic where it is hindering transport and the output of war munitions.</p>
        <p>Districts with black records will be scheduled by Order in Council on the advice of the War Office, Admiralty, and Home Office, who will appoint a Central Board of Control. The proposals outlined by the Chaneellof. of the Exchequer are as follows The duty on spirits is to be doubled. There is to be a graded surtax on beers containing over 7 per cent. of proof spirit, ranging from Is. to 365. per bulk barrel, accord- ing to the specific gravity. The duty on wines is to be quadrupled, and the tax on sparkling wines raised to 15s. per gallon. _ _</p>
        <p>The maximum dilution of spirits 'permitted by law is to be increased from 25 to 35 under proof. The Government is to take powers, limited to the period of the war, to close public-houses or to control them, in certain areas where their presence is considered to be prejudicial to the output of munitions of war, the work of trans- port, or the discipline of troops. The power will include the right to use licensed or other premises in the area for the purposes of supply- ing reasonable refreshment, and to make pur- chases from. any brewery, notwithstanding that a house has previously been tied.</p>
        <p>- i Pair compensation s to be given in respect of existing interests. The principles of compensa- tion will be the same as those already in force under the present Defence of the Realm Act, and a COMMiSgOII is to be appointed to ascer- ,tala tlta asligUt to bo</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>PRIVATE SHARPLES.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE SHARPLES.</p>
        <p>now lying in the Ist General Hospital at Etretat, France, suffering from conjunctivitis. Rifleman Sharples is the son of Mr. and Mrs. Frederick John Sharples. He is a single man. and 20 years of age. Prior to enlisting, he was employed at the Latchford Tube Works. As a boy, he attended the Wycliffe school.</p>
