      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>RIFLEMAN THOMAS SUDELL.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN THOMAS SUDELL.</p>
        <p>Wounded In Arm In St. Elol Bayonet</p>
        <p>Charge.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Thomas Sudell, of the 4th Rifle Brigade • (whose home address is 189, ,Orford- lane) now lies progressing very well in Field House Hospital, Bradford—having been wounded in the arm in a bayonet charge at St. Eloi. In a letter to a friend he tells how he received his wound: "Of course you may know I am having a grand time. Oh! yes, I should say so! Well, I got wounded in a bayonet at- tack at St. Eloi ; we did go through it. I am wounded in ray right arm, but I am making fine progress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>" It Was a Glorious Attack."</head>
        <p>" It Was a Glorious Attack."</p>
        <p>"It was a glorious attack that we made, but fancy me trying to stop machine-gun bullets! "I am very glad you are still in good health and doing your duty. lam sorry I did not</p>
        <p>• ‘"". • -.4 , 4 ' ' • • ' I , ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>RIFLEMAN THOMAS SIIDELL.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN THOMAS SIIDELL.</p>
        <p>answer your letter from France, but it came the day after I got wounded. lam being treated Al over here—and all being well I hope. to be out on furlough in a week or two." The Rifleman, when wounded, was first con- veyed to• Rouen, two days afterwards over to Southampton, thence for a fortnight to Leeds— and so to Bradford. The bullet wounds, three in number, were near his elbow. All three bullets have now been extracted—although the third was " very difficult to get at, and caused him much suffering." We hear .that he will nit come back home for a month yet. °At the battle in which Rifleman Sudell met his misfortune the 4th Rifles were badly cut up.</p>
        <p>-Aged twenty, the Rifleman is a son of an over, looker at Cockhedge Mills—where he himself is employed in peace times as a weaver on four looms. Here he had worked since leaving St. Mary's Schools at the age of fourteen. Mr. A. Kilburn was his headmaster. He attends St. Benedict's Church, and is a member 4 of their Young Men's Society. Also he belongs to St. Alban's Choir.</p>
        <p>In football he is a great enthusiast, and not only acted as secretary to the Orford St. John's A.F.C., but also gained two medals in that team a couple of years ago. Moreover, he carried off two other football medals whilst at school. He enlisted in September, about the time many others of his football friends did.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Trophies From The Trenches.</head>
        <p>Trophies From The Trenches.</p>
        <p>_ Rifleman Sudell has recently sent home a couple of very interesting photos which he found at St. Eloi in a captured German trench. They were postcards; and the first shows a handsome German soldier decorated with the Iron Cross, posing before his home. The second is of two young ladies in a garden; on the reverse side of which they have inscribed their names "with love" to the recipient, and adds "Bellheim, Kreigsjahr, 1914."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>THELWALL CANAL A MYSTERY.</head>
        <p>THELWALL CANAL A MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>Body Identified.</p>
        <p>The Lymni police have this week succeeded in identifying the dead. body of a woman which walk pulled out of the Manchester Ship -Canal at Thel*all, at about half-past ten on the morning of Wednesday, the 7th April, after it had been in the water two or three weeks. It Will be remembered that the verdict at the inquest was "Found- drowned."</p>
        <p>The body was that of Alice Griffiths, a single woman, of middle age, who was employed as a general servant by a family in Withington. To her employers she had never mentioned her relations, but it is thought that her father is a builder in Wem, Salop. Inquiries are being made in order to verify this—or otherwise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>HOW POLICE FOUND OUT.</head>
        <p>HOW POLICE FOUND OUT.</p>
        <p>The body when found was well dressed, but the police failed to derive any clue from the beads round the neck and most of the other articles of clothing. They, however, discovered the name of the Withingtcin lady in whose em- ploy the woman had been, upon one of the gar- ments—and their diligence in pursuing inves- tightions for the address has met with success.</p>
        <p>The recreation that makes conscience squirm is almost sure to be desecration.</p>
        <p>There is only One way to find ease in your work, and that is to, put heart in it. '</p>
        <p>More money is lost on the way down hill than. on the lever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <p>1/.1</p>
        <p>Ii</p>
        <p>i 1 ' .:. I ' ' I::''..;- it . i $, / I</p>
        <p>,1:1r - 11; .1i</p>
        <p>t'</p>
