      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <p>5</p>
        <p>LiYMM</p>
        <p>I _ What Lymmites Must Not Forget.—Subscrip- tions, be they great or small, towards the Lymm public roll of honour will be gladly received by the Urban District Council—to the / recognised officers of which they should be handed at the earliest possible moment.</p>
        <p>Levying the Rate.—When the question of levying the rate came up at Monday evening's meeting of the Council, it was deferred for a week for consideration at a meeting of the General Purposes Committee. On Monday that committee will meet, afterward resolving itself into Council formally to pass the new rate.</p>
        <p>Private Arthur Hadden.—A postcard to us home at 13, Rush-green, this week says that Private Arthur Badden (who fell into a "Jack Johnson" hole at the Front, as we reported at the time, caused his return to Vngland for hospital treatment to his aim) says that he is going on very well, and hopes presently to be home.</p>
        <p>Corporal Barber's Luck.—Corpqra 1 Ii enry Barber, whose pocket-book saved his life at the Front a fortnight ago, has sufferei no tux° from shock, and is going on all right.—Privat(s Percy Clare writes to a friend: "H. Barber hail a lucky escape the other night. He got shot through the pocket, and it went through his letters and shirt—but did not hurt him!"</p>
        <p>Hare Shot at the Front.—Writing home on April Nth, Private Stanley Gibbs says: "We came out of the trenches on Thursday night, and to-day is just like a midsummer's day. I shot a hare to-day with a rifle, and we are having it for dinner. All things are running well. The nose-end of a shell flew past my face yestersiay, and struck a tree trunk by my side; but it didn't touch me...."</p>
        <p>"A Rum Night."—ln a letter received last week-end by one of his friends, Private Percy Clare said: "We came out of the trenches last night for four days. . . . I've had two or three escapes. . . ." On April 18th, the Private sent a card: "We go into the trenches to-morrow night. We had a ' rum ' night last night; all the guns were going. I never saw anything like it, and don't care if I never see it</p>
        <p>_ again. . . ."</p>
        <p>Attended Council Meeting.—ln addition to the Urban District Councillors who attended Mon- day night's annual Council meeting (and whose names appear in another column) there were also present the following officials: Messrs. W. Mullard (clerk), W. Timperley and Cecil Mort (assistant clerks), G. West (gas manager), D. Mort (surveyor and inspector), and Barnes (waterworks manager), and Dr. De Jong (medieal-officer).</p>
        <p>Council Economy.—Councillor Critchley, pro- posed at Monday's meeting that the Council have the usual pocket year-books printed, but found no seconder.—Councillor Cowper re- marked that all possible economy was needed; every house in Lymm would yet be filled with wounded soldiers. (A Voice: "No, no.!).— Councillor Parker suggested the publication of a postcard with the meetings printed on.— Councillor Critchley : It is very: important.— The Chairman: I am afraid it is decided against now!</p>
        <p>Inquiry Into Loans.—At Monday's Council meeting, the Clerk (Mr. Mullard) read a letter from the Local Government Board intimating, in reference to the application of the Council to borrow a further sum of £3OO for the sewer- age and sewer disposal scheme, that a local inquiry would be held on the subject as soon as the engagements of the Board's inspectors would allow. Due public notice would be given a the inquiry. The newly-elected Chairman (Councillor Gotobed) expressed the hope that when the in- iquiry was held some of the members of the Council would put in an appearance, adding that he had been the only representative of the Council at the last inquiry.</p>
        <p>" Busy Times Just Lately."-L—PriVate Richard Percival writes from the Front to Mr. and Mrs. D. Daniels: "I am in the best of health, and have been going to write to you many a time, only something has always come and stoned me. . . . You see how it is; we have lid some busy times just lately. What with mov- ing about and one thing or another we have had very little time to ourselves. . . . I would like to send you a few postcards, but we cannot do that. . . . You will have heard from Mr. Barker; he tells me he is doing well. I expect one or two Lymm lads will be landing }come shortly, and only hope they will soon be better once more; I don't expect they will come out here again. There has been some very hard fighting here now for the last two or three days. I only hope this lot will soon be over, as it is awful at times. . . ."</p>
        <p>Primrose League Appointments.—At the Drill Hall on Wednesday evening was held the annual general meeting of the; Lymm Habita- tion of the Primrose League. There was a good attendance. Satisfactory reports were passed, a balance in hand being shown. Mrs. Legh (of High tegh) ,was made ruling. council- lor; Mrs. Callender, hon. sec.; Miss Ethel Moorehouse, assistant sec.; and Mrs. W. P. Rylands, hon. treasurer. The executive com- mittee consists of the Hon. Mrs. Cross, Mes- dames Holden, and Saville, Miss Callender, Captain T. Ridgway, and Messrs. A. W. Callen- der, JohA Coxon, F. E. Evans, W. E. Hillier, R. Hulme and John Whitlow. Special service badges were presented to Mrs. Saville, Misses Moorehouse and Shakeshaft, and Messirs. F. E. Evans and R. Itulme. Mr. W. P. Rylands gave an interesting war lecture, Mr. Edward Rey- nolds managing the lantern. The musical part of the programme was in charge of Mr. and Mrs. George Williamson and Miss Rylands.</p>
        <p>Cross Hospital, has now been increased by two wards, and room can be found for no less than 25 wounded " Tommies " should need arise. At present there are only six patients. In -view, however, of the stimulated operations at the Front, a further batch are expected any day; in which case the resources of the committee will certainly be rather heavily taxed. There- fore a ready response to the appeal for collectors is confidently anticipated.</p>
        <p>-Generous Helpers.</p>
        <p>In the Congregational Schoolroom, on Mon!. day evening, the concert given in the Statham Schoolroom on Tuesday, April 16th, was ,re- peated before a goodly assembly. As a result, Miss Dalton (of the Lymm Schools) has been able to hand over to "Brookfield," the local Red Cross Hospital, no less than X 5 10s.</p>
        <p>_ • For this the matron and staff desire . to ex- press their hearty thanks—as also for the splendid gifts of eggs. flowers, &amp;c.„ received from the school children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>RED CROSS NEEDS</head>
        <p>RED CROSS NEEDS</p>
        <p>Several Volunteers For Collecting,</p>
        <p>The Working Men's Committee of the Lymm Red. Cros's Hospital, have several vacancies for fund-collectors,, and anyone willizig to help in this patriotic work should submit their names to Mr. Owen Davies, the sectary, Oak Tree Cottage, High Legh ; or attend the committee meeting at the Conservative Club on Tuesday evening, at 8.15. This work of collecting is divided into dis- tricts so as to minimisAthe volunteers' trouble. The accommodation at "Brookfield," the Red</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>REV. J. A. PEN.XLIGGON.</head>
        <p>REV. J. A. PEN.XLIGGON.</p>
        <p>Regretted Departure Of A Fine</p>
        <p>Preacher—And Alan.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Arthur Penaliggon, minister of the Congregational Churches at .Bucklow Hill and Lymm, has accepted the hearty and unani- mous call given to him by Littlemoor Church, Glossop. His present congregations have made every effort to induce him to continue his work amongst them. At special meetings resolutions were passed expressing great regret and dismay at the prospect of his acceptance, earnestly urg- ing him to stay with them, and expressing very high appreciation of his preaching gifts; but, acting on the advice of his ministerial brethren and friends, he felt that he must accept the larger opportunity for service, and accordingly announced his decision during the, service at Bucklow Hill on Sunday morning. Littlemoor Church is an old-established edifice and has great traditions. Mr. Penaliggon commenced his pastorate at the Lymm Congregational Church abouts, year and three months ago. Previonsly.he.liad for a year and nine months been minister of the Bucklow Church—which duties he .did not, of course, relinquish upon coining to. the Lymm Church. He has greatly endeared himself to all Congregationalists with whom he has come into contact, and his loss will certainly be felt heavily when he relinquishes his local pastor- ates on the third Sunday in May.. Unfortunately, Mr. Penaliggon's health has been very poor during his stay at Lymm, com- pelling him to take long. respites from his church labours. It, has also prevented ,his taking any prominent part in the public life. At the Lymm Congregational mid-week ser- vice on Wednesday evening, a letter from the reverend gentleman was read—in which he com- municated the news of his acceptance of the Littlemoor pastorate, and said how sorry he would be to leave the many friends to whom he had become so attached.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>TO SOLDIERS' MOTHERS.</head>
        <p>TO SOLDIERS' MOTHERS.</p>
        <p>Nlrs. Ridgway Tells How to Strengthen and Inspire the Lads.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Emily Ridgway, of Lymm—literature representative of the Diocese of Chester Mothers' Union—writes in the current issue of the "Mothers' Union Journal" the following "To Mothers of Sons at the Front ":--</p>
        <p>" As mothers of sons who are doing their duty bravely and cheerfully on the great battlefield, we must remember in every way to strengthen and inspire them in their self-sacrifice, incul- cating the truth of brotherhood and pointing 'out that even towards our enemies we must bear no malice.</p>
        <p>"In a London pap6r one day last January was published an extract from a letter written by an officer at the Front, and it is thought that the gist of that leiter may help some of the mothers who have sons fighting bravely at some part of that great battlefield. "In the extract given, the officer speaks of the enormous value of cheerful, happy letters from home which inspire one with confidence, and invite in reply a candid statement of what facts can be told, well knowing that the home folk are brave and courageous. The letter points out that it is home vihiCh influences the man even in that place of awful trial, and it has been proved, it Says, that the men who shirk and who do their work only half-heartedly are those whose sword-arm is being clutched by craven hearts at home.'</p>
        <p>"Alas! that there should be parents who .hinder instead of helping: who lower the stand- ard instead of uplifting it. and who pull back the eager heart instead .of encouraging it to climb to the heights of God-like sacrifice. The officer's letter closes with these potent words: 'We are all proud and you angels at home are like the Spiritual Army Service Corps. Love and comfort flow out from you to us and keep up the moral without which an army is more. useless than without its food.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>HEALTH PRECAUTIONS.</head>
        <p>HEALTH PRECAUTIONS.</p>
        <p>Council Consider German Measles</p>
        <p>And 11, hooping-Cough.</p>
        <p>A leter was read at Monday's Council meet- ing, from Mr. George Leigh; clerk to the Bucklow Rural District Council, saying that his Council had under consideration a circular letter from the Local Government Board relating to the compulsory notification of measles, German measles (Rube°la) and whoop- ing cough, Before further considering the matter he had been directed to• ask the Lymm Council what decision they had come to in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>INVESTIGATION NEEDED.</head>
        <p>INVESTIGATION NEEDED.</p>
        <p>• The circular letter referred to stated that the Local Government Board "have decided that at the present time they will not issue a general order. but they are willing to comply with an application from any sanitary authority for an order making the system applicable to cases of measles, 'German measles and whooping cough occurring in their district, and providing that when a notification is received the medical officer, or an officer of the sanitary authority</p>
        <p>acting under his instructions, shall make such inquiries and take such step, as are necessary or desirable for investigating the .nature of the cases and the source of infection, for prevent- ing the spread of infection, • and for removing conditions favourable to infection."</p>
        <p>"The Board," added' the circular, "would also propose to include in the Order a clause ompowering the sanitary authority to provide medical and nursing assistance for the poorer classes of the district suffering from these diseases."</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 1, 1915.</p>
        <p>have seen us get up—we didn't need shouting twice :</p>
        <p>"Our horses have been harnessed up all night, and we have to sleep with our rifle and ammunition against our head, and with our boots and jackets on—ready to move at a minute's notice.</p>
        <p>Saw Hill Captured.</p>
        <p>"I suppose you .have seen where the British, had captured a hill? I saw all that. The men in the trenches said 'German legs anal arms and all sorts' were flying up in the air. "We are sleeping in a bleql works, but it has been shattered with shrapnel; it is some- thing like the salt works, with a chimney just about as big. I wish they would hit the chim- ney while we are out. We are in a field now; come out of shell-fire! SomebOdy said the Germans are ,short of . ammunition. They wouldn't think so if they were to see them over here!</p>
      </div>
