      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>"MAKE PEOPLE TAKE CARE."</head>
        <p>"MAKE PEOPLE TAKE CARE."</p>
        <p>Dr. de Jong said they did not get an epidemic of German measles once in ten years. Measles was already notifiable. There was, however, a great agitation about the disease, and he should certainly say: "Yes, make it notifiable." When they once did so people began to take care; moreover, statistical information was most valuable.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Councillors Groves and Holt, the clerk was instructed to reply to the Buck low Council to the effect that measles was already notifiable, but that with regard to the other two diseases the Council were considering what steps they would take.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>LYMM TERRITORIALS. Another Spell In The Trenches.</head>
        <p>LYMM TERRITORIALS. Another Spell In The Trenches.</p>
        <p>In accordance with the practice which has been followed for the past several weeks, the Lymm Territorials were due to enter the trenches again on Tuesday of this week, after the customary four days' rest which intervenes between tho spells in the fighting line—when. ever such rest can be safely accorded. It transpires, hOwever, that during their last sojourn in the trenches—which, by.the way, is referred to by several "Terriers" as the hot- test time yet experienced—they were not re- lieved as expected. One of the "Tommies," writing home on Sunday, said they were hoping to be relieved that night. This is the latest news available.</p>
        <p>During the week a letter was received from Sergeant Harold Clare to say- that, up to the time of writing, the " Terriers" v.-ere "all right out here."</p>
        <p>Considerable conjectura• has. been, aroused during the latter half of• this week about Private John Read, of Rush Green—now at the Front with the Cheshire,s. The origin was a field post-card apparently sent by him to Miss M. A. Whitfield, of Branch Cottages, Cherry- lane: This card stated that he was being sent down to the base, and the remarks about his being well, sick or wounded were all crossed out in ink. The card was (hated April 21st Later word from him, however, says:—" Did you get my caryl saying that I was well?"--so that evidently a mistake has been made, and there is no cause for alarm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>PRIVATE HENRY CADMAN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE HENRY CADMAN.</p>
        <p>Setveant Says He Was Indeed Lucky.</p>
        <p>Private Cadman—who, as was reported in our last issue, was recently wounded—writes home to say he is progressing quite satisfactorily. • . Sergeant Harold Clare, .writing to a relative on the 16th,. said "I am very pleased to tell -yew' we. came out of the - trenches last night without a scratch—with the exception of Harry Cadman. He is, lam sorry to say, slightly injured; we were cutting a drain from the • trench last night, when a bullet struck his .rifle, smashed it up, and - then grazed his arm. lie will be quite all right in a day or two, and was indeed very lucky."</p>
        <p>Private Pere Clare, in a leter received by a friend on the 20th, says: "ii. Calman got caught in the arm, but I ,hinlz it is not so bad. Ile went away last night."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>LANCE-CORPORAL S. LINLEY.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL S. LINLEY.</p>
        <p>Expresses Pleasure .At "Examiner ' Campaign Against Rumour.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Sydney Linley writes from hospital to his. wife at I'eppet4ireet, to say that he is going on very well, and hopes sooif to be home. , Up to the present; he adds, he has suffered no ill-after-effects of his being shot in the back; and goes out a little—to church and so on. -.</p>
        <p>The- gallant lance-corporal expresses great pleasure at the fact that "The Examiner" has "put a stop to rumours." Private Richard Percival writes to friends: "I suppose you would hear about Syd. Linley and one or two others from Lymm being wounded? We were not in the trenches then, as only one-half, go in at once; but we lost a few when we went in. This is a very hot shop at times.. ."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>PRIVATE RICHARD SILVW.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE RICHARD SILVW.</p>
        <p>Still In French Ilospital—Aiaking</p>
        <p>Good Progress.</p>
        <p>Mrs. J. Shaw, of Booth's Hill, has not this week received further news from her, son. Pte. Richard Shaw—who was wounded at the begin- ning of this month with the Lymm "Terriers" —but Miss Hatton (a teacher in the village schools) lilts received from, him a postcard, in which he tells her that he is in hospital at Ver- sailles, getting on very well, • and that a-lady visitor has given him postcards with views of the hospital, which he treasures. Thus the word which arrived at Lymm last week stating that he was to be sent to an English hospital came from an incorrectly informed correspond- ent.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>"FIGHTING STILL KEEPS ON."</head>
        <p>"FIGHTING STILL KEEPS ON."</p>
        <p>"Shan't know What To Do On Heflin Home," Says Local "Tommy."</p>
        <p>Miss Millie Clare, of "Rockwood," Eagle Brow, has received a letter written on the 17th from her brother, Private Joseph Clare. " Thanks " he says, "for your letter—which I got while in the trenches. . . . Well, I never. felt better in my life! The weather is turning out grand flow; but fighting keeps on. We shall not know what to. do with ourselves when we get back again—always look on that side of things. We are out here fighting for our dear homes in England, and the 'sth Battalion,' though we have lost a few kilted and wounded, shows no signs of fright. It has got to be done, and we are doing well. . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>Lymm Lads Doing Well.</head>
        <p>Lymm Lads Doing Well.</p>
        <p>The day before, Sergeant liar°ld Clare also wrote to his sister: "I was delighted to receive such a cheery letter from you. .. We came out of the, trenches last night, an were all very tired on reaching headquarters this morning at three o'clock. But after a sleep and our break- fast we all feel grand. "The weather here is lovely. We are at rest now till Monday • night, when we go in the trenches. again for four more days. Joe is quite all right, and doing well—in, fact, all our I.ymm boys are doing very well indeed. .. . I. shall have a great deal of news for you when I come home—you :iee, we are not allowed to give much information. I never felt so well as I feel now. So this is good news, isn't it . . ."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>" SIIIPLY AWFUL!"</head>
        <p>" SIIIPLY AWFUL!"</p>
        <p>" Jaek Johnsons " Sound Like Claps Of Thunder To L.) mm " Terrier."</p>
        <p>Private Fred Dodson writes from the Front to his parents (Mr. and Mrs. W. Dodson, of Rush Green) under (late Friday, April 23rd : " This leaves me Al. We are having grand weather at present, but it still goes ,a bit parky 'at nights. - - - -</p>
        <p>-"It-is 'simply awful over here. I would give all I possess if I was at clear little Lymm again, 'rut I am looking forward to the tint</p>
        <p>when I shall come—whiC:ll I do hope and trust will not be long. A Lively Night and Morning,</p>
        <p>"This last week has been the awtullest time of my life. Last night they were shelling us something awful! It is a big town we are in, and when they send a 'Jack Johnson' over it is like a clap of thunder. They sent one over this morning by half-past five. You should</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>Not Downhearted !</head>
        <p>Not Downhearted !</p>
        <p>"I think we are moving from here before long; it is the hottest place we have been in. While I have been writing this, four 'Jack Johnsons' have been over; I suppose you know that when shells come they are red-hot? But we shall have to hope for, the best. .We are not downhearted 'yet; so dicer up! Be sure and keep your spirits up! . . ."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>COUNCIL SENSATION.</head>
        <p>COUNCIL SENSATION.</p>
        <p>Last Year's Chairman Denied Usual Re-eleetion.</p>
        <p>TOWNSHIP 'WAR FINANCES.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Lymm Urban District Council—held on Monday evening:— there were present, in addition to the officials, Councillors C. Cowper, S. Critchley, R. H. Gotobed, W. G. Groves, J. Hardy Holt, F. Ingham, A. Maclntyre, G. H. Parker and T. H. Warburton. Councillors F. Lockyer (vice- chairman) and K. Kershaw were unavoidably absent, the former being ill, and the latter with the Forces.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>CUSTOM CONTESTED.</head>
        <p>CUSTOM CONTESTED.</p>
        <p>The election of the chairman and vice-chair- man for the ensuing year was proceeded with. Councillor Parker proposed the re-election of Councillor Warburton for the former post. It had, he thought, been the custom of the Coun- cil for the chairman to be elected two years. Councillor Cowper: As an amendment I beg to move -</p>
        <p>Mr. Mullard : The proposition has not yet been seconded.</p>
        <p>Coulu•illor Groves: We have generally re- appoiuti (1 the chairman for a second year. Why are we going to depart from that rule? I think the chairman has conducted the meetings in a very fair way, and I think he should have a second year of office. I second the resolution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>BUSINESS MEN NEEDED.</head>
        <p>BUSINESS MEN NEEDED.</p>
        <p>• Councillor Cowper then moved as an amend- ment that Councillor Gotobed, be elected chair- man. He did, not think they had a better business man on the Council, or one who had its work in the parish more at heart, than Mr. Gotobed. They two had been 'at daggers drawn' many a time; but he (the speaker) pro- fessed to be a man, and in Mr. Gotobed they had a man with a splendid business education. And at the present time they wanted a business man at the head. As for a chairman holding office ,two years, he thought it an infinitesimal matter; it was their duty to appoint a buSiness man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>NO REFLECTION ON LATE CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>NO REFLECTION ON LATE CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Councillor Holt, in seconding, said he would like to say that Mr. Warburton had filled the office of chairman of the Council very credit- ably, and he did not want him to think for a moment that they were reflecting in any sense upon him. But they must remember that the position was somewhat exceptional, and by right Mr. Gotobed should have occupied the chair some years ago. Unfortunately it was very hard for Mr. Warburton, but by right Mr. Gotobed shonld have stepped into that position last. year; and though he was often an enemy of his (the speaker's), still in common justice and fairness he must support him at this time. "No man," concluded the speaker, "is more fitted for the chairmanship than Mr. Gotobed. Re has served the ratepayers of Lymm for many years, and I heartily second the proposition made by Mr. Cowper:"</p>
        <p>Councillor Groves: I don't like this election of chairman to be put to the vote at all. I think we ought to elect the chairman unani- mously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>COUNCILLOR GOTOBED'S OFFER.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR GOTOBED'S OFFER.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gotobed: If Mr. Warburton will refrain from voting I will undertake to do the same.</p>
        <p>Councillor Warburton maintained his silence.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gotobed : Well, we must leave it.</p>
        <p>The amendment was !accordingly put to the meeting, and was voted for by Councillors Cowper, Gotobed, Ingham and Maclntyre. • No one voted against it. Councillor Groves: I hope that when Mt. Mullard puts the -.motion; we shall all be unanimous.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mullard then put the motion for Coun- cillor Gotobed's election. Nobody voted. Mr. Mullard : Then I must put the resolution. Again nobody voted.</p>
        <p>Eventually Councillor Gotobed was elected mim con, all voting save himself and Coun- cillors Critchley and Warburton.</p>
        <p>NEW CHAIRMAN'S FINANCE POLICY.</p>
        <p>Raving taken his place in the chair,</p>
        <p>The new Chairman said he felt very deeply indeed the honour which they had conferred upon him by electing him chairman of the Council, and hoped he would be able to fulfil his duties satisfactorily. He .confessed that he entered upon his year of office with feelings of diffidence, remembering that many worthy men had gone before. He could only say he would do his best, and try to treat everyone with fairness.</p>
        <p>"We meet," continued the speaker, "in troublous times—when heavy war clouds over- hang our country, bringing sorrow and desola- tion even into our own little village. I, am sure you will all join with me in hoping that before our next general meeting these clouds will be dispelled, ending in lasting victory for the Allied Forces.</p>
        <p>"You will be called Upon to consider the estimates of expenditure; and, I may make the suggestion that you study all 'the economy pos- sible during the coming year. The Local Government Board has set us an example by deciding that no further loans shall be granted to local authorities, and I hope we shall take a similar line, of action—incurring no other expense than is absolutely necessary. The smaller the burden we can place upon the rate- payers and the relatives of those who have gone to the war the better it will be appreciated."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>CHORUS OF CONGRATULATIONS.</head>
        <p>CHORUS OF CONGRATULATIONS.</p>
        <p>Councillor Groves: I should like to congratu- late Mr. Gotobed upon taking his place in the chair. I have not the slightest doubt that he will fill it with the greatest credit. . There is nothing against Mr. Warburton, but .must admit I am very concerned: I do not like alterations._ I shall give the chairman all the support possible. •••• .The : Chairman Thank you. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor Parker added his congratulations, saying: I- hope you will not think for one moment that when I proposed Mr. Warburton as chairman I did so with any ill-feeling to- wards yourself. I only did it because I was given to understand Oat each chairman \held office for a couple of years; and I shall give you my heartiest support. The Chairman: Thank you. Mr. Parker, Councillor Cowper: We have had many battles, but I shall give-my support. The Chairman: Thank you.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>NEW VICE-CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>NEW VICE-CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>For the post of vice-chairman,</p>
        <p>Councillor Holt was proposed by Councillors Parker and Cowper. • Councillor Holt: I would ask you to consider how I have been fixed for some time. Some worthy person in another ward should take on the fresh duties. As you know, my son is away at the war, and I find business very pressing; have not been able to gine to the Council the work that I ought. Now, it is hardly advisable</p>
        <p>A THEATRICAL SECRET. HOW TO POLISH YOUR TEETH.</p>
        <p>------- Actresses obtain that brilliancy and snowy white- ness of their teeth with</p>
