      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>frttrrs to the elital CLARA BUTT CONCERT.</head>
        <p>frttrrs to the elital CLARA BUTT CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Great Conlralto Welcomed By Ennio- • siastie and Crowded Audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>COMMERCIAL TRAVELLERS AND</head>
        <p>COMMERCIAL TRAVELLERS AND</p>
        <p>THEllt BENEVOLENCE.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I venture to ask for a space in your columns to draw attention to the needs of the Commercial Travellers' Benevolent Institution, one of the British charities which has stood the test of time? _ _</p>
        <p>Since its foundation in 1.819, ,it has dis- tributed upwards of £400,000 in relief to its members and their widows.. It has upon its funds PO annuitants, at an annual cost of £15,000, over 400 of these are widows whom we cannot allow to suffer want. _</p>
        <p>The commercial traveller plays a vital part in the machinery of commerce, which has built up this great Empire; "he is indispensable." Surely then, this. is a charity of all others, which calls for the support of all successful business houses, and also those who are enjoy- ing their income frdm commercial under- takings. Many give to us liberally every year, but there are many who do not, even in large Manufacturing towns. This institution is admitted to be most economically managed, for out of every guinea subscribed, one pound goes direct to the annuitant.</p>
        <p>Will you help us? We must have £5,000 to. meet our liabilities by the end of April ; I therefore earnestly appeal for help that our anxiety may be relieved. Mr. A. B. Reckitt and his firm, Messrs. Reckitt and Sons, Ltd., Hull, have generously promised upwards of 500 guineas. Any donation, large or small, will be grate- fully received and acknowledged by the Secre- tary, Mr. Leonard Courtney. Offices: 11. Iremonger-lane, London. E.C. ALFRED SENESCHALL, Chairman of the Board of Management. T. PERCIVAL, Local Hon. Sec.</p>
        <p>Glass Works, Bank Quay, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>PADOATE.</head>
        <p>PADOATE.</p>
        <p>Where Cottage Homes • Children Will Be Taught.—lt was reported to the Education Com- mittee, at their meeting on Monday, that the School Attendance Sub-committee had received a letter from the Guardians of the Warrington. Union, asking.whether the Education Authority would allow children resident in the Cottage .Homes, Padgatc; to attend the new school in Oakwood-avenue, and it was resolved that the Guardians be informed that the committee is quite willing to permit the children to attend the school named, if the Guardians are prepared to make a maintenance, contribution according to the net cost per head in respect of the aver- age attendance of •these children.</p>
        <p>Recruiting .—A ~recruitini meeting, presided over by Mr. Geo.. Mather,. was .held 'on Monday evening, on the open space in front of the Padgate Railway Station. • The band of the Farnworth Nautical School paraded the village playing selections, and at the time of the meet- ing a large crowd had collected to hear the various speakers. Mr.'Chas. Bigelow, who is an American, ,said that SO per cent. of the United States population was in sympathy with Great Britain. lie did not think the English people seriously realised the present situation. Seeing that two-thirds of the present Army were married men, this spoke well for the women of England, .though it was a disgrace to the young single men. We must not be deceived by the cry that Germany were short of food. He had a friend who had been through Ger- many and Austria on behalf of the "New York World," and he found that great preparations had been made for this war, and the statements regarding shortage of food were very mislead- ing. Mr. Austin Twist, who said he was a Lancashire lad, made a very strong appeal to every born .British subject to do his best. . Captain Sparrow, of the R.W.F., made a strong appeal to the honour of the crowd. He thought that if anything would move an Englishman it was an appeal to his honour. He had lived in Germany three years, and knew the preparations she had made for this war. He found that the l'adgate roll of honour at pre- sent was 75, and if he Could only secure five more recruits he would be satisfied with the night's work:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Concert. —ln the Wesleyan Schoolroom on Thursday evening a concert in aid of the Church Fund was given by the Albert-road (Widnes) Wesleyan, Choir. The soloists were Miss May Smith and Mr. F. 0. Robinson. Miss G. Brown and Miss L. Gray gave some good performances on the piano. Mr. 3'. Watson was .in the chair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>WAR CHARITY CONTRIBUTION.</head>
        <p>WAR CHARITY CONTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>Monday's concert by Madame Clara Butt and party (brought to Warrington through Mr. R. ll.,,Pearson's agency) was, in many respects. the most remarkable that ha S been held in the -town since Parr Hall was built. Seldom has greater enthusiasm been shown by our towns- , people at any musical event than was evinCed . at the re-appearance in our midst on this 4 4 occasion of a great public singer, after tin' .- absence of something like a decade. True, the • time, the season and the (in part) philaii' • thropic purpose of the concert all weighed on the side of probable success. Yet, against these considerations, and, in spite of Madame Clara Butt's immense popularity, were to be set the hard facts that the exigencies of the .war had made large demands on private purses, and that the prices charged for admission to the concert were unusually high.. It is greatlY . to the credit, therefore, of Warrington's reputa- tion as a musical centre, that the whole enter- prise should have turned out so entirely suc- cessful, and that a sum of over £.1.4 should Rave been contributed, during a ten minutes' inter- val in the programme, towards a fund for pro- viding employment for artistes adversely affected by the war,' which Madame-Butt and her husband, Mr. Kennerley . Rumford, have. with laudable generosity, set afoot. - Not inappropriately a somewhat mournful note was struck in the music sung by Madame Butt, although this was not entirely unrelieved; The excerpt, "God shall wipe away all tears, from " The Light of the World "—a beautifuL but HUI&amp; known work of the late Sir Arthur Sullivan's—proved touching, nay almost poig- nant, in the tenseness of feeling which Madame Butt threw into her rendering of it, and. similarly, into the concluding :phrases of Teresa del Riego's "My Son," the . great contralto vocalist imported so dramatic an appeal that one must be callous indeed not to be in some degree moved by it. The artiste, in the true meaning of the term, was, moreover, shown in the person of Madame Butt, in the easy transition into a more placid style which she made in her sing- in. with infinitely tender feeling, of Mendel- ssohn's beautiful aria "0, rest in the Lord." given in response to a vociferous In the lighter mood of songs such as "Hearts in love," "Timothy," and especially of c: I know my love," Madame Butt showed herself equally at ease, the quaint humour with which the , Patter of -the- last-mentioned. Irish .song was rippled off, quite capturing all listeners. . A patriotic song entitled "The Home Flag," composed by Mr. Harold Craxton, the accora- panist. and the better known and more worthY • "Land of Hope and Glory," by Sir Edward Elgar, ',were led up to as the culmination of Madame Butt's. singing,' and . at this poil?t it was that flags purchased by a sympathising audience were loyally waived throughout the building, the while a vigorous chorus was voiced to both songs by members of the Warrington Musical Society assembled upon the organ gallery. Great heartiness and much loud cheering marked these concluding items of the programme, the whole forming a stirring final° well rounded off with the National Anthem. in which solo verses were taken by Madame Clara Butt and./ Mr. Walter Hyde. It is worthy of mention that vocal members Of the Musical Society made their presence at this concert intelligible by contributing, in the second part of the programme, a quite praise- worthy, and in many respects adequate, per- formance of the choral epilogue. "It comes frora the misty ages," to Sir Edward Elgar's "Banner of St. George," which it will be remembered, they gave at the society's recent concluding concert of the season. In the absence of orchestral suP- • port, the instrumental parts were effectively played on the pianoforte and a small organ, bY Mr. W. H. Payton and Mr. Arthur E. GodfreY respect,ively, Mr. F. 11. Crossley officiating as conductor.,</p>
        <p>Snace precludes our noticing in detail the rest of the items of music making up the pro- gramme, a pleasant task from which we refrain reluctantly, because of our keen appreciation Of the high position in the musical world attained by Mr. Walter Hyde as a tenor soloist, and of our great admiration for Mr. W. IL Squire as a 'cello exponent. Suffice it to say that these two. in company with two gifted young lad.f violinists, in the persons of Miss Molly Blower and Miss Essie Faulkner, gave rich variety to the covert by contributing items of deep in- trinsic interest with conspicuous skill, thereby. and incidentally, earning for themselves the heartiest encomiums. The concert grand pianoforte used on the occasion of this concert was one made by the eminent firm of Chappell and Co., Ltd., whose local agents are Messrs. C. H. and J. A- Dawson, of Sankey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</head>
