      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 1, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 1, 1915.</p>
        <p>"A SURGEON IN BELGIUM."</p>
        <p>First=hand Account of The Doings of German Fiends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>GREAT STORY WELL TOLD.</head>
        <p>GREAT STORY WELL TOLD.</p>
        <p>Infamies for Which Germany Will Be Brought to Judgment.</p>
        <p>The author of this wonderful and vivid narra- tive, H. S. Souttar, F.R.C.S., was one of the surgeons in charge of the British Field Hospital for Belgium, which began its work in Antwerp last September. The book is of special interest. to me because it is written by a member or a Scotch family I know well. Attached to the hospital were eight doctors and 20 nurses, and accommodation in beds for 150 patients. On ()ether 9th the hospital bad hurriedly to leave Antwerp on account of the expected immediate occupation by the Germans. The story of the retirement when they removed the staff, apparatus and 100 wounded patients in seven motor 'buses is of thrilling interest. A fresh start was made at Fumes, near the present lighting line, where the hospital is still at work, and now acts as the official advance base field hospital of the Belgian Army. The author tells of his visitsjo several pliices famous in the history of the war, and the scenes he witnessed are vividly described in the book. The descriptions are so natural and real that I do not purpose so much review- ing the book, but rather will let it speak for itself, simply linking up selected passages. Dr. Souttar pays a warm tribute to his Belgian Patients. Ile tells its " They all did their best to make light of their troubles, while their gratitude for what was done for them was extra- ordinary. These were brave men, and we felt glad we had come .out to do what we could for them."</p>
        <p>By C. E. PARKER. our country. .Fresh air, for example, is to them full of dangers, one would almost think that it savoured of the powers of evil. We went into one huge hospital of the most modern type, and equipped lavishly, and such was the 'atmos- phere that in ten minutes I had to make a rush for the door. The wards were full of wounded, and every window was tightly shut. now the patients could. live in such an atmosphere is beyond my comprehension." Tho4e who have been brought • into contact with our Belgian guests here, recognise the same fear of open windows. The doctors arranged that the English nurses belonging to the hospital should daily take a motor ride in the fresh air, and. to this and other precautions is attributed the absence of breakdown amongst the staff under very trying circumstances. These motor excursions were sometimes used to bring 'patients from the firing line to the hospital. And the doctor learnt and saw- in these. a great deal of what he describes of the conditions of Belgium resulting from the war, lie says: "We learnt more about the condition of the country and of the results of German methods in One after- noon than all the literature in the world could ever teach. If only it were possible to bring home to the people of Britain one-hundredth part of what we saw with our own eyes, stringent laws would have to be passed to stop men and women from enlisting. No man who -deserved the name of man, and no woman who deserved to be the mother of a child, would rest day or night till the earth had been freed from the fiends who have ravaged Belgium and made the name of German vile."</p>
        <p>"HE WAS MY BROTHER."</p>
        <p>He speaks of a visit to Malines on a glorious September .afternoon. Ater describing the points of interest of-this cYthedral town, where the brave Cardinal Mercier holds sway, the doctor tells us of 'his visit to the Belgian. Red Cross establishment, into which, so far, a doctor in charge told him no cases had come. They were talking together and Souttar was about to return when a Cyclist Scout was brought in. He was wounded in the abdomen and his right arm was shattered. "He was in a desperate state, but the local doctor begged me to do what I could for . him, and, indeed, the power of recovery of these fellows was so remarkable that it was always worth a trial. As rapidly as pos- sible we got ready stimulants and hot saline solution to inject into his veins. We had not come prepared for actual operating, and the local equipment was meagre, but we succeeded in improvising - a transfusion apparatus out of •various odds and ends. It did not take long to get it to work, and in a few • minutes he began to respond to the hot salt and water running into his • vessels. Alas! it was only for a moment, He was bleeding internally, and nothing could be done.. I went ()Ver to the priest, who had just come, and said " C'est a vous, Monsieur." He bowed, and came forward, holding in his hands the holy oil. A few 11111r- inured words were spoken, the priest's finger traced the sign of the cross, a few moments ot silence, and all was over. Death is always im- pressive, but I shall never forget that scene. The large schoolroom With its improvised equip- •ourselveS, a •erow&amp; of nurses and doctors standing round, in the .centre the sandalled priest bending downwards in his brown mantle, and the dying man, his lips moving to frame the last words he would speak On earth. It was in silence that we stole out into the sunlight of the courtyard. .Presently one a the soldiers came up to me, and asked about the man we had just left, and who had come from near by. I told him what had happened, and for a moment he did not speak. At last he looked up at me with tears in his :eyes, and said simply was my brother, and this morning we were laughing together." 1 held his hand for a moment, ,and - then he 'turned away and went back to his post."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>MEDICAL AND SURGICAL TRIUMPHS.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL AND SURGICAL TRIUMPHS.</p>
        <p>There are one or two chapters dealing with the hospital work which will be of special in- terest to medical men, but all can understand something of the following:—</p>
        <p>Perhaps even more wonderful is the newer method of spinal amesthesia, which we used largely for the difficult abdominal cases. With the injection,' of a minute quantity of fluid into the spine all sensation disappears up to the level of the arms, and, provided he cannot see what is going on, any operation below that level can be carried out without the patient *flowing anything about. it at all. It is rather un- canny at first to see a patient lying smoking a cigarette and reading the paper. whilst on the other side of a screen a big operation is in progress.</p>
        <p>This, of course, an operation on the smoker's own body. Souttar tell S us the Belgians take chloroform better than his coMpatriots on this side of the water, and attributes it to the fact that they are, 'as a whole, more abstemious. " With surgery on rather bold lines it was extraordinary how much could be done, especially in the way of saving linib. During the whole of our stay in Antwerp we never once had to resort to an amputation. We were dealing with healthy and vigorous men, and once they had got over the shock of injury they had wonderful powers of recovery." Absence of amputation did not ,hold good at Fumes, where the wounds were of a different character. He makes an interesting stateinent about the German bullets, which''sreems curious: "The German bullet is short and pointed, and when it strikes it turns 'completely over and goes through backwards. The base of the bullet has no cover, and consequently spreatis in a manner precisely similar to that in a dum-dum, with equally deadly results. There could be no greater contrast than that between the wounds with which we had to deal in South Africa, produced by ordinary bullets, and those which our soldiers are now receiving' from German rifles. The former were often so slight that it was quite a common occurrence for a soldier to discover ay. identally that he had been wounded some time previously. In the present war rifle wound, have been amongst the most deadly with whi,h we have had to deal."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>FRESH AIR AND WOUNDS.</head>
        <p>FRESH AIR AND WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>The author has a 'good deal to say about septic poisoning, and the following is of import: " There is one way in which all such infections may be defeated—by plenty of fresh air, or, better still, by oxygen. • We had some, very striking proofs of this, for in several cases the wounds were so horribly foul that it was im- possible to tolerate their presence in the wards: and in these cases we made it a practice to put the patient in the open-air, of course suitably Protected, and to leave' the wound exposed to the winds of heaven, with only a thin piece of gauze to protect it. The results were almost magical, for in two or three days the wounds lost their odour and began to look Mean." Our last quotation from the portion dealing with surgery is on the extraction of bullets. The doctor says:—" This brings me to a subject on which I feel very strongly, the folly of removing bullets. If a bullet is doing any harm, press- ing on some nerve, interfering with a joint, or in any • Way eausing pain or inconvenience, by all means let it be removed, though even then it should in most cases never be touched until the wound is completely healed. But the mere presence of a bullet inside the body will of itself do no harm at all. The old idea that it will cause infection died long ago. It may have brought infection with it; but the removal of the bullet will not remove the infection, but rather in most cases make it fire up. . . It is a matter in which the public are very largely. to blame, for they consider that unless the bullet has .been removed the surgeon has not done his job. . . . It may be the mark of a Scotch ancestry, but if I ever ..get ..a bullet in my own anatomy; I shall keep it." We are all aware that Scotchmen are twitted with sticking to silver, but we did not before know his liking applied also to lead. All through filo book the doctor speaks of the kindness of the Belgian people. "(n all sides '.ive were welcomed, with a. kindness and a con- sideration not always accorded to those who are so bold as to wish to help their fellow-men. Everywhere we met with a courtesy and a generosity by which, in the tragedy of their country, we were deeply touched. They all seemed genuinely delighted to see us. from. the Queen herself to the children in the streets. Wherever we moved amongst the Belgians, we always found the same simplicity of purpose, the same generosity of impulse. ' Everywhere we met the same gratitude for what England was doing for Belgium; no one ever referred to the sacrificv w Hell Belgium had made for England. individually they are brave men and magnifieent fighters. They only lacked the 'Organisation which has made the little .British Army the envy of the world."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>IF PEOPLE ONLY KNEW.</head>
        <p>IF PEOPLE ONLY KNEW.</p>
        <p>Speaking of the Belgian hospitals, he notes # Point applicable to both France and Belgium; that is that there is azi absence of trained nurses corresponding to the efficient staff we have in this country, lie says: "In most of the hospitals we visited the nursing was carried on by sisterhoods, and though some of them were evidently- good nurses, most of them had no idea whatever ut nursinga it 4 pr4et4ed in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>AN AWFUL EXODUS.</head>
        <p>AN AWFUL EXODUS.</p>
        <p>We have All read accounts of the sad exodus of thc inhabitants on the approach t of the German Army, but the following scene on the road leading out of Antwerp is So well told that I give it in full :—. On each side of the toad rose poplars in . ordered Succession, and the long straight stretches of the 'road were framed in the endless vista of their tall trunks. And in</p>
        <p>that frame moved o pieturo too utterly</p>
        <p>piteous for any words to describe—a whole country fleeing before the Buns. The huge unwieldly carts of the Belgian farmer crept slowly along, drawn by great Flemish horses. In front walked the men, plodding along beside the splendid animals, with whose help they had ploughed their fields— fields they would never see again. In the carts was piled up all that they possessed in the world, all that they could carry of their homes wrecked and blasted by the vandals, a tawdry ornament or a child's toy looking out pitifully from the heap of clothes and -bedding. And Seated on the top of the heap were the woman and the children. But these were the well-to-chi. There were other little groups who had no cart and no horse. The father and a son would walk in front carrying all that a man could lift on their strong backs; then came the children, boys and girls, each with a little white • bundle over their shoulder, done up in a towel or pillowslip, tiny mites of four or five doing all they could to save the home; and last- came the 'mother with a baby at her breast, trudging wearily through the dust. They came in an endless stream, over and • over again, for mile after mile, always in the same pathetic little groups, going away, only going away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>GERMANY'S CRIME AGAINST LITTLE CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>GERMANY'S CRIME AGAINST LITTLE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>We lowa some little children in Warrington who took part in this weary march. Agaiwa similar sceneis witnessed, and to this one are added the 'horrors and discomforts of night.</p>
        <p>Of all the pitiful sights I have 'ever seen, that road was the most utterly pitiful. We moved on slowly, through a dense throng of fugitives—men, women, and little children— all with bundles over their shoulders, in which was all that they possessed. A woman with three babies clinging_ to her skirts, a small boy wheeling his grandmother in a wheelbarrow, family after family, all moving away from the horror that lay be- hind to the Miserk that lay in front. We had heard of Louvain, and we had seen Termonde, and we understood. As dark- ness came down we lit our lamps, and there along the roadside sat rows of fugitives, resting before commencing. their long,jour- ney through the night. There was one row of little children which will live for ever in my memory, tiny mites sitting together On a .bank by the roadside. We only saw them for an instant as our lighls fell on them, and they disappeared in the darkness. Germany will have to pay for Louvain and Termonde.' It is not with man that she will have to settle for that row of little children.</p>
        <p>Doctor Suultar, as he sboiNs again anti again, has a very teuder heart fur the children, uud</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <p>the needless cruelties endured by these innocent little ones stir him to the depths and call forth his tenderest and at the same time hottest words. In speaking of the destruction of a chateau near Malines, he says : Upstairs the remains were in glorious con- fusion. Apparently .the Germans had • opened all tbi drawers and flung their con- tents on the floor with the idea I suppose of taking anything. One room was plainly the nursery, for the floor was covered with children' ii toys of all descriptions,, all broken. It may be very unreasonable% but that room made me more angry than all the rest of the house. There is something so utterly wanton in trampling on a child's toys. They may be of no value, but I liave a small opinion of a Man whi; does not treat them with respect. They are the symbols ,of an innocency that once was ours,- and tokens of a contact with the unseen world, for which we in our blindness grope long- ingly in vain.</p>
        <p>DEVASTATED TERMONDE. In the centre of the Town's Square were the remains of a bonfire, and all around were broken bottles, packs of cheap cards in con- fusion. Think of the, scene. A blazing town around them, and every now and then the crash of falling buildings; behind them Notre Dame in flames towering up to heaven the ancient Town Hall and the Guard House; burning across the square, and in the centre a. crowd of drunken soldiers round a bonfire playing -cards. And miles .away across the field ten thousand homeless wanderers watching the destruction of all for which they had spent their lives in toil</p>
        <p>But no words can ever convey any idea of the -utter destruction of the whole town, or of the awful loneliness by which one was surrounded. 4 One felt that one was in the presence of wickedness such as the world bas rarely seen, that the powers of darkness were very- near, ;and that behind those blackened walls there lurked evil forms. Twilight was comings on as we turned back to our car, and a cold mist was slowly rising from the river. lam not superstitious, and in broad daylight I will scoff at ghosts with anyone but I should not care to spend a night alone in Termonde. One could almost hear the Devil laughing at the handiwork of his children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>BRAVE NUNS AND PRIESTS.</head>
        <p>THE BRAVE NUNS AND PRIESTS.</p>
        <p>He gives a graphic description of the scene of the destruction by shell fire of the Hospital of St. Elizabeth at Lierre; und here pays a well- merited tribute to the Nuns connected with that hospital:— The Nuns were doing for the wounded everything that was possible under con- ditions of undescribable difficulty. They may not be trained nurses, but in the record of this war the names of the Nuns in Belgium ought to be written in gold. Utterly careless of their own lives, abso- lutely without fear,' they cared for the sick, the wounded, and the dying, and they have faced any hardship, any danger, rather than abandoned those who have turned to them for help. On the train he men were looked after by the priests, splendid fellows, who never seemed tired of doing all they could for the soldiers. One found the Belgian priests everywhere—in the trenches, in the hos-. pitals, and in the trains—unobtrusive, always cheerful, always ready to help. From the brave Archbishop Mercier to the humblest village cure, regardless of their comfort and careless of their lives, they have stood by their people in the hour of their trial. May their honour be great in the hour of Belgium's triumph.</p>
        <p>_ here is a night 'scene from the flooded dis- trict of Flanders, in which the Belgian priests and the Germans for once play a noble part in helping some Belgian wounded who had been left in a farm between the two lines:—</p>
        <p>Two Belgian priests, taking their lives in their hands, walked out to the farm, but they found that the wounded were. beyond their powers, of carriage. .Nothing daunted, they went on to a' farm occupied • by the Germans and asked for help, and a few minutes later the astounded Belgians saw a little procession coming up the road. In front walked the two priests, and behind them came four wounded Belgians, lying on stretchers carried by German soldiers. They came right into the lines, and they had a royal welcome. They all shook hands, and the little party of Germans walked back down the road amid the cheers of their opponents. The spirit of chivalry is not dead in Ger- many; it is only stifled by her present rulers. Is it too nmch to hope that some day its voice may be heard and may com- mand?</p>
        <p>ENGLISH NURSE AND BELGIAN SOLDIER. We close the book with .ft feeling of admira- tion for the braiVe. English "Surgeon in Bel- gium," aml his devoted staff of assistants. Per- haps we who have to stop at home and do our best to hold' the home trenches cannot alto- gether suppress a feeling of envy towards those, who, like the doctor, come into close touch with realities, and are' able to render such service. But this feeling is overshadowed by the admira- tion felt. not alone for the work done. Nit also for the power of this writer in painting such realistic pictures of the events in which lie took part. The two following descriptions can- not but move everyone who reads and feels. The scene of the English nurse bending in pity over the Belgian soldier on his palate of straw, and lovingly administering to his wants by the dim light of a stable lantern, is one that etches itself on the minds, stirs the pulse, and makes one feel it is good to belong to the. English breed. The first scene relates to the beginning of the work at Fumes, the second at the same, when some sort of comfort had been established. For, the first few tights, as a matter of fact, we; had .to sleep in these rooms- on straw ourselves, and in the greatest luxury. No one who has slept all his life in a bed would ever realise how comfortable straw is, and for picturesqueness has it an equal? I went into the Straw Ward on my round one wild, stormy night. Outside the wind was raging and the rain fell in torrents, And it was so dark that one had to feel for the door. Inside a dozen men lay covered up with blankets on a thick bed of straw, most of them fast asleep, while beside one knelt a nurse with a stable lantern, holding a cup to his lips. In was a picture that an artist might have come far to see—the wounded soldiers in their heavy coats, covered by the brown blankets; the nurse in her blue uniform and her white cap, the stable lantern throwing flickering shadows on the walls. It was something more than Art, and as I glanced up at the crucifix hanging on the wall I felt that the picture was com- plete.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>WHICH SHALL BE GREATEST?</head>
        <p>WHICH SHALL BE GREATEST?</p>
        <p>The Queen of the Belgians visited the hos- pital twice a week and spoke kindly words to all, and this is how our author, the doctor, paints the scene " The dimly lit ward, with it crude furni- ture., the slim figure in black, bending in compassiod over the rough fellows who would hart: gladly given their lives for her, and who now lay wounded in the cause in which she herself bad suffered. The Ger- . mans may destroy Belgium, but they will never destroy the kingdom of its Queen. Sometimes the King came to see his soldiers --a tall, silent man, with the face of One who has suffered much, and as simple, as gentle, and as kindly as his. Queen. It was good to see the faces light up as he entered a ward, to see heads painfully raised to gaze after no splendid uniform, but a man.</p>
        <p>When they to history's judgment seat shall come. Which shall shine glorious in the eyes of men, Huge Clermany—or—heroie 13Plgium? Which will be hailed great, Wilhelm or Albert then?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>AMERICAN OPINION.</head>
        <p>AMERICAN OPINION.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Whitham Tells Us What Our Cousins Are Thinking.</p>
        <p>THE BLIGHT OF DRINK.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Whitham, the Sanfranciscan- Warringtonian, sends a contribution to the great discussion of the relative merits of our three deadly foes—Germany, Austria, and drink. It will be valued as coming from one who views a question in which we are closely interested from a distance equal to the width of the Atlantic Ocean.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>"THE DEADLIEST FOE."</head>
        <p>"THE DEADLIEST FOE."</p>
        <p>As I opened my paper this morning the words I have placed at the head of these few lines were the first to draw my attention; and the speaker was David Lloyd George. The Chancellor was replying to a deputation of the Ship Building Employers' Association, the members of which were unanimous in urging that in order to meet the national require- ment at the present time there should be a total prohibition during the period of the war of the sale of intoxicating... liquors. This sliciuld apply not only to public-houses, but also to private clubs, so as to operate eq.ually with all classes of the community.</p>
        <p>The answer, in part, was in these words: " We are fighting Germany,' Austria, and drink, and, so far as I can see, the greatest of these deadly foes is drink." More of the Chancellor's speech I do not care to repeat. It was one long story of drinking, carousal and working short hours. These words will go down in history to the everlasting dis- grace of the British workman. I know by experience, gained not only in my dear old homeland, but in the land of my adoption, what the silent and ofttimes answer is: " The masters have their clubs in which they enjoy themselves, they also have abundance of liquor in their homes; why shouldn't we have a good time as well as them?" • This is no answer, nor yet is the opinion often expressed, "The English workman's habit of drinking is too strongly ingrained into his system for his will power ever to suppress it."</p>
        <p>If this is a correct statTem- ent of the situa- tion, then.thcre is only one cure—" stop the tap."</p>
        <p>" Patriotic " Brewers.</p>
        <p>From my fairly complete knowledge of the brewer and the publican, I am sure I am right in saying that now, more than ever in. the past, they are the loudest-voiced patriots within the British borders—words, mere words. Help the Government to keep your dear land from -L.te tread of the invaders' foot; you cah do it by joining hands with the Gov- ernment in the effort to protect the British workman from the evil that may, or will cer- tainly, result from his own lack of clear thinking. • It really is a fearful condition of things that forces one of the Empire's ablest States- Men to utter these words : " I believe it is the general feeling that if we are to settle German militarism we must first of all settle with the drink."</p>
        <p>I cannot forget these words, they will haunt me.</p>
        <p>Great Britain is but a part of the Empire, but, along with Ireland. is called upon to furnish practically all the munitions of the tvar. Can it be that in the event of a "draw" or that the Allies should fail in their object, the British workman -would care, or like, to meet the jibes and jeers that would be hurled at him? I know the words that would be used; iudsed, I have heard them already : " The. Allies •lost because the British workman is a drunken and lazy sot." Scout the bare idea!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>Opinion in the American Parks.</head>
        <p>Opinion in the American Parks.</p>
        <p>Several times a week I spend a couple of hours in the parks. Big crowds of men con- gregate there; men of all nationalities air their opinions. .• Perfidious Albion and its past history ,are•forcibti- brought to the front, and the shots have to be met with such knowledge as the Allies' supporters may have at hand. During the first three 'months of . the war German Kulture simply went wild. Here, as in Germany, it was said Belgium had just what she descried for allowing the English to coerce her • France the same, and Calais .the goal. The German army, aided with a fleet of Zeppelins, would soon be settling matters in London. • Soon a change came,, and with back to Paris and facing the Rhine, the German army began its slow' reluctant, yet certain march back to its dear fatherland. The change in Europe brought a change in argu- ment in the parks. Ireland's wrongs were told with utmost pathos. Egypt's fearful position Under English rule had to be told, and by a truthful German. From the Soudan to India and her famine-stricken peoples, and so to China and the ophim question. The champion cards of the cultured Germans were the Boer war artd poor old Ireland. ,</p>
        <p>I never knew before how much uncultured British history 1 had stored away, waiting to be used.</p>
        <p>Suppose</p>
        <p>The British Enqp:re expects every titan to do his duty. If Kitchener were coming to Warrington or to Manchester to address the people, and, his subject was to be Might -v. Right, you would not have a ball one quarter large enough to hold him, that is, if he came as the victorious leader of the British Army. Suppose be had to .say that every man had done his duty with the single exception of the men whose work was to provide the ammuni- tion, you would, with -bowed head, cry out, " Mea culpa." "Stop the tap." Now is the time.</p>
        <p>Since writing the above a very pleasing artiele has reached me. It is written by T. P. O'Connor, and the caption is, "England united to end war in shortest time."</p>
        <p>The battle and victory at Neuve Chapelle have convinced the Germans that the British Army when fully equipped with shells will prove more formidable than the Germans ever calculated.</p>
        <p>The article is dated. March 27, and the writer 'goes on to say: -" I became conscious to-day of a big rise in temperature throughout the British Isles por- tending the opening of a great and new chap- ter in the war." The most significant proof' of the new departure referred to is that con- centration on the question of armaments, as explained by Lord Kitchener through Lloyd George to the masses of the people, is an essential necessity if the war is not to be indefinitely prolonged; of giving - Generals French and Joffre the number of shells and rifles which will first demoralise, then over- throw,-the German soldiers, even though they are strongly entrenched, has penetrated even the mind of the careless man of the street. Good. As the Americans say, " I feel fine now.,,</p>
        <p>IL WHITHAM.</p>
        <p>March 30, 191.5.</p>
        <p>The Deacon's Point of View.</p>
        <p>" Then you don't: think I practice what I preach, eh?" queried the minister in talking with one of the deacons.</p>
        <p>"No, sir, I don't," replied the deacon. "You've been preaehin' on the subject o' resignation for two years all' ye haven't resigned yet."</p>
        <p>A Reasonable Request. "Excuse me, madam, but would you mind walking the other way, and not passing the horse?" said a,cabman with exaggerated polite- ness to the lady who had just wad a minimum fare with no fees. "Why," she inquired,</p>
        <p>" Because if 'e :4(MS' Wot: 'C'S been earrying for a shilling ell have a fit," was the freezing answer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <p>rE</p>
        <p>()MING AND MOTORING. Bipycling Exercise As A Successful Cure-All.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>SMALL MAKERS SCORE.</head>
        <p>SMALL MAKERS SCORE.</p>
        <p>jQueries and correspondence should be addressed to "The Hub," c/o_ the Editor. Replies can- not be answered by post, and all queries will be Answered in our columns.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>CYCLING FOR HEALTH.</head>
        <p>CYCLING FOR HEALTH.</p>
        <p>No other form of pastime is so beneficial a; cycling/For young, old, or middle-aged, it is a sport which brings health in its train. The experiences of Mr. Frank Bowdon in this con- nection are very interesting. He tells, in his very readable book, "Cycling for Health, and Points for Cyclists," the story of his return to health, through cycling. Fe use his own words: "Not long before I commenced cycling I had returned in a very feeble condition from a tropical English colony, 'where my constitu- tion 'had apparently been irreparably injured by the debilitating climate and the two long periods of chronic diarrinea, with all its attendant evils. Finally cured of the principal disease by an old woman's remedy, after all the doctors in the ,colony had failed, I arrived in England troubled by an inactive liver, sleep- lessness towards morning, bad circulation, dyspepsia, constipation, pains around the eye- lids, varicose veins, rheumatism, hemorrhoids and general debility, a tendency to catch, cold without any apparent cause; and to, perspire at the slightest exertion. A CURE EFFECTED.</p>
        <p>" Following a Harrogate doctor's advice, I took to cycling, and kept at it steadily during the winter of 1886 and the early spring of 1887, in and near Arcachon, in the south-west of France. Within three months I had thrown away my elastic stockings, and have never used any since. In four months Most of my ills had disappeared, and I finished up my stay in France with a ten days' tour to Bayonne. "During my journeys I walked three or four miles daily in this hilly country, without ,n reminder that I had had. varicose veins. Months later, when I stopped cycling for a time they returned with some of the other complaints in a milder form, and doubtless would have become worse had I not driven them away by becoming a regular all-the-year-round cyclist. Before I became a cyclist I often had very painflil attacks of rheumatism, lasting for days,. but now [never get any."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>WATER FOR CLEANING.</head>
        <p>WATER FOR CLEANING.</p>
        <p>I have received an inquiry as to whether water is a suitable cleaning agent for cycles. It is and it is not. For ,the framework of 1 machine water can .be used sparingly and it is a very suitable method of cleaning. Enamel is quite impervious to water, and a 'damp sponge is an excellent .way of wiping off the mud. If the enamel is destroyed in any way, water should not be employed, since it will reach the metal and set up rust. Such action will in time weaken that point to such an extent that it may give when called upon to withstand auy additional strain. In any ease, the water must not be allowed to come in contact with the bear- ings and working parts of the machine, since it will tend to ,shake them and Make them work harAer. A damp or wet cloth is the best way to apply wateti; the hose should never be turned on the machine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>TWO STROKES AND PETROL CONSUMPTION.</head>
        <p>TWO STROKES AND PETROL CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>When two-stroke engines were first con- structed it was considered an achievement to get the engine to two stroke at all. The question of spirit consumption and thermal efficiency were not taken into account to any extent. There is to-day a very common complaint made by users of two-stroke motor cycles to the effect that they can average such a small num- ber of miles to the gallon of petrol. This engine is attractive.with its lightness, simplicity and suitability as a power unit for a small mount. Thus it is a great pity that the quantity of petrol used is so excessive. I do not want to condemn the two-stroke engine, but to render it really effective 'many improvements are needed. It may be that a special carburetter iS required. Perhaps the position and design require altering, so as to render this engine as efficient as a four-stroke. Whatever is the cause it is up to the manufacturers to determine it and overcome it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>SMALL MAKER.</head>
        <p>THE SMALL MAKER.</p>
        <p>Since the outbreak of war conditions have been•abnormal. This year may be reckoned as the opportunity of the small maker. The panic which was noticeable last August in the motor- cycle trade was, fortunately, short lived. Even in that time it dislocated the ordinary routine of some factories. On all sides orders for material were countermanded. When things came right again, and orders were renewed, makers found the supply of raw material was inadequate, with the result that these works have been disorganised ever since. Many Mg firnth are working to a large extent for. the Government, which interferes with supply to private purchasers. The outcome is that the supply of motor-cycles is not what it is in normal times. This is the chance of the small makers. They only need to bring the excellence of their machines to the knowledge of the buy- ing public and they, will find a market for their cycles. There are plenty of small men who are sufficiently enterprising to push their goods PraPerly. Now is their opportunity. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>SCOTTISH AUTOMOBILE CLUB.</head>
        <p>THE SCOTTISH AUTOMOBILE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The annual report of the above club for the past year states that 199 new members have been enrolled, which after deducting the deletions brings the total membership up to 2,310. The financial position .of the club is satisfactory. sine() it Atows a surplu# amount- ing to £5,351 odd. A recital of the activities connected with the war fornei interesting read- ipg, old the club is to be eungratuluted on its</p>
        <p>t!!</p>
        <p>accomplishments. A motor ambulance fund was started, and upwards. of £70,000 has been collected. This has enabled the Scatish Branch of the Red Cross Society to send nearly 150 motor ambulance vehicles to the seat of war. A very large number of members are on active service,. and it is understood that every road guide in the employment of the club offered himself for service at the outbreak of hostili- ties. It has to be recorded, unfortunately, that several members have been killed in action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
