      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>MR. JOHNSON HONOURED.</head>
        <p>MR. JOHNSON HONOURED.</p>
        <p>A very notable function was held recently at the Horse Shoe Hotel, London, to celebrate the completion of twenty-five years at the Humber depot on Holborn Viaduct, by Mr; E. F. Johnson, twenty-one Years of which has been spent as London manager. Mr. A. W. Gamage was in the chair, and he was supported by Mr. Martin Rucker, Mr. Walter Phillips and Mr. Frank .Shorland. Mr. Johnson was presented with an illuminated address, a complete can- teen. of cutlery, and Mrs. Johnson a diamond ring.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>RACES IN THE PHILLIPINES.</head>
        <p>RACES IN THE PHILLIPINES.</p>
        <p>The first motor track in the Phillipines was opened oit. the 29th of .December last. The " Manila Times" gives some interesting details of the races held on the track on the first occa- sion of its being used. 4 The chief event of the day was a 100 lap race. The winner of this was Mr. William Levan, a marine surveyor. of Tanjuan Luna, Manila, who drove his own touring car. His machine was a 12-16 h.p., and it is stated that he only entered for the sport of the thing. The only alteration made in the car was to take off the mudguards, the screen, hood, muffler, &amp;c% It is impossible to say what speed was attained, since the circuit is not mentioned, but judging by the type of the car used, I take it that the lap was .about three-quarters of a mile. The. following is an interesting quotation from the paper mentioned : " All Baguio had a thrill to-night when at 6.8 the. winning Sun- beam in the 100-lap race was given the check- ered green and white flag for the last .lap by starter Tinan with the aid of the green lens in an automobile tail light, and when--the red lines was turned on the flying car for the final lap of .a sensational event, a cheer rent the air that shook old Mirador and caused a tremble in the walls of the new Dominican building." THE HUB.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL DRINK BILL. £164,463,00 Spent Last Year.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. B. Wilson, secretary of the United Kingdom Alliance, estimates that 4164,463,000 was spent on intoxicating liquors in 1914 in the United Kingdom, compared with 04;166,681,090 in 1913.</p>
        <p>This estimate, includes the following figures:</p>
        <p>UNITED KINGDOM (population 46,407,000). Liquors. Gallons. Gallons. Cost in 1913. 1914. 1914.</p>
        <p>British spirits 31s. 6d. a proof .0 gallon Other spirits ditto 5,680,000 5,753,000 9,060,000 Total spirits ditto 31,844,000 31,694,000 411,917,000 Beer 575. a bulk barrel (II months) Beer 755. a bulk barrel (1 month) . ...... Total beer ...... Wine 18s. • a gallon . • British wines, cider, etc., esti- mated at 2s. a gallon</p>
        <p>03,662,000 95,937,000</p>
        <p>1,999,000 7,49g,000 36,799,000 35,661,000 103,433,000</p>
        <p>11,427,000 10,682,000 9,613,000</p>
        <p>15,000,000 16,000,000 1,500,000</p>
        <p>164.463,000 The consumption of spirits Per head of th° Population .was .68 'gallons, beer 27.6 gallons, and wine .23 gallons, compared with .69, 28.78, and .245 gallons respectively in 1913. The average expenditure per . head in 1914 was „1:3 Ns. 10d., and per family of. live persons 0117 14s. 2d., compared with 'l2s. sd. and J:18 2s. it!. respectively in 1913. Less. than 3 per cent. of the money was spent in registered clubs. •</p>
        <p>The increase in spirit consumption, notice- able during 1913, was followed by a further increase in the earlier part of 1914, but by the end of the third quarter this increase had ceased and become a decline. During the last quarter this decline was almost completely arrested, and a rise began, which has continued during the first quarter of the present year to ,a very remarkable figure. But a -very considerable portion of this excess is due to the extraordinarily heavy -clearances which ate taking place in view of the cowing Iludget</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
