      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>WOMAN'S WORLD.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S WORLD.</p>
        <p>The Importance of Collars and Pockets. Collars and skirts form the two burning questions of the hour, in the realm of dress. New ideas in both appear every day, and are far too numerous to chronicle. But one new collar deserves special notice, for it is both novel and becoming. It has been named L'Aiglon, after the unfortunate son of Napoleon Buona- parte,-and is much nattier than the well-worn collar named after the great French Emperor. It consists of a deep and full pleating of tulle or net, which is attached to a piece of black ribbon velvet, worn almost under the ears. The collar is open in front, and the velvet gives a finish to the neck as it is usually fastened by means of a quaint ornament in paste or jewels. Linen is used for turnover collars, in plain shapes, as well as pique, muslin, and many other materials. Pockets are a great feature of the tailored suit; they are in all sizes, from the small patch to the military, which although large, is more for ornament than use. Slanting pockets are placed on the, fronts and sides of skirts, in imitation of the trousers pockets, and are piped or corded at the edges.</p>
        <p>This Season's Sunshades. Sunshades are to be very much used this summer, as the small hat provides little or no protection from the sun. Transparent brims are to be a feature of this summer's millinery, and they also, although wider, give no shade, con- sequently more attention than usual has been devoted to parasols, and to the en-tout-cas, which appear regularly every season with the flowers of spring. There are a number of new designs, some of which are quite fantastic, others are neat and sensible. The flat Chinese shape will be.much in evidence for every day wear; it is covered with flowered, striped and checked silks. Embroidery, in coloured silks, plays an important part in the higher-priced sunshades, and in these many exceedingly hand- some models are shown. Quite a new shape has at least ten ribs, each of rhich curves up- wards at the outward edges. There will'be as great a variety in sunshades as in collars, and models are shown to suit all tastes and pockets.</p>
        <p>Motor Millinery. The motor bonnet has been deposed from the position it occupied for so many seasons, and in its place reign the übiquitous toque and turban.</p>
        <p>4•4• 4•4* •• 4. 4. 4. 4. 4.* 4. 4. 4. 4. The trimmings of both are eminently practical, whether of ribbon or straw. The motor toque is quite as comfortable as the bonnet, and much smarter in appearance. An ideal model is com- posed of a turban, with kid crown and a close brim, covered with overlapping leaves, made of biscuit straw. A new straw bearing the name of bamboo—from its extreme lightness—is much employed for motor headgear. There are many new materials for motor veils, but ninon is still first favourite; aerophane is used largely in this connection, as in every department of millinery. As a veiling for silk, or crepe de chene hats, it is frequently requisitioned, also for pleated edgings to under-brimmed sailor shapes. Black aerophane is employed in com- bination with tagel and black velvet for slight mourning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>The Empress Eugenie.</p>
        <p>The aged Empress Eugenie' naturally takes the liveliest interest in everything connected with the war, and her dearest hope is to see Alsace and Lorraine restored to France. Had peace been possible in 1871, without the loss of French territory, it is probable that Napoleon and his family would never have been driven into exile.. For over forty years the Empress has made her home in this country, and she was represented at a very pleasing ceremony which took place lately at Cimiez, when the monument to Queen Victoria was publicly un- r piled. French civil and military authorities attended, and poems and songs specially written and composed for the occasion were recited and sung, whilst numerous beautiful wreaths were deposed at the base of the statue, and many grateful and graceful tributes were-paid to the British nation.</p>
        <p>French Women and the War. The war has dislocated social life in France to an extent we hardly realise in this country. There are few labourers left to till the land, consequently vegetables are scarce and dear, and in the country places the baker has dis- appeared, and nearly every family now bakes its own bread. As the French prisoners, like the British, are being half-starred, on German war bread, the women throughout the French provinces have agreed for the duration of the war, to make an extra large loaf once a week for their unfortunate country men in Germany. The bread is not as light as that made by the French bakers, but it is pure, wholesome, and very nourishing. It keeps well, and is in fact good at the end of three weeks, so that it will be eatable for some time after its arrival at the camps, provided the Germans permit their prisoners to receive it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes and Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Fig or Date Pudding. l'ut a handful of odd crusts or pieces of bread in a basin, pour over some boiling water, and cover till soft. Then press it, pour away the water and beat up the bread with a fork till there are no lumps left. Chop up a teacupful of mutton suet and two cupfuls 3f criking figs or dates. Take out any date stones and hard tops and mix all well together with two cupfuls of flour and one of sugar. Add about one cupful of milk or water (the mixture should not be 'very stiff). Put it into a greased basin, tie a cloth well over it, put it into a saucepan of fast boil- ing water, and keep it boiling for three hours. The pudding can be turned out whole on a dish or sent up in the basin. Washington Eggs.</p>
        <p>Ingredients: An equal quantity of eggs and well-shaped, even-sized tomatoes, salt and pepper, a little butter, chopped parsley. Method: Cut the tops of the tomatoes and then scoop out the greater part of the pulp, leaving a neat case; season the insides with salt and pepper. Break the eggs, one at a time, into a cup, slip carefully into the tomato cases, and put a small piece of butter on each. Bake in a slow oven until the eggs are set; then sprinkle with salt and pepper and chopped parsley, and serve on rounds of buttered toast or fried bread.</p>
        <p>Facts Concerning Flies. Absence of house-flies means perfect sanita-</p>
        <p>tion. Both house-flies and stable-flies are carriers of disea.se.</p>
        <p>A fly laden with disease germs is more danger- ous than a bullet. The number of flies in a home is a measure of unhygenic conditions. One female fly can produce 506,250,000 flies in the brief space of one month. To prevent flies multiplying it is necessary to deal effectively with the breeding-places. The best time to deal with flies is in their early stages of development, before they are fully formed. Manure must be carted away at least once a week, and all possible fly breeding-places must be efficiently cleaned at least once a week. Remember that flies breed in manure heaps. stables. slaughter-houses, garbage, collections of waste food, dustbins, ashbins, swill cans, mid- dens, and all places where there is a collection of decaying matter and filth. A mere fly speck may contain the most viru- lent germs of disease and if deposited on bread or other food and taken into the body may set up a fatal disease. Flies may carry the disease microbes of</p>
        <p>mggeOkstogsoo-V)Ascaeo:4o:2dra CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE NED. 0 intswitiosewicoletvots. 244203.61,0 i-w</p>
        <p>FIRST-RATE NEWS.</p>
        <p>Children's Competition To Be Held Every Week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>BEGIN STRAIGHT AWAY!</head>
        <p>BEGIN STRAIGHT AWAY!</p>
        <p>Mt DEAR NZPHEWS in NIECES,— I have important news for you—and joyful news! In a word, we are going to have a com- petition every week onwards, starting straight away!</p>
        <p>Well, now the news is broken what do you think of it? Ala, fancy what a chance it will be for you to show what you can do. What a wonderful range of subjects we shall to able to corer; what a lot of splendid essays we ought to get!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>Ono Condition.</head>
        <p>The Ono Condition.</p>
        <p>But ! (There is a " but" in every- thing, isn't there? and there is in this, too—as you will see): it must be remembered that only those who belong to the Childgen's Corner can take part in the comp'etition. Therefore all whl do not already belong to it should join without delay. Beneath this letter you are told how to become a member of the Corner—it is quite simple-and I shall most cordially welcome every one who joins. _</p>
        <p>Of cofirse you know'that I have already quite a large number of nephews and nieces—and I can remember them each by name; I make a hobby of repeating all their names to myself every Saturday morning to make perfectly sure that I have not forgotten any. So roll up, new nephews and nieces!- And don't be content with joining yourselves; bring your brothers, sisters, cousins, half-cousins. schoolmates, playmates—and anybody else and everybody else whom you can think of! Don't pause a moment; join right now! Ab I have said before—The More The Merrier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>Next Week's Competition.</head>
        <p>Next Week's Competition.</p>
        <p>You will be wondering now about this week's competition? It is this: I want you to pen me a few neat lines telling me something you bare dune to help our (loud Old England in this</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
