      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>Many Women in the Profession. Few people realise that fully 20,000 American women of to-day have qualified for the law, says the New Idea Magazine. Nearer the average conception is the number who have become advocates before the United States Supreme Court-40. Yet it is undeniable that there are splendid opportunities for women lawyers, and the 20,000 of to-day promises to be largely in- creased in the coining years, one of the most authoritative legal publications declaring that women are needed to analyse, digest and classify the quarter-million decisions of Federal and State Courts handed down in the 10 years ending with 1910. The fact that out of 20,000 admitted lawyers only 40 appear before the gowned justices at Washington does not imply that the remainder are engaged in housekeeping or other pursuits. These women lawyers are represent- ing their clients and appearing before the Courts in their own States, doing valuable and remunerative legal work.</p>
        <p>Cheerfulness. A bright, happy manner is an excellent virtue. It is essential for harmony and peace in home life, causing things to run smoothly and pleasantly. - It keeps the heart young, and is a panacea for most ills. Its influence for good is boundless. It imparts joy to the joyless, hope to the hopeless, and comfort to the comfortless. It is as sunshine along the pathway of life— cheering and refreshing to all. Those who are endowed with this attractive gift are welcome everywhere, because they have that which money earinot buy—a genial, help- ful. cheerful disposition, which makes their society a pleasure in life. .</p>
        <p>" 'Tis gentle good humour that Makes life so sweet, And picks up the flowerets that garnish our feet."</p>
        <p>—Henry Lee.</p>
        <p>war. Let me have them, together with your name, address and age, on or before MONDAY, May 10th. I shall not give the prize book [and it is a fine one; you should just see it!) for the greatness of the thing you have done, but for the way in which you tell me about it. Don't forget. Last day for entering, May 10th.</p>
        <p>Your loving - UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN Or Au. A GES are cordially invited to join this Corner if they read it regularly. Application for membership, stating name, address and age, should be made to "UNCLE NED, Examiner.' Office, Bewsey- , street, Warrington." Every girl or boy, whose name is in the Big Book, is entitled to enter the competitions held every week, tofthe winners of which useful book-prizes are sent.</p>
        <p>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK. Grant me the power of saying things Too simple and too sweet for Words. —COVENTRY PATMORE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>QUITE RIGHT, TOO!</head>
        <p>QUITE RIGHT, TOO!</p>
        <p>Impatient Motorist (who is blocked in the lane by a countryman's cart): " Come, hurry up, old Turniptops, I don't want to be stuck here all day." Countryman: " Wa, wot's up wi' thi? Awr doan't see wot tha 'as to grumble abeawt. Aw goin' as fast as thee?! ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>SHE MISUNDERSTOOD.</head>
        <p>SHE MISUNDERSTOOD.</p>
        <p>" Always be definite, Ma. darling," said the fond grandmother. " and when. you are asked a question answer definitely in the affirmative or negative, and always answer politely." Edla listened attentively to her grandmother and determined to follow her direction. 'chat day at dinner grandma said: " Edla, dear, will you have some more beans?" "No," replied Edla. "No what?" inquired grandma, reprovingly. "No beans," answered Edla definitely.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>SILKWORM MOTH.</head>
        <p>THE SILKWORM MOTH.</p>
        <p>The caterpillars of the silkworm moth come from the eggs laid by a moth in the autumn, and in 'Ally Of the following year the eggs are hatched. . The you irg ones are very niull, less than the</p>
        <p>tenth of an inch in length. They grow in size very rapidly; a, great change may be Observed daily. Their 'colour at first is nearly black, later on they become nearly white. In India, the caterpillar of this moth arrives at its full growth in forty-seven days, and has finished spinning in five more, Making altogether fifty- two days. During this period it sheds its skin three or four times, and on each occasion its colour becomes lighter and the head larger. When the silkworm is nearly ready to spin it ceases to eat, and its colour changes to a trans- parent yellow. It then begins *to spin a few threads of silk among the leaves; then a regular oval ball of silk is spun, which is suspended from a twig or leaf. In the inside of this ball of silk the caterpiller continues to work from five to ten days; its cocoon will then be finished and compact. In about a fortnight or three weeks after the cocoon has been finished, the caterpillar within has already entered upon a further change in its existence, the chrysalis state. The final change which ends this strange and eventful history is now at hand: the skin (or • disguise) in which the 'chrysalis has been enveloped cracks and opens—rand the perfect moth appears.</p>
        <p>THE BOY WHO FORGETS. I love him, the boy who forgets! Does it seem such a queer thing to say? Can't help it! He's one of my pets; • Delightful at work or at play, I'd trtist him with all that I own, And know neither worries and frets; But the secret of this lies alone In the things that the laddie forgets.</p>
        <p>He always forgets to pay back The boy who has shown him an ill; Forgets that a grudge he owes jack And smiles at him pleasantly still. lie always forgets 'tis his turn To choose what the Others shall play; Forgets about others'to learn The gossipy things that "they say."</p>
        <p>He forgets to look Sulky and cross When things are not going his way; Forgets someone's gain is his loss; Forget ig in his worktime his play. This is why I must take his part,' Why I say he is one of my pets; I repeat with the whole of my heart, 1 love „him—for what he•forgets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>Knotted Laces.</head>
        <p>Knotted Laces.</p>
        <p>The present is an age of revivals, and one of the most interesting in the world of needlework is the ancient art of knotting. This is supposed to have originated in the fringes that decorated the clothing of Assyrian sculptured figures, and of those found in ancient Byzantine mosaics. Queen Mary 11. was very expert in this kind of work, which she probably learned in Holland, as it was known there many years before it reached Britain. Macrame lace, that was so popular in the 'eighties, is a form of knotting. Another variety is known in Italy, where some fine specimens still exist, as for instance Ragusa Reticella guipure lace. This was worked in gold and silver threads, knotted over cotton cords. In the early part of the 19th century, children were given long pieces of string, which they knotted to form edgings of string lace. Queen Charlotte was an adept at this kind of work, as was also Madame de Maintenon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>Canine War Paint.</head>
        <p>Canine War Paint.</p>
        <p>Some eccentric women are putting their lap- dogs into khaki coats, provided with a minia- ture pocket, and decorated with flags, or devices in the colours of the Allies. IT many instances these decorations consist of buttons, badges and other tokens of war. Even the braid used for binding, often recalls the plaid or uniform of some friend, who is fighting for his country. Dog collars are also very ornamental, and many are provided with a tiny frame for holding the portrait of some absent hero at the Front. ,V7l</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular – Language.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular - Language.</p>
        <p>Alcohol To-day. The day will come, I believe, tvben alcohol as a' beverage will be practically abandoned. . . .</p>
        <p>Temperance reformers have never before had the scientific batking which they have to-day. . . . Alcohol is now given a place among the habit-forming drugs such as opium and cocaine. Physicians often abandon alcohol even as a medicine, and at any rate advise. their 'patients to discard it as a beverage. In a short time the habitual use of alcohol will be regarded by the Medical profession in the swim light as the use of patent medicines. 4n fact, one of the most effective arguments that a reputable physician can make against any patent medicine to-day is that it contains alcohol—as many patent medi- cines certainly do.—Professor Irving Fisher, in March " Good Health."</p>
        <p>The Medicinal Value of Fruit. Fruit may be accurately described as the natural medicine of the body. Does anyone suffer from bloodlessnes or anfemia ? Prescribe grapes and bananas.</p>
        <p>Have you a torpid liver? Take oranges or grape fruit.</p>
        <p>Are the kidneys inactive and going on strike% Take lemonade or limeade freely, but always home-made, so as to ensure that the lemon juice comes from the lemon and not • from the chemist.</p>
        <p>Are you constipated? Take home-made syrup of figs, which is readily and easily prepared from stewing figs. Prune puree may also be recommended.</p>
        <p>Is your blood bad, and do you require a blood purifier? We recommend apples, the king of fruits.</p>
        <p>Are you suffering from brain weariness, and do you feel a lack of mental energy? Then take dates, one of the best energy foods that we have. Perhaps you feel the needs of a general tonic, something to brace you up and make you more fit. A diet consisting solely of fruit for a few days will soon work marvels, and you will feel quite another person.</p>
        <p>Indeed, all the functions of the body are stimulated in a natural and healthy way to in- creased action by the free use of fruit. In our opinion there can be no doubt but that the fruitarian is healthier and stronger and possesses greater powers of endurance than the meat eater and that be is less liable to disease and better able to resist its onslaught when it conies because he has a cleaner alimentary canal, more efficient digestion, more .active eliminative organs, sounder bones, better flesh, purer blood, a healthier skin, stronger and steadier nerves, and a clearer brain. Further. more, he stands a better chance of attaining to a happy, healthy and active old age.</p>
      </div>
