        <p>typhoid or enteric fever, cholera, tuberculosis, meningitis, diphtheria, swine fever, ophthalmia, various Iforms of dysentry, some diarrheas, infantile enteritis, smallpox, and a considerable number of other diseases.</p>
        <p>Lentil or Pea Soup. Soak a pint of split peas or lentils all night in enough water to cover them. Slite two onions (a carrot and a turnip, if you have them), put in a saucepan with a bit of dripping, and fry a few minutes. . Add the lentils, and two quarts of water, or liquor in which meat or bacon has been boiled. Boil about two hours, till soft, and serve with fried bread. Bones or bacon rind improve the soup. No salt Should be added uptil the lentils ari. soft. It is an improvement to pass them through a sieve. A mixture of peas and haricot beans can be used for a change.</p>
        <p>INEVITABLE!</p>
        <p>Are War and Human Nature</p>
        <p>Inseparable ?</p>
        <p>War, it is frequently stated, is a part of human nature; it is inevitable. . . Is it con- ceivable that in the old days such a thing would have been possible as the Christmas truce at the Front? I have heard it said that the men stood so long speaking to each other . . . that it was impossible to preserve dis- cipline . . . and that if it had been allowed to last -! Which was human nature, the thing behind the fearful bayonet charge, or the thing behind the Christmas truce?</p>
        <p>r..,..?7,77TZ,</p>
        <p>At a Cost of a Hundredth Penny.</p>
        <p>The above, passages are quotations from all earnest and eloquent address on " Christianity and the war," delivered at the Friends' Meeting Douse, Warrington, .on Sunday evening, by Mr. C. Ernest Elcock, (of Manchester).</p>
        <p>MEALS FOR WAYFO</p>
        <p>Who could grudge the payment of a hundredth part of a penny for the previi't°, meals for wayfarers at workhouses o'4, centres.? Doubtless. the question is and the majority of people would gbiolTni great deal more to help to alleviate • too joyous lot of the bottom dog, but it esting to know just what the latest (4ewt the kind does cost. A report was read,:v week's meeting of the Runcorn Board of 1/ ians, of a meeting at Crewe of a speci!'w mittee, comprising representatives of the county of Cheshire, appointed to the matter 'of the provision of meals farers at workhouses and elsewhere, anudt stated that the estimated cost of the. was equivalent to a rate of a hundred P3' penny.</p>
        <p>FEWER VAGRANTS. The n m umber of inmates in the. Ruor.n ..her' house. was reported to be 132, and the 11,11'.corf vagrants. was 53, as against -122 for tile sponding period of last year.</p>
        <p>TWO RESIGNATIONS. loot It wlts reported that Mrs. Shalleross the House) had resigned. Miss Potts will'of signing the position of portress at the, 004 house. The matter of filling the poslto left in the hands of the Board. CHAIRMAN AND VICE-CHAIR-</p>
        <p>RE-ELECTED. Apd. Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., who presi"" unanimously re-elected chairman for tbed,o ink year, on the proposition of Mr. SPru'rori ended by Mr. Wright. Mr. Pilling tPelitl ,the proposer And seconder in the comPird, remarks they passed about the tactfliiru which the meetings of the Board had been (luring the past twelve months. Mr. Linaker briefly replied. Mr. Morris moved and Mr. Brown sty that Mr. J. A. Jones be re-elected v tuan.</p>
        <p>It was carried. • ~„ tej; The Chairthan and the Clerk r pointed representatives of the Board Association of Poor Law Unions' mectilit.</p>
