      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>Otijurrij Reim.</head>
        <p>Otijurrij Reim.</p>
        <p>RELIGIOUS HXI`E.</p>
        <p>The Monopoly of the Catholic Church,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>Says Dr. Downey.</head>
        <p>Says Dr. Downey.</p>
        <p>The Catholic Church to-day has a monopoly of *le religious hatred of the world. Baptists do not hate Wesleyans, Wesleyans do not hate Congregationalists, Congregationalists do not hate Methodists. Their emotions towards each other are not sufficiently strong to be termed hatred. But they do hate the Catholic Church. They will sink their differences and unite against the Catholics, through the Press and from the platform, and why is this so?</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. R. Downey uttered the above sentences in the course of a sermon he delivered at St. Mary's Church, Warrington, on Sunday morning, in aid of the school funds. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>Christ's Worst Enemies.</head>
        <p>Christ's Worst Enemies.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Doctor chose as his text: "Art Thou the Christ? tell us. And He said unto them, If I tell you, ye will not believe." (St. Luke xxii., 67). He:opened by remarking that between the. principles of Christ and the prin- ciples of the World there was -a natural and irkeconcilable opposition. There .was nothing surprising in the fact that Christ was crucified, but it was surprising that the religious powers of .tltO ..day rose up against Him. The worst enemies Christ ever had were not his political enemies, but His religious enemies. The great religious tribunal that tried Jesus outraged and set at defiance the common laws of justice and fair play—a tribunal from which, considering they claimed to have power straight from God, one might expect justice if it could be expected from any human source. They did not even carry out the very first principte of justice—the hearing of the evidence against the accused be- fore deciding on the punishment. They were not there to try Jesus and, if He had committed some offence, to assign the penalty. They meant to inflict the death penalty, and were there to find an excuse to convict. Then, they ignored another law ;by convicting Christ on the first day of His trial. There was,a law that no con- viction could take place on the first day of a trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>Cause of the Hate.</head>
        <p>The Cause of the Hate.</p>
        <p>Why did the religious authorities hate Christ? Because He claimed to be unique. If He had only claimed to be on a level with other religious -teachers, or even with the members of the tribunal, His life would have been spared; but He claimed to be above and beyond them all. He claimed this teaching to be above every other religious teaching in the world. That was why they dragged Him up on a trumpecl-up charge. The Catholic Church was hated to-day just as Christ was hated of old. The other churches, the preacher went on, hated the Catholic Church for the same reason that Christ was hated as He was, by the religious contemporaries of Ills .time. The Catholic Church claimed to be unique. The Catholic Church was the church founded by Christ, and she claimed to be above and beyond all others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>Cannot Leave Her Pedestal.</head>
        <p>Cannot Leave Her Pedestal.</p>
        <p>If the Catholic Church would leave her pedestal and admit that she was only • one of many good religions, then the hatred woirld be removed and the right hand of fellowship would be extended by the other churches; just as Christ could have saved Himself by admitting that Ms teaching was only on a level with other religious teachings, and that He was only on a level with religious contemporaries. But Christ would make no such admission; and neither could the Catholic. Church, the great Church of Christ that must follow in the foot- steps of her unique Master.</p>
        <p>. Dr. Downey also occupied the pulpit at the evening service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>SEED AND THE \(W.</head>
        <p>THE SEED AND THE \(W.</p>
        <p>Powerful Discourses At IN .% riffle Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The seed, is the Will of God! I would not for a moment decry social progress; I have lived in a district where they spent two hun- dred pounds an hour on drink, and where out of the seven thousand babies born every year a thousand died within twelve months. Hence it would be impossible for me to be anything but an ardent social reformer. I am all for more leisure, more treasure, and more pleasure; but "every forward movement must be an inward movement."</p>
        <p>So spoke the Rev. William Evans (late of Blackpool and Salford) on Sunday at the Wycliffe Congregational Church., The occasion was the markedly successful .Sunday school anniversary, at which Mr. Evans occupied the pulpit. Splendid congregations were the order of the day, and the collections (taken in aid of the Sunday school) totalled 4:35 18s. 3d. Upon "Flags" Mr. Evans in the morning gave the children a most interesting address, showing by history how crosses represented kingdoms, and eagles empires. "I don't like empires," added the speaker. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>UNSECRET NATURE.</head>
        <p>UNSECRET NATURE.</p>
        <p>The texts of the sermon were Matthew x. 26: " There is nothing covered that shall not be re- vealed, ;mil hid that shall not. he known"; and also 'Mark iv. 22, and Luke xii, 1 and 2. Ono of the great facts of Nature, said the preacher, was its lack of secrecy.; t\he universe was never mysterious, for there was nothing covered only, to he manifested. The door was always on the latch for them if they could find it; the natural man had 310 reserve. Jesus used the fact: (1) As an illustration. The lamp of .truth was there to shine, and must shine wherever it was; if a man was only leading the life of a bookworm in a back room he. Might as well be the most ignorant creature on earth—for wealth dimin- ished by keeping. (2) As an incentive and en- couragement; they did not know what failure was. And. (3) as a warning; the seed was for the soil, and the soil for the seed: Let them follow the Great Revelation! Mr. W. H. Payton playing the -organ, the choir rendered the anthem, "0 how amiable are Thy dwellings" (Faning).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>CHILDREN'S HARMONY.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S HARMONY.</p>
        <p>An inspiring choral service in the afternoon was given by the .scholars of the Sunday school and primary department; the selections in- cluded "The March of Lice"—set to rousing mu-sic by 311. Payton. Mrs. Smallshaw's rendi- tion of "Giver of Life" was beautifully given, whilst Messrs. F. Fieldhouse and W. Booth sang very synipathetically "Watchman, What of the Night?" Mr. W. Brown (superintendent) conducted through the service, Messrs. Payton and T. F. Wallington presiding at the organ. In a short address .Mr. Evans dwelt upon the importance of anniversary—which, he said, was impressing the importance of daily life. " Annus," he continued, signified the thing that was'passing; " month " the period measured by the moon; and "week" a, series. ,But "day" was given to Deus-7-God ! In the daily task their part would be determined not by ability but by capability—the application of their ability. Like Ruskin's, let their motto be: "To-day, to-day, to-day !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>SOUL OF PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>THE SOUL OF PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>At the evening service the choir rendered the anthems, "0 Gladsome Light" (Sullivan) and "I Waited for the Lord" (Mendelssohn);• solo parts were taken by Misses M. Atkinson and E. Hewitt and Mr. J. Ainsworth. Basing his discourse upon "The seed is the word of God" (Luke viii. 11), Mr. Evans said they might of course find the will of God in history if they had the training, or in Nature if they had the ability, or in experience if they had lived long enbugh. But they could -net miss it. It was an old and lutekneyed aphoriszu that the soul of progres:-, WaS the progress of the soul; but it was perfectly true. It. wits deem red con ti ii Util ly through the generations—the great Jewish nation was built upon theocracy. Every- thing "Progress" stood for was in the incarna- tion of the divine ideal. The zenith of our own power was the spread of that ideal; it was not the printing press that made us, but the use to which it was put.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>Little Tin God Fallen.</head>
        <p>Little Tin God Fallen.</p>
        <p>Moral plogre7;sion, continued the preacher, was Proved in the present; we were at war with</p>
        <p>Continued in nexi—c-olu-m-n-,--</p>
        <p>the most cultured nation- in Europe. Some .of them had hoped that the growth of signs and increasing power of the intellectual movement —music and art produced to a great extent by that wonderful country—would have prevailed. But, lo and behold! kultur covered cruelty— Belgium would never be forgotten. It was going to be a great leSson of the war; they had wor- shipped the, little tin god of progress, and the little tin' god hail fallen.. True progress must 1)0 morals:first and worah last; " the seed was the word-01 (Ad</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>CAN HATE KILL HATE?</head>
        <p>CAN HATE KILL HATE?</p>
        <p>.31r. Elcock, at the outset, deplored the ten- dency in these times to say and think the worst of the people with whom we were unfortunately at war. "By greater hate we will kill hate !" That, apparently, was the doctrine of the jingo- ists who stirred up the blood of the people by telling them stories calculated to make them think that the enemy consisted of a race of savages. It was the equivalent of setting fire to all the houses in a street because one house was on fire. What we had got to do was not to try to change ideas by brutality and brute force— a thing that had been tried since the beginning of man's history, without succeeding—but to change ideas by bringing in other ideas. TO cite the' case of Christ. He was crushed by brute force. Here was a Man going through life trying to impress certain principles on the people amongst whom He lived, and finishing up by being crucified—a hopeless failure from the point of view o.brute force. And yet the principles Jesus expounded .in a short time moved a great part of the civilised world. It was astonishing what a large number of people, when the war broke, claimed. to be •students of German philosophy. They got ,Bernhardi's book, and talked about Germany being a militarist people by natural inclination, thirst- ing to go to war With anybody. Those who talked like that had evidently not read the first chapter, in which Bernhardi said . that the reason he wrote the book was that the German PeoPle were se rapidly getting full of peace principles that they would, if 'something was not done, become very soon a nation of pacifists instead of a nation of soldiers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>Is Human Nature, Unalterable?</head>
        <p>Is Human Nature, Unalterable?</p>
        <p>War, it was stated frequently, is a part of human nature,: it was inevitable; human nature was unalterable. That statement, however, was not borne out by facts. Illustrating his conten- tion, the speaker cited witch-burning and tor- ture, which at one time were eommott practices in this country. Then there were the old-time' gladiatorial contests. All these things Were now illegal and thought of wait horror, and yet in the old days a fiendish delight was taken in them. It wasn't really human nature that prmnpted those horrible proceedings; the fine thing that was putting a stop to all barbarous practice was human nature Men and women would always rise to the nobler and higher put before them. Was it conceivable that in the old days such a thing would have been; pos- sible as the Christmas trues. at the Front? A great numbers of officers on. both. sides were sent home and dismissed the service .because of allowing their men to take .part in the truce. He had heard it said that the men stood so long speaking to each otter; as best they could, and exchanging cigarettes and other trifles, that it was impossible to preserve discipline, and that if it had been allowed 'to last _a few hours longer it would have •spread along the greater part of the line. It was stopped, and stopped most de- cidedly, by the officers of both sides. Which was human nature, the thing behind the fear- ful bayonet charge or the thing behind the Christmas truce? In one of his recent writings, Gilbert Ches- terton quoted the words of a friend, who said: "I think' this 'war shows the failure of Chris- tianity." And Mr. Chesterton's reply was: "No, my dear fellow; Christianity has not failed. It has never been tried." ''hat was true. Christianity had never been given a real trial. But that would have to be changed, and when the war was over we should set to work to see that the principles expounded by Christ wexe reflected in our social conditions as well as our international relationships.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>What Men Will Die For.</head>
        <p>What Men Will Die For.</p>
        <p>Why had all the thousands of recruits re- sponded: like one man to the call to arms? There was no doubt at nll that if it had been put before them that they were wanted to tight for more 1811(1 and material possessions, there would not have been anything like the repponse there had been. They were told that a little nation was being oppressed by a big nation. An ideal thing was put before them, and they responded nobly. This WitS an incentive to strive lo'attain something even higher: Because of that response tokthat appeal, we could hope. Men would die for an ideal, when a material in- centive would not move them. The life that was being spent on the plains of France and Poland would live again in the great attain- ments of the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>MEMORIAL.</head>
        <p>A MEMORIAL.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the service the following memorial was extensively signed : "To the Right Honourable H. H. Asquith, M.P., Prime Minister, and the Members of His Majesty's Government. The Memorial of the undersigned, who are the faithful and loyal subjects of His Majesty King George V., re- spectfully showetlp. that we believe that it is possible to establish international relationships upon • a basis of justice, freedom, mutual re- spect, trust and goodwill, and that it is the duty of statesmen of all nations at once to unite in a serious attempt to apply these prin- ciples with the utmost thoroughness: we also believe that a settlement .resting upon these foundations can only be aChieved by mutual consent, and cannotbe imposed upon one people by another at the point of the sword; we there- fore urge His Majesty's Government to take or make the earliest possible oprfortunity for dis- cussing the terms of settlement with all the other governments engaged in this war."</p>
        <p>A German Confession. "For every maltreated German submarine seaman," says "Die Post," Germany must seize an imprisoned British officer and subject him to a tenfold more cruel torture. No middle course is possible. We have the example of the Middle Ages before us; let us-follow it." " This frank confession," says " Punch," " on the part of Germany that she is a bit behind the Middle Ages is illuminating"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>EMPHATIC TALK.</head>
        <p>EMPHATIC TALK.</p>
        <p>The Kind That Carries Convict'Di</p>
        <p>To Every Reader.</p>
        <p>, Conviction must follow such elnlnia-04 as this. Here is an Earlestown case• roliP and see if doubt can exist in the face evidence: 9n November-14th, 1013, Mrs..-E. 186, Crow-lane 'West, Earlestown, 6201 S -31 believe it was a cold which hrougin sharp pains in my back. I began to suffe,oo these pains after my last contineraent'kidepid was a continual ache right across InY especially when I stooped. I knew 112 Y</p>
