        <p>- - Never Called " Baby." 101 One noteworthy feature about NV fr`, that none have been sculled baby:' their earliest years royal children 1.10'„1- e called by their names or possibly by name, but an English prince or Pri,ll-0( I never called " baby " either by relativr'dj his (or her) nurses. From the age 0%., r prince is styled " sir " by his attendottr4 a princess "madam." c 00(</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>HELD UP BY THE WAR.</head>
        <p>HELD UP BY THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Lair-slrect School Building Operations To Stop.</p>
        <p>The Sites and Buildings Sub-committee reported to the Education Committee, at that body's monthly meeting, on Monday evening chat they had had an interview with the con- tractor for the purpose of considering tne pro- posals with regard to the temporary ces.iation of further building operations at Parr-street School in accordance with the request of the Local Government Board that during the continuance of the war oulv the most necessary new public works should be undertaken or proceeded with, if the borrowing of 'money be necessary. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>COMING TO TERMS WITH THE , CONTRACTOR,</head>
        <p>COMING TO TERMS WITH THE , CONTRACTOR,</p>
        <p>As a reSult of .their interview with the con- tractor, the sub-committee recommended that as soon as the foundation level of the, main buildings and approximately sill height on the street side of the special subjects block of the new school shall have been reached and com- pleted, all further building operations at the school should he suspended, and that, subject to the Local Government Board's sanction to the raising of the necessary loan, an agreement be .entered into with the contractor to, among other things, pay him the cost of all work which at the time of the cessation of operations shall be completed, together with the cost of materials purchased by him and remain- ing on the site, or in his, own stores, such total sum not to exceed,£2,soo;and to pay him £5O in consideration of the disturbance of the con- tract, and a further sum at the rate of £l3 per annum to cover the cost of insurance and storage of his materials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>WHEN THE WORK IS RESUMED.</head>
        <p>WHEN THE WORK IS RESUMED.</p>
        <p>Other clauses in the suggested agreement pro- vided that the schedule of prices as contained in the quantities shall, upon the resumption of building operations, be the basis of the prices for the resumed wprk ; that where there is any increase or decrease in the cost of material and labour, either the committee or the contractor as the case may. be, shall be entitled to the advantage of such increase or decrease; and that, upon the suspension of building opera- tions, the responsibility for the care of., the buildings, so far as erected, and the materials upon.the site shall rest with the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>COMMITTEE FAIRLY TREATED.</head>
        <p>COMMITTEE FAIRLY TREATED.</p>
        <p>In proposing the adoption of the minutes, C. Jackson _Holmes said the sub-coinmittee had had several interviews with the contractor and. in view of the special circumstances, they con- sidered that the contractor had met them very fairly. . Alderman Smethurst se.conded.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Jarman wanted to know what going to be the position of the Education Authority in regard to fines for overcrowding, if on .the one hand the Local Gbvernment Board .were goring to refuse to sanction loan., whilst, on the Ipther hand, the Board ,ol Education were going to insist on adequate accommodation for the children. of the town. The Chairman (Counciltor Roberts) said that dere could be no doubt about it that such a position as Mr. Jarman had suggested would be illogical and untenable, but be felt confident that representations to the Board of Education on tlw matter would be suitably met. . Mr. Jarman: It is absolutely necessary that they _should be.</p>
        <p>Alderman Smethurst said that he put the same question in committee, feeling, as heidid, that surely one Government department could not fine the committee for not doing the work which they were willing to do, but which they were previmted from doing by another Govern- ment department.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>IS IT PEACE?</head>
        <p>IS IT PEACE?</p>
        <p>The Controkersy Betvteen the Health and Medical Inspection Committees.</p>
        <p>The monthly' meeting of the Warrington Edu- cation Committee, held at the Town Ilan on Monday evenimg: was of brief duration, the agenda being- gottthrouglt in a little over five minutes.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN (Councillor Henry Rob(rts) re- ferred at the outset to the recent controversy between the Medical Inspection Sub-committee and the Health Committee of the( orporation— with reference to the appointment of a school dentist—and said that he had bad a conversa- tion with the Chairman of the Health Com- mittee, and the result of it was that he agreed to withdraw a minute which his committee had drafted for presentation to. the Town Council, and which dealt with the matter in issue. There- upon, he (Councillor Roberts) took it upon him- self to say that the minute on the same subject, which bad been prepared for the consideration of the Education Committee, should also he withdrawn, the undqstanding being that the whole matter should be re-discussed between the health Committee and the Education Com- mittee, with, 110 thought he might add, a pros- pect of an arrangement being arrived at that would .prove satisfactory to both sides. murmur of applause signified the com- mittee's approval of the Chairman's action in the !natter.</p>
        <p>In proposing the adoption of the minutes of the Medical Inspection Sub-committee, later on in the meeting, Mr. C. E. PARKER said the mem- bers of the sub-committee welcomed the pros- pect of their being able to come to some sensible arrangement with regard to the difficulty which had existed. He was sure that there was a dis- position on all sides to arrive at an amicable and reasonable settlement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>How to Reduce Your Meat Bill!</head>
        <p>How to Reduce Your Meat Bill!</p>
        <p>Make delicious and nutritious puddings with apples, figs, dates; golden syrup, jam, &amp;e.. and Shredded ATQRA..Beef Suet. Always ready for use and saves all the trouble of chopping. Ask your grocer for it; refuse zubstitutos.</p>
