      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>FIELD OF SPORT</head>
        <p>FIELD OF SPORT</p>
        <p>Widnes Worsted By Eight-Point Margin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>VISITORS DESERVED BIGGER VICTORY.</head>
        <p>VISITORS DESERVED BIGGER VICTORY.</p>
        <p>Warrington completed their fixture card on batlirdaY, when they journeyed to Widnes and eat the locals by a. Margin of eight Voints, after a fairly interesting game which, however, Ty attracted 'a very moderate crowd. The Isillt-1840 (3 goals 4 tries. to 2 goals 2 tries)— represented the respective merits- of the Learns, and on the whole Warrington were ra,ther lucky to win, for .the home team did most Ili the attacking, but. were persistently dogged oy ill-luck. On the other hand the Warrington 112. en made the most of such chances as fell their way and pressed them home; iideed, hardly an 4'Portunity presented itself that they did not ,izt,• Widnes held the lead until well in the hair, and kept the defenders severely on tTe'r mottle.• Then; all of a sudden a complete ,ilat)ge seemed to come • over them, The spirit :lent out Of their attacks and their defenCe grew and in a very few minutes the Wilders- wiricl"ds -were enabled to put on the three tries I Inade the game theirs.</p>
        <p>NMe.,E4.I,I)I°CRE DISPLAY OF FOOTBALL. great di be said to have given a really spi can many 3' 2, indeed there were moments—and untin----when the football was of.a very rra.LPPY order. There was also far too much •11,,ez"ing about the game, and most of that kick- -1-we'r:l)l4eared to be pretty aimless. But there • - 8(mle in really incidents, especially the Closingexciting stages, when Warrington were vri,s3' snatching their eleventh-hour victory. first troubled the scorer,. Taylor drop- bear a workmanlike goal. This 'considerably ;artened the homesters, and they made things t aPil fee or Jolley, who worked might and main t°l„'°• his end up and without whom Warring- -2 the earlier -stages at any rate, would -"iv(' been. badly beaten. The second try was an rcitirtg °lie- Bradshaw was tackled over the and, whilst two Widnes players were Llgleavourirtg to put him down. Reid took the t:11 f ;rota him and scored, before the Warring- on quite realised what had happpned. It •a neat wangle, and was deserving of the Oritnatur of complete success, which a hefty. lek bY Taylor set upon it.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S OPENING POINTS. Warrington's first points came per Fearnley, hg lon the interval was close at hand. Follow- a strong forward movement. Tranter tuated aeross and Harding, in trying to kick the ball dead, screwed it, Fernley picked it up .41(1•/r1Pped over right under the noses of the !Igrined defenders. Jolley .put .on the minor t%tits. In the second half Widnes, emboldened ttlit'ir half-time lead, burst off with a rare and for time•the Warrington men had " 1 their work cut out. Time and again a score 40Peared imminent, but Jolley. made .few mis- takes, whilst the :attackers made -many, and dually, play was forced back to the half-way</p>
        <p>Notes by " Centre."</p>
        <p>line, where it hovered, uninterestingly, for a minute or two, until Bradshaw infused a fine spice of excitement into it. Dashing along the wing he dodged the bigger part of the Widnes pack and, just.gs he came up with the doughty Brassington, swung across to Tranter, Who collected nicely and hoppe4 over the line for a really pretty try. Jolley improved with a real Jolleian kick. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
