      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>VISITORS BUSY AND SUCCESSFUL.</head>
        <p>VISITORS BUSY AND SUCCESSFUL.</p>
        <p>Widnes were not at all pleased about this try, and put .in all they knew, with the result that the Warrington men had one of the busiest times of their lives, but gradually the heat of the fray cooled down without any damage being done. Then came the prettiest try of the match and Warrington were the heroes. Young snapped up a short, sharp pass and threw the ball put .to Laws, who cleverly: manceuvred his way to the line and nipped over in the teeth of strong opposition, grounding the ball at the cover. Jolley had another try at goal, but this time his kick went - all awry. A minute or two later Warrington . added still further to their right-side balance. And this score, by the way, was another piece of really smart work. Tranter picked up the ball near the half-way line and started off in swing- ing style. He fondled the leather closely to his breast, and it seemed as if he was going to spoil every\thing by sticking to it too long. But Tranter knew what .he was about, and when he found himself too closely beset he tipped the leather.over to Fearnley, who was able to cross unopposed. Jolley made an excellent job of the kick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>HOW THE PLAYERS PLAYED.</head>
        <p>HOW THE PLAYERS PLAYED.</p>
        <p>Jolley was, as he so often has been durinc, the season now closed, abut the most useful reliable man in -the Warrington team. He was nearly alwqs atv hand when wanted, and he displayed much re§ourcefulness and initiated more than one attack. He was, markedly, a better man that his vis-a-vis, though Brassing- ton played a very useful game; albeit he was at times a trifle slow, whilst his kicking was neither so long, nor so sound as Jolley's. Once he came near to letting his side down badly, but he made good in the nick of time, and just managed to bring Cunliffe (W. of that ilk!) to book within an ace of the line. Young was the best half-back on the field, and was responsible for more than one really smart int)ividual effort, whilst he was also a fearless and hedroit tackler; indeed, this, the last, was in several respects this player's game of the season. The Warrington forwards were. a pushing lot, and a little more mutual understanding would have enabled them to have achieved even a more brilliant victory than was, in fact. theirs. Taylor was the outstand- ing Widnes player, both as ti aggressive and defensive work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <p>Swinton (home) Barrow (away) Batley (away) Leigh (home) Leeds (away) Huddersfield (home) St. Helens (away) Barrow (home) Leeds (home) Salford (away) Oldham (home) Dewsbury (home) Broughton, Rangers (away) Hunslet (home) Swinton (away) - 2 11 Widnes (home) Dewsbury (away) Runcorn (home) , 29 5 Rochdale H. (away) Batley (home) Wigan (home) Wigan (away) Widnes (away)</p>
