      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>LEAGUE TABLE.</head>
        <p>THE LEAGUE TABLE.</p>
        <p>The N.V. League Table, including last Satur- day's results, stand's as follows:</p>
        <p>For. Agst. Per P.W. L.D. G. T. Pts. G. T. Pts. cent. Huddersfqd 34 2.8 2 4 144 200 888 47 47 235 88.23 Wigan 32 25 6 1 104 157 679 39 42 204 79.68 Leeds Rochdale H 35 24 9,2 46 72 308 28 4g 196 71.42 Hull . . 36 24 11 'l. 105 164 702 53 65 301 68.05 Broughton B. 30 18 11 1 46 72 308 44 67 289 61.66 St. Helens 33 20 13 0 46 95 387 60 74 342 60.60 Halifax Oldham 34 17 13 4 66 81 375 62 59 301 55.88 Wakefield T. 32 17 14 1 45 73 309 56 76 340 54.68 Hull K.R 34 17 15 2 61 84 374 51 74 324 52.94 Widnes 32 14 15 3 54 61 291 45 67 291 48.43 Warrington 32 14 15 3 43 52 242 40 81 323 48.43 Batley Leigh Swinton Dewsbury Hunslet BramleY Bradford Salford Barrow York Keighley Runcorn</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>" A " TEAM'S SHORT SEASON.</head>
        <p>" A " TEAM'S SHORT SEASON.</p>
        <p>The wartiiut an early termination to the "A" team's fixture list, so many players having joined the colours that it was found practically impossible to put anything like a satisfactory second string into the field, whilst difficulty was also experienced on the score of expense, reserve team matches in Warrington, as elewhere, fail- ing to find anything like satisfactory "gates" in these days.</p>
        <p>The "A" team matches played resulted as follows :</p>
        <p>Points. Opponents. \ For. Agst. Leigh "A" (home) Broughton R. "A" (away) Rochdale H. "A" (away) 3 23, Oldham "A" (home) Salford "A" (away) . ' 3 12 • Swinton "A" (away) St.. Helens Rees. (home) • ' 3 10</p>
        <p>It will be seen from the foregoing list that of the F even "A" matches played, Warrington won two, lost four and drew one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>DRAIN OF WAR.</head>
        <p>THE DRAIN OF WAR.</p>
        <p>When, at the commencement df the season, the question of." carrying-on" as usual in face of the war was discussed by the N.U. League Committee, Warrington were one of the clubs who favoured the abandonment of the season's fixtures, but, finding themselves in the-min.ority on the question, the committee decided to.do all in their power under the circumstances to put as good a team as possible in the field, and to provide local enthusiasts itvith football that should be as nearly as it could be up to the standard to Which WildersPool patrons have been accustomed for years past. To that end there were frequent re-shufflings in the team, particularly in the forward line, several more or less new and unknown players being given trials..</p>
        <p>No fewer than 25 of the. Warrington players offered their services to King and country dur- ing the season, a very creditable percentage. Two of the men who had doffed the club's colours for the King's uniform were able to re-visit the scene of their former triumphs, and to figure once more in their old places in the Warrington team: viz.: Sergeant Skelhorn and Bombardier Chesters, both of whom were able to demonstraN to the entire satisfaction of large and enthusiastic. crowds that their military training had, if anything, improved their foot- ball form:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>RYLANDS'S RECS. RECORD.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS'S RECS. RECORD.</p>
        <p>Only Three Ordinary Matches Lost</p>
        <p>During The Season.</p>
        <p>Rylands's Recreation Rugby Football rl-ib had a most successful .season, losing only three of the twenty-two matches which they played, as distinct from N.U. qualifying and cup matches, a record of Which they may well be proud. All three matches they lost were played away, and when they entertained the same teams on their own ground in Gorsey-lane, they exacted revenge by beating two of them and drawing with the third.</p>
        <p>The teams whom they beat both at home and away were: Cadishead, Weaste, Seedley Ran- gers, Broughton Central, Ma'ndlebergs, Bradford and Clayton, and Swinton St. Mary's. The teams which beat them away were Ward- ley, Pendleton and Swinton Park, but the Recs. later on defeated Pendleton and Swinton Park on the Gorsey-lane ground.. Only two of their matches were drawn, those with Wardley and the Anchor Cable Works team from Leigh. The drawn game with the Anchor Cable Co. was played at home, but when they met the Leigh club .at' Leigh, the Rylands's men comparatively easily. disposed of them.</p>
        <p>The Rylands's Club finished second in the Manchester and District Rugby League, Ward- ley coming out at the top of the table. Owing to the war, however, the usual medals will not, this year, be given to the runners-up. The " Rees " also ran into the last stage in the Northern Union Qualifying Competition, but. were eventually beaten by St. Helens Recrea- tion, on the Gorsey-lane ground. They entered for the Salford Charity Shield and got into the semi-final round, but owing to the char-a-bane, in which they were travelling to fulfil an engage- ment on Good Friday, breaking down, they were unable to play, and the points were awarded to Weaste.</p>
        <p>There was no Lancashire Junior Cup Com- petition; a matter of some disappointment to the Recs., for they have generally gone in for it, and have done pretty well. Thirty-six mem- bers of the club have joined the fsirces, and two of them—Joseph Welldon and F. Singleton have been killed at the Front. Another member of the club who "joined up "—D. Slater—is at -nreseht a prisoner in Germany. So far as is known, none of the soldier-members have been wounded.</p>
        <p>H. Povey, who left Rylands's in December. has turned out a good capture for Broughton Rangers during the latter half of the season.</p>
        <p>CRICKET. War Effects Upon The Prospects For</p>
        <p>The Season.</p>
        <p>It looks as if no matches will be played, but attempts will be made to arrange friendly games.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Wallington (president of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club) made the above statement to our representative the other day, in reply to an inquiry as to the pros- pects of the club for the season that starts to- day.</p>
        <p>ADVISABILITY OF "CARRYING ON." At the annual meeting of the club, held on March 22nd, a letter was read by the Cricket Secretary (Mr. Bertram Silcock) from the Man- cheater and District Cricket Association, an- mouncing that that body had held a special . , , meeting to take into consideration the war and its effect on local cricket, and the advisability or otherwise of "carrying on" during the coming season. At that meeting the following resolution was passed: "Believing that the playing of local cricket is not incompatible with the feelings of the majority and that it is neees- iary in the interests of the game, this meeting expresses the opinion that as far as possible matches, already arranged for, should be played and that if that is found impossible other fix- tures shall be arranged." Mr. Wallington suggested, at a meeting of the Warrington Club's Committee on Monday evening, that for whatever matches were played during the year, the gates should be thrown open to the public free of charge. The suggestion, however, was not widely en- tamed.</p>
        <p>'CALLED TO THE COLOURS. The committee were empowered, at the annual meeting already alluded to, to arrange what matches they could, but owing to the drain on the ranks of all the clubs affiliated with the Manchester Association, consequent on the call to arms, great difficulty has, been experienced all round in obtaining a sufficient number of players to warrant a club secretary's making definite engagements. A large number of the members of the Warrington Club have joined the colours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>TENNIS SECTION. ...</head>
        <p>THE TENNIS SECTION. ...</p>
        <p>There will also be a big slump in tennis play- ing. The games will practically be confined to practice and friendly matches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>SLACKER.</head>
        <p>THE SLACKER.</p>
        <p>Tennis Club's Effort To Point The Way To Duly..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>APPROVAL FROM THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>APPROVAL FROM THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>The Grappenhall Tennis Club have passed a resolution only to "accept new male members who give satisfactory reasons for their. inability ,to serve their country either through health, age, Government work, or special business." The resolution has been adversely criticised by members of other local clubs. All the appli- cants for membership of the Grappenhall Club .were considered to have valid reasons for not enlisting, so that none were refused. SLACKERS DELAY DAY OF VICTORY.</p>
        <p>Lanee-Corporal J.-Swinton, of Orford, and a member of the Grappenhall Club, writing from the Front last Saturday, says' he is pleased to hear that the resolution has been adopted. "It is to be hoped," he adds, "that the other local clubs will follow your excellent lead. There .are plenty of their younger male members who, I know personally, are eligible in every way to serve their country. They evidently think tennis service much easier, and also that there is less chance a getting hurt. Their love for their country counts as much as love 'in their game, i.e., nil.</p>
        <p>„ "They prefer to stay at home to amuse(?) their, lady friends; whilst others fight and often go under in their gallant efforts to protect•the fair fame of English women. I often wonder if they have read the official acconhts of the awful outrages committed by the Huns out here. Bad as these have been, they would be nought in comparison with what England and her women and children would suffer if the Huns got into our country.</p>
        <p>"Those who have come out here have given the Huns a taste of their fighting qualities. Judg- ing by the feeling'ent here, some people at home (the slackers) are in for a taste of their talking qualities when this job is over. "The future position of the stay-at-home-and- enjoy-myself 'patriot' is typically represented by the illustration on the front page of the Bystander.' It shows a wounded officer in khaki, sat on a couch, surrounded by three lady (friends. Judging by the latter's eager attention, they are evidently intensely interested in the officer's narrative. In the background are standing two gentlemen in evening dress, looking very much 'out of it.' They realise that the ladies have forgotten their existence entirely. A good way to make, the slacker feel his position would be to post the 'Bystander' to him, and ask his opinion of the illustration on the front page. Ile would have to be excep- tionally dense not to 'take the hint. I knew there are plenty of good fellows whose duty keeps them at home; there will be a hearty grip of the hand for them, and a word of praise for keeping Business as usual.' For the Pleasure as usual' class there will be the cut direct' in Mr. Tommy Atkins' best style. "The 'slackers'. will not keep us from win- ning a sure and glorious victory, butt hey will lengthen the date of our home-coming to dear old England."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
