      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <p>To-day's Final In The " Starkey " Cup.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0285" n="art0285">
        <head>SOME EVENING MATCHES.</head>
        <p>SOME EVENING MATCHES.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the Walton and Longford United Clubs arranged to 'play two games on the Wal- ton ground. The first game proved to be a very one-sided affair, the visitors registering five goals in the first portion. The second half was contested in rather a tame fashion, and the visitors only succeeded in adding another goal to their credit, winning the first hatch by six goals to nil.</p>
        <p>The second game was entered upon without any change being made in the constitution of the teams, and the, home team gave a much imprcved exhibition, Berry and Mortimer de- fending splendidly. Marsh, beating the visitors' backs, scored a splendid goal with a shot which completely beat Sturgess. They retained that lead at the interval. The last portion was en- tered upon very confidently by the Waltonians, and the visitors becoming excited in their enk &amp;avows to get on equal terms indulged in too much long kicking. This game just suited the home defenders. The United were unfortunate in losing the services of Stephens. He quite accidentally received the full force of a strong clearance by West on the wrist, which, on being examined by a doctor, was found to be disk located. He will be unable to follow his em- ployment for a few weeks. It was only in the closing stages of the game that the United equalised the score by a shot from Wainwright. This success stimulated the visitors to make further efforts, and a shot from Hughes was partly saved by Barlow, but the latter did not sufficiently deflect the course of the ball, and it entered the net and gave the visitors the lead. In the last minute of the game the home team became dangerous, but failed to press home their attack, and thus had to retire defeated in both games.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the Fairfield United team were due to .play the Mission at Earlestown, and the secretary of the United Club may be sympa- thised with, as, after getting promises from the players to travel by train as arranged, he arrived at Bank Quay Station to find only five players to meet him. As it was impossible to play such an important fixture with so few players, a telegram was sent to the Midsion officials intimating that the United were not travelling.</p>
        <p>_Mr. W. Hunter, the old Warrington Albion footballer, and this season a popular member of the Fairfield United team and also a member of the Warrington Conservative Cricket Club, has joined His Majesty's Forces as an engineer. He .hops to ,serve his country as well in the ,Present straggle as. be has served ,the various clubs he has previously been connected with in sport in the town. We are sure all his admirers will join us in wishing him good luck in his new occupation and a safe return.</p>
        <p>Last Monday night the Orford White Star and Longford United teams met to play their return League engagement, ;and after a keen and strenuous game they shared the points.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night the monthly meeting of the Warrington and District League Committee was held at the Lion Hotel, Mr., J. Wright presid- ing.</p>
        <p>Arrangements were made ,and officials ap- pointed for the klal tie of the "Starkey" Cup Competition to-day. It is expected that Mr. Starkey, the donor of the cup, will attend the match and make the presentation to the win- ners at the close of the game.</p>
        <p>The Fairfield United Club agreed to mnk(. the journey to Earlestown to-day and play the Ws- sion two games.</p>
        <p>Notes by " Linesman."</p>
        <p>team on level terms. The last few minutes were very exciting. The visitors got away on the left and Hughes caught the ball on the rebound, after hitting the crossbar, and put the United in front. The home team made a strong effort to equalise in the last minute. Result: Longford, 2 goals; Walton, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>LEAGUE TABLE TO PATE. • P. W. L. D. For. Agst. Ps. *Fairfield United Orford White Star Longford United *St. Barnabas' Earlestown St. J. 31 l5 7 5 3 34 23 17 Paddington</p>
        <p>Hollins- Green Warrington Central l9 512 2 31 47 12</p>
        <p>Walton</p>
        <p>10 4 4 2 16 20 10</p>
        <p>Elmwood Cons. Grappenhall *Fairfield United and St. Barnabas' have had 2 points deducted for playing ineligible players.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0287" n="art0287">
        <head>WALTON v. LONGFORD UNITED.</head>
        <p>WALTON v. LONGFORD UNITED.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on Saturday on the Walton ground to play two games in the above competition, when the. following players turned out:—</p>
        <p>Walton: Barlow: Berry and Mortimer; Sid- dall, Andrews and West; Cartwright, Beswick, Marsh, Brereton and Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>Longford United: Sturgess; Mason and Holden; Stephens, Smart and Fearnley; Wain- wright, Sherratt, Ecelestun, Bennett and Hughes. Referee, Mr. J. Smith. , _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>'The home team won the toss and decided to play up the slope. Eccleston set the ball roll- ing and the game commenced very evenly, the defenders on both sides being early in action., with Berry the most prominent. At the end of fifteen minutes' play the visitors opened their score from a corner kick, Bennett putting through. The visitors were the more consistent team, and Bennett sending in a long shot, Bar- low allowed the ball to enter. the net, after par- tially saving, and the same player a few i min- utes later added a third. goal; The home team retaliated and Paid-several visits to their oppo- nents' goals, and Wilkinson gave Sturgess fine shot to save. The visitors again attacked the home goal, and,reeleston scored the fourth goal and Bennett the fifth. Half-time: Long- ford. 5 goals; Walton, nil. After the change of ends the game was very evenly contested for about ten minutes. when the visitors broke away on the left and Hughes sent in a long shot, which beat Barlow all the way, the ball just passing inside the upright. After this reverse the home team on several occasions became very dangerous. Wilkinson was the most prominent forward, testing Stiirgess several times with good shots. At the end of the game the score was: Longford, G. goals; Walton, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0288" n="art0288">
        <head>WALTON v. LONGFORD UNITED.</head>
        <p>WALTON v. LONGFORD UNITED.</p>
        <p>In the second game the same players again took part. The home team started, and for the first ten nlinutes the play was confined to the visitor? half, after which play was transferred to the home end, but Berry and Mortimer de- fended well, and Marsh, receiving the ball in midfield, beat the opposing backs and scored for the home team with a remarkably fine shot. The visitors now played desperately and tried hard to get on equal terms, but the home de- fence held out well, and •about this time Stephens, in attempting an individual run, placed the ball a shade too far and West cleared with a hard drive. The ball hit Stephens on the wrist and sprained it badly, causing the player to retire for.the rest of the game. Half- time: Walton, 1 goat; Longford, nil. In the second portion the Visitors were handi- capped by having only ten players, and for a time the home team more than held their own. Sturgess had several awkward shots to deal with, but was successful in saving his citadel from downfall. In the closing stages of, the game Wainwright got away on the right, and after beating Mortimer managed .to put his</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0289" n="art0289">
        <head>ORFORD WHITE STAR v. LONGFORD UNITED.</head>
        <p>ORFORD WHITE STAR v. LONGFORD UNITED.</p>
        <p>The above teams played their return fixture on M. to!ay night, on the Fairfien United ground, padgate-lane, before a good ,number of spectators. The clubs were fairly relliesented by the following players White Star: Millar; Pilling and Evans; Gifford, Ford and Parr; Smith, Connor, Meachin, Stubbs and Hankinson. Longford: Sturgess; Mason and Holden; Fearn! Sherratt and Wainsveight; Finch, Eccleston, Bennett and Hughes.</p>
        <p>The White Star set the -ball in motion with the wind in their favour, and the game opened very keenly. The defence on both sides were quickly called upon to clear their lines. By some good passing movements the home team set up a strong attack, Connor leading the way and passing out to Stubbs and Hankinson the latter, though strongly appealed against for off- side, opened the score for the Star. The visitors retaliated in a strong manner, and from a splendid overhead kick Eccleston equalised the scores. Following this both teams - played a strenuous game, but failed to, penetrate the defence again, the interval arriving with the scores one goal each. On re-starting the visitors Were the first to become dangerous, and quickly ousained a couple of corner kicks, but failed to increase their lead. The Star took up the offensive, but SturgesS and his backs proved equal to the occasion. Some keen play was witnessed in mid- field, but no further goals were scored, and time was called with the result: Orford White Star, 1 goal; Longford 'United, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0290" n="art0290">
        <head>FAIRFIELD UNITED v. LONGFORD UNITED.</head>
        <p>FAIRFIELD UNITED v. LONGFORD UNITED.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night the above teams met (in the ground of the former in their return League fixture, before a good crowd of -spectators, with the following players : Fairfield United: Potter; Bebbington and Kelley; Gleave. Ditchfield and Payne; .Wood- house, Lewis, Millward, Whitfield and Taylor. Longford: Sturges; Holden and Mason; Fearnley. Smart and Sherratt; Houghton, Smart, Eccleston, Parr and Hughes. Referee, Mr. J. Smith.</p>
        <p>The Fairfield captain won the toss and de- fended ' the goal nearest the railway. The visitors commenced operations with only nine players and, despite this disadvantage, they. were the first to become aggressive. The home defenders not settling down to their usual game, were early in difficulties, but they rallied and succeeded in clearing their lines; Play was then transferred to the visitors' end. where 'offside against Taylor gave relief. Both teams were now at full strength, and some keen and strenuous play was witnessed, the ball travel- ling very quickly from end to'end. The home half-backs gradually gained supremacy, and from a quick return by Ditchfield, Mason, in attempting an overhead clearance, had the mis- fortune to put through his own goal, thus giving the Fairfield team a lucky. lead. • From the centre kick the Longford lads made tracks for the home goal, and Potter only cleared in the nick of time. Eccleston returned the leather, and Smart, jun., was offside and spoilt the effort. Following this there were some keen tussles in midfield, Ditchfield and Fearnley• being in the thick of it. The home forwards threatened the most danger, but Holden and Mason defended splendidly, and the visitors' forwards were not allowed much scope. They frequently kicked too strong and Bebbington and Kelley had not much difficulty in clearing. The interval arrived with Fairfield leading by one goal to none.</p>
        <p>The game was quickly resumed, and the Players continued to 'play a very keen game. The home forwards were smartly checked on several Occasions by Mason and Holden, while Smart at centre-half was working very hard. After a visit to the home quarters had been cleared the home forwards combined well to- gether, and were soon at the visitors' end again, when a low centre from Woodhouse was met by Taylor, who sent the ball past .Sturgess with a low shot, and 'made the score two goals for Fairfield. After this reverse the visitors made stronger efforts to reduce the arrears, and the home goal was hotly besieged, when Ditchfield, in attempting to clear a grand centre from Hughes, kicked through his own goal, and scored for Longford. From the centre kick both teams played strenuously, and the visitors were very near to equalising the score, the home team getting off very luckily. Play was soon in the visitors' goal area, and a couple of corner kicks were gained, but nothing came from them. Bebbington, Kelley, Ditchfield, Whitfield and Taylor for the home team, and Mason, Holden, Fearnley, Smart and Hughes for the visitors were the pick of the teams. Result: Fairfield, 2 goals; Longford, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0291" n="art0291">
        <head>ITCHES FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>ITCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>• " STARKEY " CUP FINAL TIE. On King and Queen ground, Padgate-lane. Longford -United v. Paddington. Referee, Mr. E. Aldred, Newton-le-willows. Linesmen. M. .1. Kite and B. Howell.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Earlestown Mission v. Fairfield United; Mr. S. C. Essenhigh. • Walton •v. Orford. White Star; Mr. J. T. Hadkiss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>SHOOTING MATCH.</head>
        <p>SHOOTING MATCH.</p>
        <p>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB v. FLETCHER,</p>
        <p>RUSSELL AND CO.</p>
        <p>A match took place at the Appleton range on Saturday, and resulted in a win for Apple- ton by 29 points. Conditions, 12 men a.side; 10 shots each at a decimal target. Scores:— Appleton: W. J. Fetherstone 77, F. J. Grocott 93, Harrison 87, I. Broady 77, F. C. Bennion 92, H. Christopher 95, H. W. D. Cook 80, J. W. Elliott 93, F. Tole 97, H. Baines 95, A. Shepherd 95, TT. C. Westnioreland 92-1,073. Fletcher, Russell and Co.: J. Buchanan 99, J. Phillips 87, G. MePhio 98, J. Towers 96, J. Quarmby 80, A..Dennett 86, A. Mason 89, W. Harrison 77, S. Gleave 82. IT. Whitley 90, E. Daintith 89; J. Colley 91-1,014.</p>
        <p>To Save the Dog. An inhabitant Of Cologne has been fined £3 for giving war bread to his dog. The proceed- ings were instituted., we understand, at the in- stance of the local Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to_Animals.—",Punch.",</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <p>IYELCO FREE GIFT PURE BRITISH COCOA SAVE THE COUPONS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <head>WITH IHE WOODS.</head>
        <p>WITH IHE WOODS.</p>
        <p>Ezwling Notes, News and</p>
        <p>Comments.</p>
        <p>BY THE JACK."</p>
        <p>On most bowling greens in the district may now be heard each evening the click of wood on wood, for the trundling season has com- menced, and although in these days of war it may not provide so many keenly-contested match games for more or less valuable prizes, as in happier times, yet, nevertheless, the ancient game will be much resorted to as forming a legitimate and well-earned means of quiet and healthful recreation for thousands of the workers of the town. The local bowling season officially opened on SaturdaY afternoon; when the weather was about as unpropitious as it could well be, for the time of the year, and caused the wholesale abandonment of the games that had been arranged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0295" n="art0295">
        <head>BAD WEATHER DELAYS WORKS' ASSOCIATION'S START.</head>
        <p>BAD WEATHER DELAYS WORKS' ASSOCIATION'S START.</p>
        <p>The Works' Amateur Bowling Association had proposed half a dozen fixtures in their tourn- meat, but the unpropitious weather disposed, and the games were rellictantly postponed until to-day, when, if the glorious climatic conditions Prevailing at the moment of writing continue, the following teams will meet on the green at the Association's Headquarters, with Mr. S. Shave as referee: Walker's No. 1 v. Pen- keth Tannery; Crosfield's Chemicals v. Fletcher, Russell's; Cheshire Lines No. 1 v. Longford No. 1; Longford No. 2 v. Orford Tan- nery; Grappenhall Tannery v. Randall Bros.' Tannery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0296" n="art0296">
        <head>CURTAILED PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>A CURTAILED PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>The members of the bowling section of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club have—as was hinted would be the case at their annual meeting—decided on a much curtailed pro- gramme this season, the feeling of the majority of them being, apparently, that whilst the health-giving exercise afforded by the good old game is as necessary now as ever it was to keep the bowlers fit for-their work, the times are not propitious for the holding of competitive matches outside their own ranks, which are calculated to absorb a lot of time and money that can be better expended in other directions. To that end, their committee this week decided to hold no outside matches at all, but nmsrely to arrange a short, but as attractive as pos- sible a programme of matches for the benefit of their own members, such matches to be played on Saturday afternoons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0297" n="art0297">
        <head>DISAPPOINTED CONSERVATIVES.</head>
        <p>DISAPPOINTED CONSERVATIVES.</p>
        <p>There was a disappointed knot of would-be players in the shelter on the Conservative Club green at the rear of Sankey-street on Saturday afternoon, for arrangements had been made to hold the opening main of the season, but the objectionable weather rendered the making of a start quite out of the question, save at the cost of wet jackets and damage to the carefully prepared green. However, a little formal open- ing ceremony was held, Mr. Jno. Wells, the chairman of the Bowling Green Committee, at- tending and making a brief speech on the sub- ject of games in, war time, in the course of which he mentioned that in common with the other bowling organisations in the town, that in connection with the Conservative Club would this year carry on its operations in an un- ostentatious manner. Breaking off his remarks, Mr. Wells then literally set the ball a-rolling for the season by trundling it across the green both ways ~and sending the woods after it. following which he went on to pay a compli- ment to the manner in which the club steward, Mr. Holland, had prepared and looked after the green, bringing it to such a pitch of excel- lence that it was now second to none in the town. At the close of his remarks Mr. Wells was accorded a vote of thanks for his services in formally opening the green, Mr. J.‘ll. Gittens proposing and. Mr. H. P. Turner seconding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0298" n="art0298">
        <head>LIBERAL BOWLERS TO BOWL,TOR " LOVE " ONLY.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL BOWLERS TO BOWL,TOR " LOVE " ONLY.</p>
        <p>The bowling green at the Liberal Club- on of the most delightful greens in the town end neighbourhood—has been splendidly prepared against the coming of the present season, and the turf is in irreproachable condition, thanks largely to the care bestowed upon it by the steward of the Abwsey-street Club, Mr. Albert T. Clay, himself an enthusiastic and expert bowler. Those .who have, so far, trundled woods over it this year pronounce the green sward to be in even better than its wonted excellei state, and during the present week there been a rush of devotees to sample its quality indeed so many have been the bowlers that, although but a few days old, the season may be said to be in full force and virtue so far as this particular green is concerned. There has been a rush of new bowling club members, and thptt despite the fact that the war will considerable curtail the usual attractions of the bowling side of the club's activities, for it has been decided that the time is not opportune for indulgence in bowls on the old competitive scale. There will be no participation in outside bowling tournaments daring the war period; there will be little or nothing save the game for the game's sake, the only matches to. be .arranged being those of a semi-private nature, between the members themselves, and, such .affairs, purely friendly ones. The pot-hunter will be quite out of it on this green this season. Yet there are no signs of waning in the number of devotees nightly to be seen on the green sward. On the contrary, the absence of matches is bringing out the more timorous bowler-7-the man who likes bowls bat ivatild "rather not play" in case he makes an .ass of himself—and the auguries all point to an even bigger bowl's membership than in normal times. And that is just what the committee want, for the green has been .pre- pared and provided as a means of health-giving recreatinn, and not as a mere competitive arena, whereon none but the expert shall dis- port themselves. Mr. George Marshall has been re-appointed secretary of the bowling green for the steason. and a better or more enthusiastic secretary it would be hard to find, lie has the interests of the green and of the game eqnally at heart, and be may be depended upon to do all in his power to see that the members get full value for their money. Mr. Joseph T. Smith has succeeded Mr. Joseph Wilkinson as chair- niaii of the grtell committee,- and be, fon, is a</p>
        <p>keen bowler. With an excellent green, a first- rate membership and plenty of enthusiasm for the ancient game, the Liberals are, despite the fact that .the usual competitiOns have been dropped, looking forward to a most successful and enjoyable season.'</p>
        <p>t :,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0299" n="art0299">
        <head>AMATEUR ASSOCIATION NOT YET AT WORK.</head>
        <p>AMATEUR ASSOCIATION NOT YET AT WORK.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District Amateur Bowl- ing Association have not yet got to work, though the xiew Secretary (Mr. H. Whittle, of 9, Fennell-street) and the committee are busied with the arrangements for carrying out the -curtailed programme which, in view of the war, has been decided upon. The championship competition is to be run this year as usual, and a consolation main is also being arranged; but it has been decided, in view of the tight- ness of money and the difficulty in obtaining subscriptions for the purchase of anything like valuable prizes, to abandon the usual Indivi- dual Merit Competition, while it is possible also that the customary handicap will not be held. The association finished last year in a satisfactory financial position—indeed, the bal- ance in hand was larger than in the year before—but at the annual meeting it was stated that there were indications that the committee could not this year look for Eruppcil:t in several quarters from which, in normal times, it was readily forthcoming. When the games do start, the absence of one team who enjoyed a reputation as real, good sports- men will be noticed, Earlestou-n Viaduct Insti- tute having dropped out, owing to the fact that a number of their members have "Joined up," and: are either on active service or are in training for the Front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0300" n="art0300">
        <head>LATCHFORD CONSERVATIVES OPENING MAIN.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD CONSERVATIVES OPENING MAIN.</p>
        <p>Rain did not damp the ardour of the members of the Latchford Conservative Bowling Club, for on Saturday afternoon they brought off their opening main for the "Garton Stone" Rose Bowl, on a green that had been carefully pre- pared, and was a treat to trundle on. Forty- four entrants were secured, and the contest for the President's rose bowl was keen and pro- duced some excellent bowling. The final lay between J. Bent and A. Jones. The former, who had played consistently well throughout the afternoon, and who had an allowance of three, made the ten points which be required whilst his opponent, a scratch mail, was compiling seven. In the semi-final Bent disposed of W. J. Wilson (who had an allowance of two) by the narrow margin of two points—the score reading 13-11. The other semi-finalists were Jones and IT. IT. Garton, who with an allowance of one, pilt on eleven whilst Jones got his 13, all up.</p>
      </div>
