      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0314" n="art0314">
        <head>STOCKTON ii EATH</head>
        <p>STOCKTON ii EATH</p>
        <p>HOME DEFENDERS' PARADE.</p>
        <p>Local Corps Complimented By The</p>
        <p>Adjutant.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath Detachment 'of the Cheshire Volunteer 'Regiment (for home de- fence) was inspected on Thursday by the Battalion Adjutant (Mr. Ford), , who compli- mented the men on their soldierly bearing and general smartness, virtues that are attributable to the splendid training given by Sergeant Stirrup, and to the whole-hearted way in which the men have responded to his ,enthusiastic and expert efforts to produce in the township a detachment that should be in every "way worthy of the whole to which it belongs. The parade took place on the &amp;ill ground, the muter representing almost the total mem- bership of the detachment. The Battalion Adjutant was supported in his inspection by the Battalion Quartermaster (Mr. Wormald) and the Battalion Secretary (Mr. Langley). Amongst those present was the Battalion Sub- Commandant (Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., C.V.0.) The inspecting officers looked very smart in the new grey-green uniform. _</p>
        <p>The first part of the -parade was conducted by Sergeant Stirrup, the Adjutant subsequently taking charge of the detachment for half an hour. lie afterwards addres.sTd the men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0315" n="art0315">
        <head>CREDIT TO THE REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>A CREDIT TO THE REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>The Adjutant expressed the gratification he felt at seeing reflected in their ranks so high a degree of efficiency and neatness of execution. lie regarded the Stockton Heath Detachment as a credit to the regiment. The excellence of the men's military deport- ment and their smart all-round appearance were admirably shown off in the ensuing march past. The new -Cheshire Volunteer badges will be issued next week, and the brassards as soon as they can be obtained from headquarters. During the past week fourteen new members hare been enrolled. The Battalion is still open for recruits, and Mr. 11. B. Houghton (hon. sec.). of 18, Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath, will be pleased to enrol eligible men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0316" n="art0316">
        <head>Citizens' Temperance League.</head>
        <p>Citizens' Temperance League.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of. the Warrington and District Citizens' Temperance League was held on Friday, when the Rev. T. T. James presided over a representative gathering. The treasurer's report, given by Mr. F. Gandy, showed that ' such support had been given to the work of the League as would help them to continue. The Secretary (Mr. Benjamin T. Parkes) gave a report which told of the temperance activities of the past year. Locally the League's efforts, he said, had not been very successful. They had not been inactive, but ,their efforts had been frustrated. Nevertheless, nationally, the Trade had received a blow. Alcoholic drink had been publicly proclaimed as one of the nation's eneinies; in fact it had been described as the greatest.. To destroy the nation's greatest enemy mould, indeed, be a noble work. The following were elected as the officers for the ensuing twelve months :—President. Rev. T. T. James; treasurer, Mr. F. Gandy; secretary, Mr. B. T. Parkes; assistant secretary, Mr. J. Simpson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0317" n="art0317">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 1, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 1, 1915.</p>
        <p>A DEEP SEA INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>Early navigators meeting the whale were appalled by its size, its majesty and its ap- parent indifference to any other creature of the seas. At last some fishermen whose nets had been destroyed went out to confront the monsthr and fight it in its natural element. Finding that its vast carcase was vulnerable to their poor weapons, they soon began to look upon the leviathan of the deep as a prey to be descried, since it furnished a great store of oil and meat, for at one time whales were considered highly edible. From the discovery of these fishermen sprang the great trade of whale hunting, which has lured so many adventurers to the frozen north.</p>
        <p>At the present time the whale fisheries of the world may be conveniently classed under three heads, the British, the Norwegian and the Canadian. At one time the British was the most important, for our wha:ers were aided by grants from Parliament. In 1732 a sum of twenty shillings Der ton was given to every vessel of more than 200 tons burden, the figure being increased to forty shillings in 1749. One of the principal reasons for this bounty was that the Government looked on whale fishing as excellent training for men who would eventually come into the navy. In 1819 Britain had over 140 ships en- gaged in the ,industry, but after the bounty was stopped in 1824 the number gradually dropped until now there are less than a doz- en, belonging exclusively tb Dundee and Peterhead. A TYPICAL WHALER.</p>
        <p>The whaler usually is a small vessel of from 400 to 500 tons gross register. Built after the strongest fashion she is protected along the water-line by an additional plank- ing of what is known as iron bark, an. Aus- tralian wood of great hardness, the bows are strengthened inside by transverse beams, and outside by bands of iron. Underneath the hold-beams about fifty iron tanks are fit- ted to receive the oil, which is extracted from the blubber by boiling. Above the hold-beams a deck is laid, engine and boiler- space being reserved in the stern. A screw steamer of this description is usually manned by about fifty hands, and carries half a doz- en whale-boats.</p>
        <p>An ordinary whale-boat is about twenty- seven feet long and six feet in breadth. In bow is a sort of platform on which a har- poon gun is mounted. The weapon now al- most universally- used weighs about 751135., and is discharged by means of percussion caps, doghead and trigger line, the nipples being protected from spray and rain by a movable brass cover. Mounted on a swivel the gun commands both sides of the bows as well as right ahead, and projects the harpoon with force and precision to a distance of twenty-five yards, the barbed missile tearing its way through the blubber and int() the vit- als. The only other weapons used are the hand harpoon and the lance. After a whale has been secured by the aid of the gun, the boats are rowed towards it, and when near enough the whale men thrust the hand har- poon (which seldom nowadays has a line at- tached) and the lance into its body in the hope of piercing its lungs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0318" n="art0318">
        <head>IN POLAR SEAS.</head>
        <p>IN POLAR SEAS.</p>
        <p>Starting from port in April, the British ships proceed by sail and steam to the whal- ing grounds at the north of Greenland, ar- riving there some time in May. On nearing Use fishery certain conditions are sought which the whale men know by experience are favourable to the appearance of the animal. The, first of these is whaling ice—moderate!y loose ice with close pack or floes in the neigh- bourhood, the second is plenty of natural whale food, the existence of which may be ascertained by means of a surface net, and the third an abundance of higher forms of life, such as birds, also intent on whale food. The whales live on the edge of the Polar ice- cap, following it up as it shrinks. The final disappearance of the seaward ice barrier ends the North Greenland ghing—it rarely lasts more than a month in any case—and vessels that have not obtained a full cargo some- times proceed further west, and cruise about the coast of Greenland until the darkness of the nights puts an end to the fishing. They usually return home again in autumn. As soon as a ship is in the vicinity of the whaling grounds a look-out is stationed in the "'crow's nest "—a barrel fixed high up on the mast. When a school of whales is dis- covered the greatest care has to be exercised in approaching. The vessel gets as nem as possible without disturbing, and then the boats are lowered, and the gunner or har- pooner makes ready. The boats are rowed di- rectly towards the animal selected, and when the first is within range the gun is fired and the harpoon, .which has a line attached is sent quivering into the body of the monster.' Now comes the moment of danger, for the whale makes a desperate effort to escape, and in its struggles lashes the water into foam.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0319" n="art0319">
        <head>MOMENT OF DANGER</head>
        <p>THE MOMENT OF DANGER</p>
        <p>If the boats are not handled smartly they would stand- a good chance of capsizing, while it would be fatal to get anywhere with- in reach of that powerful tail, a blow from which easily smashes a boat to splinters. After a few seconds the whale dives, some- times going straight down to the bottom of the sea, and sometimes ' descending to. a great depth, and then travelling straight ahead at a furious speed. ,The utmost care and attention is necessary on the part of the men in the boats when the harpoon lines are running out, as if one happens to run foul and cannot be cleared on the instant it will draw the boat under water. So fast does the line run out that it soon commences to smoke and if it was not constantly watered it would catch fire r</p>
        <p>When " struck " the .whale stays down foi about half an hour. The longer it remains down the better for the 'hunters, for when it comes up to breathe it is exhausted in pro- portion to the tint(' it has been under water, for a whale cannotilive without air any more than a human being. As soon as it reap- pears the boats make for the spot, and each harpooner then plunges his weapon into the ani,tnal's back. It is afterwards attacked with lances which are thrust into its body with the hope of reaching some vital spot. At length the huge animal indicates the ap- proach of death by discharging blood from the air-blows. which shows that its lungs have been pierced. Finally there are con- vulsive struegles, in which the tail violently thrashes the water, making a noise that can be heard for several miles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0320" n="art0320">
        <head>STUDENT SINGERS. I</head>
        <p>STUDENT SINGERS. I</p>
        <p>Fine Performance Of "St. Paul" At Training College.</p>
        <p>ts*</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0321" n="art0321">
        <head>MUSIC'S PLACE IN THE SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>MUSIC'S PLACE IN THE SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The annual concert of the students of the Warrington Training College may always be relied upon as providing a musical treat for those who are fogtunate enough to receive in- vitations to attend it, and this year's concert, which took place on Thugsday night, was cer- tainly no exception to the rule. This time the students decided to devote their vocal talents to the performance of an oratorio and Mendelssohn's, "St. .Paul "—easily one of the most effective and interesting of the greater musical epics—was selected, and at the hands of a large chorus of the students, assisted by well-known local vocalists and instrumen- talists, it was accorded an interpretation of no mean order, such as evoked unstinted applause from the audience. The solo parts were .takchn by the Misses Dorothy, Albrighton, Lilian Blackham and Mary Hamer (Members of the College), and Messrs. J. H. Draper and J. B. Poole (two of the best-known of our many ex- cellent local singers). All the principals were in excellent voice, and each gave sympathetic and understanding treatment to the arias and recitatives falling to their lot, the gentlemen singing in a manner that was entirely worthy of their big local reputations, and the ladies dis- tinguishing themselves for clarity of voice and clearness of enunciation, their respective per- formances denting enthusiastic and careful rehearsal. Without exception the principals were successful, and the same compliment may be unhesitatingly paid to the chorus, who sung with an earnestness and an appreciation of the character of the music that was a delight to the ear. The voices blended beautifully. and the attack was spirited, a fact which was specially noticeable when the effective chorus "Stone Him to death !" was reached. In the more purely melodic numbers--as, for instance, ,the poetically tuneful "How lovely are the messengers" the chorus were equally success- ful, and their treatment of the beautiful num- ber mentioned was one of the many delights of the evening. To sum up, the performance was a thoroughly enjoyable one, and everybody concerned worked conscientiously and artis- tically to produce a "tout ensemble " which should be such as would enhance the reputa- tion which the Training College annual con- cert enjoys among the local musical events of the year.</p>
        <p>The performance was under the most effi- cient conductorship of Mr. W. S. Woods and the orchestra comprised the following:— Miss Hackett (pianoforte), Meors. R. H. Pear- son (first violin), A. Knowles (second violin), J. E. Balmford (viola), E. Balmford (violon- cello), J. Sudworth (double bass), and W. H. Payton (harmonium). At the close of the final chorus the Rev. Canon Stevenson—the principal of the college— briefly expressed the thanks of the students for the appreciative reception accorded their efforts by the audience, and introduced to the assembled company Mr. Geoffrey T. Shaw, H.M. Inspector of Music in Training Colleges, who had been a critical auditor of the performance, for not only does this annual musical. event serve the purpose of an entertainment for the members of the College who are not actually concerned it it, as well as for a host Of friends, but it also serves as an examination—an oppor- tunity for H.M. expert to gauge the Value• of the training in vocal music afforded by the College and the aptitude of the pupils for absorb- ing the same.</p>
        <p>Mr. Shaw took the opportunity to briefly address the students and the company in general. He extolled the delights of music, and reminded the students of the golden days that lay before them, when they came to teach the subject in the schools in which; in the future, they -• would be • engaged. Musicians, he admitted, were perhaps rather inclined to think that their own special subject was the only one of culture that mattered in the school, but he could assure them that personally he would far rather that his boy made mistakes in spelling than that he did not enjoy a good folk song. Music in the school had that in it which a great many of the other subjects taught had not, for it con- tained within it a direct appeal ,to the finer side of the childish emotions, and for that reason.it was a subject that thoroughly deserved its place in the school curriculum. In the spacious days of Queen Elizabeth, when great Englishmen thought great things, did great things and lived fine lives, education meant something far more to them than the mere gaining and having of money and the spending of it. He was not quite sure that successive generations of gradgrinds had not, since then, rather diverted education into an attempt, to turn out so many machines for business pur- poses. If that were so, was it not time that they began to think of the finer and nobler sides of education, and with this subject of music they had a great chance. The speaker went on to emphasise the importance of the song in school music, for, after all, if music was going to do them any good in the schools it could only do it in one way; viz.: through the song. It was quite tragic to go into the schools and find that of a music lesson of say, half an hour's duration,, twenty-five minutes were given up to what he could only call dry bones—given up to the tyranny of the blackboard—and only right at the end came one poor, shivering little song. (Laughter, and applause.) He did think that it should be made a rule that at least half of the music lesson should be devoted to song singing. Mr. Shaw went on to speak of folk songs, and opined that a revival of the old love of the folk song would inean far more to the nation than' the nation was apt to imagine; indeed, it was a great work, educationally, that the teacher of music could do—a great work for the good of the nation. Those splendid, old songs of the past should never be lost sight of in the schools —the songs, traditional and national, , which, reflected in the most wonderful way the spirit' of the great nation for which they were com- posed. They were straightforward, manly and honest; they were at times virulent, but yet un- derlying many of them was a tenderness--a tenderness which, though it might not show on the surface, was always there. No nation had a finer heritage of that class of song- than had the British nation, and it was for the teachers in the nation's Schools to make the best' possible use of that heritage. He could not conclude without thanking those gentlemen who had so kindly come forward to help the students that evening; help, withoht which the students of the College could not have given so enjoyable a pfrformance. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>The concert cohclu' ;led wiih the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>ission .of the Proprietors of "Punch.")</p>
        <p>PEOPLE WE SHOULD LIKE TO SEE INTERNED.</p>
        <p>VISITOR (brightly) : "Now, chatter away, and tell the all about it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0322" n="art0322">
        <head>CULTURED WARRINGTON. "The Athens of England," Says</head>
        <p>CULTURED WARRINGTON. "The Athens of England," Says</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett.</p>
        <p>LOCAL LITERATEURS AND ARTISTS.</p>
        <p>Warrington has been styled "the Athens of Lancashire " and " the Athens of Eng- land "—I prefer the latter descr'ption. Taking size into consideration, and except- ing the University towns, I doubt if there is any town in the country that is in front of Warrington in the matter of the number of people who are interested in the higher things of life—in literature, and in culture in general.</p>
        <p>So spoke Alderman A. Bennett at the quarterly meeting of the North-Western Branch of the Library Association, held at the War- rington Academy, Bridge Foot, on Thursday afternoon, when three interesting papers were read. The President, Mr. Geo. T. Shaw (Chief Librarian of the Liverpool Public Libraries), was:in the chair, and he was supported by the lion. Secretary, Mr. James Hutt (Librarian of the Lyceum Library, Liverpool). There was an excellent attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0323" n="art0323">
        <head>HI§TORY OF THE ACADEMY.</head>
        <p>HI§TORY OF THE ACADEMY.</p>
        <p>The -first paper was read by Mr. C. Madeley. It was entitled: Warrington Academy, with especial reference to its book-producing activi- ties."</p>
        <p>Warrington Academy, said Mr. Madeley, was one of a number of attempts made in the 18th century to supply for Nonconformists some- thing in the place of that University education from which they were debarred by the Test Acts. The academies, of which Warrington Academy was one, were pioneers in the devel- opment of that ideal of education which was represented by the modern side in our Gram- mar School's and the faculties of Science and History. The originator of the idea of estab- lishing the Warrington Academy was the Rev. John Sedden, then minister of the Cairo-Areet Chapel. The Academy was opened in 1757 in the house in which that meeting was assembled. During the twenty-five years of its existence, nearly 400 students passed through it. It was never a success and was finally dissolved, and a new institution, carrying on the same tradi- tions, was established .at Manchester.</p>
        <p>Other Famous Academicians.</p>
        <p>Having dealt on the various achievements of others who were associated with the Academy—Joseph Priestley, the Rev. Wm. Enfield, Gilbert Wakefield, Reinbold Forster, the Aikin family, Wm. Rus- coe, Thomas Tennant, John Howard, Thos. Percival, and Thomas Barnes—Mr. Madeley alluded to the debt owed to Alderman Bennett for the way he came to the rescue and saved that building from destruction by the Corpora- tion by joining the Warrington Society (a liter,ary and social Club). The Chairman, at the conclusion of the paper, observed that there had been shown in Eng- land, in the nineteenth century, far too much readiness to sacrifice interesting buildings, and it stood to the credit of Warrington tbat the Academy had been saved. It was, by reason of its historical interest, of considerable value, not only to Warrington, not only to Lancashire, but to the country in general.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0324" n="art0324">
        <head>OLD LITERATURE AND NEW.</head>
        <p>OLD LITERATURE AND NEW.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett said that as President of the Warrington Society, he had the greatest pleasure in welcoming the visitors. It was very 'appropriate that such. apaper as M. Madeley's; should be read in that building.. He thought they must all feel doubly grateful to Mr. Madeley for his excellent paper, in view of the fact that he only last week lost his , wife, and had had in consequence been greatly disturbed from the preparation of the paper. Having made the remarks quoted in the opening paragraph, Alderman Bennett went on to allude to the fame enjoyed by the Eyres Pres.s. It was often a relief to turn from some modern book to some of the old literature, such as used to be given to the world through the medium of the Eyres Press. He spoke in terms of high' appreciation of the poetical works of Miss Lucy Aiken, and referred to Dr. Joseph Priestley as "one of the greatest Englishmen of all time." It. was a source of great satisfaction to know that much of his literary and scientific work was done in Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0325" n="art0325">
        <head>ARTISTS FROM THE ACADEMY.</head>
        <p>ARTISTS FROM THE ACADEMY.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Warburton Wright (of the Holton Public Library) opened his paper on "The Artists of Warrington" with the statement that though there were no great artists living in Warrington at the present day, great artists had, been 'born in the town, and yet others bad re- ceived their early training here. Just as War- rington had contributed a valuable quota to letters, so it had contributed in no small measure to art. George Sheffield was not born in the town, but his first training was obtained at the Warrington Academy. His best work was done in monochrome. He had the rapidity almost of a "lightning cartoonist." Sir Luke Fildes was born in Liverpool, and had his earliest artistic tuition at Chester, but the scope of the instruction there was limited, and, seek- ing a wider and more ambitious training, he came to Warrington, and became a pupil at the Academy. After three years at Warrington, he migrated to London, where, at the age of 19, he became attached to the South Kensington Schools, and began doing work for the illus- trated periodicals. It was impossible to say to what extent his subsequent rise to fame was attributable to the splendid training he obtained at the Warrington Academy, but there was no doubt that the excellence of that training was an enormous advantage to him. Mr. Wright went on to speak of .Henry Woods, James Charles, Edward Frederick Brewthall, J. War- rington Wood, Mrs. Ellen Houghton and Miss Florence H. Laverock. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0326" n="art0326">
        <head>LIBRARY GUIDE SIGNS.</head>
        <p>LIBRARY GUIDE SIGNS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. Singleton (of the Accrington Public Library) read a very humorous, and at the same time practical, paper on "'Guide Signs in Libraries," out of whichj arose some comnien-. tary on the want of intelligence displayed .by a large number of those who use public libraries. •</p>
        <p>The meeting concluded with votes of thanks to the readers of the papers, the Chairman, and to the Council of the Warrington Academy (for lending the premises). The majority of the company then adjourned to Atkinson's Cafe for tea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0327" n="art0327">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>DRAMA OF THE MILL AT 'I'HE ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>"A Mill Girl's Wedding," a drama of the order beloved, by the " gods " is proving a. big draw at the Royal Court Theatre this week, and deservedly so, for all who like this kind of fare will find it quite one of the best—full of just the right sort of sentiment, and with its many merry moments of comic relief. The play is from the pen of Mr: Herbert Shelley, whose company, headed by himself as the funny man, play it in swinging style, bringing out in most effective fashion all its strong points. Mr. Herbert Shelley himself gives a rare study of a weaver whose absorbing passion, next to his love for the poor mill girl who has been basely betrayed, is football, and Miss Alma Shelley acts sympathetically and convincingly as Nannie Gray, the much-wronged heroine, and the mill girl of the title whose troubles all end in the joyous clash of the marriage bells. Mr. 'Victor Gardom makes just the rigtt type of Villain as Luke Thorndyke, and all the minor characters are most. capably taken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0328" n="art0328">
        <head>Real Revue Next Week.</head>
        <p>Real Revue Next Week.</p>
        <p>The merry London revue." What Ho! Tango" will form the fare at the Royal Court Theatre next week. This revue has had a record run in London and the provinces. The company which is to visit Warrington comprises no fewer than forty artistes, and the music will be accom- panied by ln augmented orchestra. Dancing is specialised, every type of dance being intro- ducted, from the old-fashioned English Gavotte to the latest Tango and Maxixe. The prin- cipals are Mr. Tom Tutty, the Australian comedian (who appears in the part of Mr. Pan- kake) and Miss Eva Rosslyn, a favourite music hall star (who takes the part of Marie Papilliard). "What Ho! Tango"- is the revue- proper; that is. to say it is neither pantomime nor musical comedy, but just a gay and. exhilarating medley of all the good things in stageland, presented with wonderful rapidity and without any attempt at cohesion; in fact, it is quite . plotless. Beautiful girls, glorious voices, exquisite dresses; bewitching dances, and screaming comedy is the keynote of this revue, and its visit to Warrington should prove a great success. It is to be played twice nightly, and there will be a matinee on Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0329" n="art0329">
        <head>CYCLING EXTRAORDINARY AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>CYCLING EXTRAORDINARY AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The outstanding feature of the ^voellent programme at the Hippodrome I•,</p>
        <p>thrilling cycling act -by Romani a ... 'Lea</p>
        <p>- - Belgian artiste, who performs feats that positively send a shudder down the spine, so daring and highly sensational are they. He balances himself and his machine in the most perilous and difficult positions, he rides with the same facility on one wheel as on two, and the absence of handle-bars and ,framework is of no concern to him; he tan' ride upside-down with even greater facility than the ordinary wheel- man can pedal along • right-side up, and he concludes a thrilling turn with a remarkable Fide on a contrivance which is not inaptly named " The Devil's Table "—a revolving plat- form spun round by electric power at an enormous rate. First he circles the table on a motor-cycle, and then he goes round it on an ordinary pedal machine, in each instance riding in the opposite direction to which the table is spinning. Next he releases his grip of the pedals and standing upside-down on the handle- bars of his bicycle, submits to being whirled round and round the table at an almost in- creilible. rate Of speed. He is the most daring trick cyclist we have glimpsed for many a day. Many other tip-top turns go to make up a most attractive bill; indeed Mr. Turpin's last pro- gramme—far he is going to manage a bigger house in Southport—was in fact among the best of the many first-rate ones he has been able to give to his patrons. The Sisters Sprightly, true to their name, give a most sprightly sketchlet, in, the shape of a skit on "Sister Turns " past and 'present. Jno.• Paley, who has in his time had charge of well-known military bands, sings that fine Song "The Trumpeter," with a bugle-call obligato, and also plays a number of cornet solos in fine style. Tom Drew and Com- pany extract roars of laughter with their farcical sketch "The Registry," whilst Clifford Morgan sings some splendid baritone songs in splendid style, his numbers including Kipling's "Barrack-room Ballads," " Here's to You, Fuzzy Wuzzy " and "Follow Me Home." The Long Brothers contribute some step-dancing extraordinary, and Retta, Halkett sings Scotch songs and dances charmingly. An edition of Gaumont's Graphic pictures goes to complete the bill.</p>
        <p>Novel Musical "Turn" Heads Next Week's Bill. Chevalier and Riego, who provide quite a novel kind of turn, top a most attractive bill prepared for the delectation of Hippodrome habitues next week. Their special line is song, music and story, the lady member .of the duo singing lightsome airs in the most dainty of costumes, whilst the gentleman extracts merry music from the piano, what time he cracks equally merry • jokes. Ted Le Roy and his troupe of juvenile performers are announced to appear in a vocal and dancing scena, and another star turn will be provided by the Barnard Family, in -a miniature revue, entitled "Dolls Alive."' The sensational element will be provided by the English Provas in a trick cycling act, and Phil and Phlora promise a novel show in their "silent comedy" turn. Several other artistes will go to the making up of a first-rate programme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0330" n="art0330">
        <head>LANCASHIRE LIFE AT THE STAR</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE LIFE AT THE STAR</p>
        <p>KINEMA.</p>
        <p>An especially popular programme obtained at that favourite resort, the Star Kinema, during the first half of the week—for in premier place was allocated to a heart-stirring Lanca- shire drama of love and rivalrY. "A Monkey Cabaret" proved a highly entertain ing animal acrobatic display. "Pathes'Animated Gazette" . depicted a store of latest war hap- penings—including those in Lancashire. "Native Tradesmen of Calcutta" was an in- teresting as well as an educative descriptive. One of the best presented of recent war films was "After Fifty. Years," a romantic and altogether thrilling series of episodes from the Italian war of 1859. "The Lancashire‘Lass " has by common consent been voted a remark- ably faithful portrayal of typical mill life in this county, and the fascinating plot, could hardly have had a better setting. "Those Love- Pangs" was n 'screamer' featuring ,the in- imitable Charles Chaplin.</p>
        <p>To-day's programmes are as good as ever, and include 'The Unwelcome Mrs. Hatch "—backed up by the usual " star " pictorial varieties.</p>
        <p>Next Week's " Star " Films.</p>
        <p>Two fine films have been booked to form the premier attractions in next week's excellent pro- grammes. The big film on the first .three nights will be "The Straight Road," and on the last three nights. "Captain Swift," which was founded on Haddon Chamber's famous play of the same name, and should be well worth seeing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0331" n="art0331">
        <head>GOOD FARE 1V1"111E GRAND.</head>
        <p>GOOD FARE 1V1"111E GRAND.</p>
        <p>Tip-top programmes continue to be the order at the Grand. During the first half of this week, in addition to "Pathe's Animated Gazette," numerous other attractive films were shown, including two screamingly . funny comedies entitled "A Strand of Blonde Hriii" and "The Manicure Girl." Other pictures shown were "The ;Polly of Anne," " The In- evitable Retribution," and the "star" picture, "The Submarine Spy," a thrilling war drama. Another fine programme is being submitted dur- ing the week-end. This includes a first-rate western drama entitled "Sunshine and Shadow" —a picture Which should not be missed.</p>
        <p>"Fog THE BLOOD IS THE LIFE." —" In the organism of man the materials for the nutrition of every portion of the structure are supplied by the blood. If the blood is diseased the body is diseased." Clarke' s Blood Mixtuie can be relied on to cure all Blood and Skin diseases. It is the most searching blood cleanser ever discovered, and will free the system from all impurities from what- ever cause arising. For Scrofula, Scurvy. Eczema, Abscesses, Ulcers, Glandular Swellings, Bad Legs, Pimples and Sores of all kinds, its effects are mar- vellous. Thousands of testimonials. Sold every- where at 2s. 9d. per bottle. Refuse substitutes.— Advt 7.</p>
