        <p>DUNKIRK.</p>
        <p>Story Of Bombardment Contradicted.</p>
        <p>The Press Bureau informs us that tile rumour that the German warships have bom- barded Dunkirk is untrue.</p>
        <p>_ The rumour probably originated in a 11115" reading of the French official conimuniqw' issued yesterday.</p>
        <p>REV. T. T. JAMES</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0332" n="art0332">
        <head>Going To The Front For Three</head>
        <p>Going To The Front For Three</p>
        <p>Months.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. James, minister of the Wycliffe Congregational Church, Warrington, is to go to the Front—arrangements having bee'? . made for .his absence, in a ministerial capacitk with the Y.M.C.A. in France, from June to Sep- tember. At the ordinary monthly meeting of the mem- bers of the Church, held on Wednesday . the following special ..resolution -was. brought forward under the rules and carried:— That the members of this Church havinfl • heard Mr. James' statement of his desire to render service .with the British Expeditionary Force in France, willingly grant him thrV months' leave of absence, and earnestly pral that his work amongst the troops will be signally successful and richly blessed. Mr. James, feeling it. impossible to make holi- day this year during his annual month's abseil° from Wycliffe, offered his services to the Y.M.C.A. on behalf Of the forces. Being in- formed that the usual period was of three months' duration, he consulted his church , deacons. with the above result. Mr. James entered upon his pastorate at Wycliffe at the beginning, of February, 1913. coming from the Wycliffe Church at Sheffield' , As is well-known, his local ministry is in eveff , respect proving highly successful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-05-01_WidnesExaminer_art0333" n="art0333">
        <head>REPREHENSIBLE PRACTICE.</head>
        <p>A REPREHENSIBLE PRACTICE.</p>
        <p>Sontething The Recruiting Authorities Do Not Approve.</p>
        <p>It has come to the knowledge of the recruit- ing authorities in the Warrington district that various unauthorised methods are being adopted to bring pressure to bear upon eligible young men, with a view to getting them to enlist. Al proper means, of persuasion are welcomed IV those whose duty it is to search out and sign Or recruits, but the method which has beer brought to light locally within the last foc days does not meet with their approval, and, needless to say, is not adopted with their sanc- tion.</p>
        <p>The objectionable method consists in sending through the post white feathers and postcards of an insulting nature, these apparently being addressed indiscriminately to young men in tbe town, regardless of the fact that many of the recipients may have applied for enlistment and have been rejected on Medical grounds, or that they may be engaged on Government work, the doing of which they are rendering service to their country which is almost as essential a 4 that which is being done by the men at the Front.</p>
        <p>"The- practice of sending these feathers and postcards," Colonel Xfocatta told our representa- tive, "is much to be deprecated, and is, in fact, strongly deprecated by the recruiting author" ties, who feel that it may have a contrail effect to that which is apparently desired. Whilst- we welcome all legitimate endearour9 on the part of private individuals to swell the ranks of our army—in-fact, we do everything .0, encourage such endeavours—we do not approve' or give countenance to, efforts of this sort, and - the more do we discountenance them from the fact that the feathers and the postcards ar,e; invariably sent through the post anonymouslY•</p>
        <p>Fire at Grappenhall. About noon on Thursday it was discovered that a . haystack on the farm of Mr. Jas. Garnett, at. Grappenhall, was in flames. 'rile Warrington Fire Brigade were summcihed, but' despite the promptness of their arrival and the efficiency of their .efforts to subdue the flame; the stack, which was fortunately only a small one, was destroyed. The brigade is to be cots; plirnented on saving from harm a number 0,.1 ether stacks standing very close to the one thal was burned. Finding it was impossible to aver' the destruction of the burning stack, they coll" centrated on preventing the other stacks from being involved in the conflagration. The cause of the fire is wriknown.</p>
        <p>The religion—that costs you, nothing costs 0° much.</p>
        <p>A nice new irrnbrella is used up when it is uSed at all.</p>
