        <p>was in hospital, and might be coming home. Private Jenks is the son of the late Mr. Arthur Jenks—who was for some time host of the Ship Inn, Bank Quay, Warrington—and he was employed prior to the war at Messrs. Hy. Milling and Co.'s grocery establishment in Horse market-street. He is 22 years of age, and be- longs to the 4th South Lancs. Regiment. As a boy, he attended the People's College, Arpley- street, Warrington. He was a regular wor- shipper at Holy Trinity Church. Second-</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Arthur, a brother of Private Jenks, is with the Army, and is on active service in India. CORPORAL LUKE ATHERTON. Nature Of His Injuries So Far Unknown. News was received on Thursday that Corporal Luke Atherton, of the D Company of the 4th (Territorial) Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment, had been wounded in action. The intimation, which was conveyed on a field card, was received by his parents at 8, Kent-street, Latchford, with whom Corporal Atherton's wife</p>
