        <p>PRIVATE RALPH WOODS. Hit Whilst Taking Food To Comrades Wounded In Back By Shrapnel Whils In The Trenches, On Sentry-Duty. Private Ralph Woods, of A Company, 4th Lancashire Regiment, whose home is at 61, Foundry-street, Warrington, is amongst the in- jured local Territorials. He has written to his</p>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL. Rifleman A. Sharpies Now In Hospital In France. Joining the 4th Battalion of the Rifle Brigade on the outbreak of the war as a mem- ber of Kitchener's Army, and proceeding to tthe Front in January, Rifleman Arthur Sharpies, of 6, Robert-street, Warrington, was recently wounded, and news has been received that he is</p>
        <p>that I should take the vice-chair under these circumstances; some of these genflemen have more time. The proposition, however, was carried unani- mously. "FOR WHAT I AM WORTH." The Vice-chairman, having taken his place at the other end of the table, said: I ktppreciate very much the honour you have done 'me, and I take it that you take me for what I am worth'. I shall' do my best under the particular circum- stances. (Hear, hoar.) COUNCILLOR' LOCKYER'S ILLNESS. Councillor Groves (at the close of the meeting) said they all knew one of their members, Coun- cillor Lockyer, had been stricken down with serious illness, and proposed that the Clerk be instructed to send to him a letter of sympathy, coupled with the hope that he would soon be restored again. Councillor Cowper seconded. The Chairman: He is one of our oldest mem- bers now, and I am sure we all wish for his , speedy return. Unanimously, the resolution was carried. ALL THE COUNCIL ON ALL THE COMMITTEES. When the question of appointing the various committees came up, Councillor Groves proposed that the whole of the Council constitute each committee. Councillor Maclntyre having seconded, this was unanimously carried. The following chairmen and vice-chairmen were appointed : Water Committee: Councillor Groves. Roads: Councillors Lockyer and Parker. Sanitary and Sewage Farm: Councillors • Maclntyre and Parker. Housing and Town Planning: Councillor Critchley. Fire Engine: Councillor Parker. Gas: Councillors Cowper and Groves. Finance: Councillor Ingham. General Purposes: Chairman and Vice- chairman of the Council. MEETINGS AND QUORUMS. The Council's monthly meeting; it was de- cided, shall be held as before—on the last Mon- day of each month; Councillor Groves' proposal to alter it to Thursday having been defeated. The quorum continues to consist of three itilembers. OUG-HTRINGTON. PRIVATE JAMES OWEN KILLED. Death From lVounds Reported Without Warning. Private James Owen, of South View Cottages, Sandy-lane, Oughtrington, a member of the IA Cheshires, has died from wounds received apparently in the firing line. The totally un- expected news was broken with tragic sudden- ness to his wife on Thursday afternoon, when at half-past three the following telegram arrived : Shrewsbury,—Regret to inform you of death of Number 24655, Private James Owen, Cheshire Regipent—died of wounds 15th April—and to express sympathy of Army Council. Private Owen enlisted at the beginning of January, and went out to the Front only two months ago. The Ist Cheshires are situated there like the sth Battalion. Every week he had sent regularly a letter home—but a fort- night ago these ceased to arrive without any ex- planatioh at the time. Mrs. Owen had never heard a word about her husband being wounded until Thursday—indeed, nobody in Lymm seems to have done; and nothing further is yet known. Yesterday morning Mrs. Woods received from the War Office confirmation of her husband's death, together with the usual business papers. WORKED AT WARRINGTON. Twenty-eight and a half years of age, Private Owen, son of Mr. and Mrs. John Owen (who also live in South View Cottages) was born in the lane where his wife still lives. Leaving the</p>
        <p>• • .. • •• • • • I • • • • ..ilt*:Ller.t'4,.:q .7 •. • • • ' 45;;:11,A. • • • • - '4,l`tai : • • , . • ,s . . . • . • • . .4 ' • • • ••• , ;•.. . • , • • • •• . . . • . • . • , •••••....• . .</p>
        <p>Oughtrington school at the age of fourteen, he ,went to work for the London and North-Western Railway Company as 'a cleaner. Afterwards he became a labourer for Mr. Thomason, but recently had been engaged on street labour near Bank Quay Station, Warrington. His father is also a labourer—at the Lymm Gas Works. In 1913 Private Owen was married, and now leaves two little sons, besides his widow. He attended St. Peter's Parish Church, to the Sun- day school of which he had also belonged. Served in Navy. Before the outbreak of war he also served for three Months as a stoker in His Majesty's Navy. "MARCHING 'WITH RIFLE AND SPADE WAIST-DEEP IN WATER." Lymm Lad With Lancashires Writes To Oughtrington. Private Jack Kenwright, of .Rush Green, Lymm—who at the beginning of the war en- listed with Some Warrington fellows in .the 4th South Lancashires, and is now at the- Front—sends a graphic letter to his employer, Mr. S. Critehley, of Sandy-lane, Oughtrington. Having thanked that gentleman for his kind gift of chocolates and cigarettes, Private Ken- wright goes on to say that they have been hav- ing a rough time in the trenches—making a • double line of defence. Suddenly a volley of fire came over, followed by an attack from the 'Germans. Severe Losses In Repulsing Attack. "We had to retire just a little way," the private continues, ." and then our. boys stuck it to the last. We lost a lOt of men and an officer, and I had given up hope of ever getting through. However, the Germans met their match, and retired. Each Shoulder Laden. " And then .we had to march home waist- deep in water with a rifle on .one shoulder and a spade on the other. I was a lucky man to get back to receive your box !. . . ."</p>
